      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Ittitati in the tl3 Mint,</head>
        <p>Ittitati in the tl3 Mint,</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Tin EDITOR of "The Examiner" invites corre. I LETTER'S MUSD in all cases be accompanied by the spondence on all subjects of public interest. I name and address of the writer. Lamas should be brief and pointed. Their COMMUNICATIONS received early in the week— object must be to impart or seek information, except 'urgent matters—can always be given not abuse. the best. attention. ervice are held over till</p>
        <p>Letters on National next week.</p>
        <p>hustle! Hustle is not the name! Everyone growls, everyone grumbles; some few of the more excitable ones have been suspected of really "wicked things muttered berieath their breath, but, as the old proverb truly says, Everyone's business is nobody's business," and so the matter goes on. Could not some under- standing be arrived at by .a conference between the District Council, the Ship Canal Co., the Warrington Tramways Committee, &amp;c.? The greater number of the business men of War- rington live on the Cheshire side of the Ship Canal, and must be interested in obtaining a reasonably free use of the public highway at present so often monopolised in the interest of one limited company—the Ship Canal Company. If the bridges were only opened twice in the. hour, say, at 20 minutes past and 10 minutes to the hour at stated times, things would be better. If the Stockton Heath trams were ordered to return from Stafford-road, if, on arrival at the Swing Bridge, the same was closed to traffic, it would help considerably! At present it is fairly common to see two cars held up on the Cheshire side, and one waiting to go through from Warrington, or the whole service at a standstill. Meanwhile, anyone who has ' been lucky enough to get across the bridge con- tinues the journey on foot, frequently arriving at Warrington before the first car comes arong. Such a state of muddle and mismanagement could never, exist in any other country than ours, where men boast foolishly that all are free and the King's highway is open to all. Good luck to the doctor and anyone else public- spirited enough to agitate for improvement! PRO BONO PUBLIC°.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>SPIRITISM AND ROMAN CATHOLICISM.</head>
        <p>SPIRITISM AND ROMAN CATHOLICISM.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l never had, and I have not now, any intention of discussing the whole subject of Spiritualism with Mr. Chrimes by means of letters in your paper. Mgr. Benson very ably and lucidly expounded the Catholic attitude on the subject in his lecture. I merely wrote to emphasise one point that possibly Mgr. Benson did not touch upon, or, if he did o that Mr. Chrimes had not understood. I think any intelligent -reader of our correspondence wilt see that the explanation I gave lays down a prin- ciple which, when applied, answers the question that Mr. Chrimes keeps repeating about the apparition to the Passionists, and the Catholic attitude towards this and similar cases. I can only refer Mr. Chrimes back to my first letter. (Rev.) P. L. BUGGINS. St. Alban's, Bewsey-street.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l have been very much interesteein the correspondence in your columns on the Spirit World between Father Buggins and Mr. Chrimes, and I would like, before saying any- thing, to quote two texts from the good old Book. The prophet Isaiah asks: "Hast thou not known, host thou not heard, that the ever- lasting God, the Lord, the' Creator of the ends of the earth, fainteth not, neither is weary? There is no searching 'of his understanding." The great Apostle tells us: "Eye hath not seen, nor ear heard, neither have entered into the heart of man, the things which God bath pre- pared for them that love Him."</p>
        <p>- With these texts for a preface, I proceed to review from my standpoint the arguments advanced on either side, and, for my own part, I see no necessity for either the Spiritualists or the Roman Church to dabble in the mysteries of the unknown. When the Creator has anything to communicate to his creatures He will take such steps as shall place beyond the power of cavil or doubt His desire and intention. . I do not see for myself much difference between Spiritism as practised by Spiritualists and as practised in the Roman Church. As between the two forms, I think the latter is the least creditable to its professors. The Maid of Orleans, because of her patriotism, was brought before an Ecclesiastical Court, presided over by the Bishop of Beauvais, assisted by the Vicar- General of the Inquisition, and condemned for sorcery and witchcraft. Is there any better ground of proof for the apparition 'of the Virgin at La Salette and at Lourdes than might be produced at au ordinary Spiritualist seance? Was it not suspected at the time that the so- called Virgin was none other than a certain Mdlle. de la Merliere, formerly a nnn, and then a lady of somewhat doubtful character.? Was it not proved that she was present in La Salette on the day the children were said to have seen the vision? Is it not a fact that she commenced an action for the rehabilitation of her char- acter, but the witnesses against her were so numerous, and their evidence so coherent, that the Court of Grenoble decided in favour of her accusers, and condemned her in costs? In the course of the trial it came out in evidence that Mdlle. de la Merliere had played the same farce at other places, notably at the Convent of Laus, in the Commune of Saillans ; at Espeluches, near Montelimar; a second time at La Salette. Mr. Hitchman, in his biography of Pius IX., tells us that "all these co-called apparitions were, care- fully hushed up by the clergy, on the ground, as described by one of the witnesses in the trial, that belief in this pretended miracle would do good to religion." I invite Father Buggins to show wherein the merits of this is any better or as good as a ,Spiritualist seance.</p>
        <p>PHIL SQUOD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>DELAY AT SWING BRIDGES.</head>
        <p>DELAY AT SWING BRIDGES.</p>
        <p>v ,</p>
        <p>, ; • ' I 1 '</p>
        <p>i 1,',/ttt ' ,:ii!: - . -;!o. P, ~C,:.,</p>
        <p>1;1 'f't '</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is to be hoped that the protest recently made by Dr. Binns against the shocking delay and inconvenience caused by the Swing Bridges at Lower Walton and Stockton Heath receives support from the many others whose business is hampered and delayed in the same way. The people of Stockton Heath and district are virtually slaves, ruled by an autocrat, whose sceptre is a small brass horn. The pleasure, convenience or liberty of these modern serfs is of no moment whatever. Have they trams to catch? appointments to keep? sick to visit? Do they walk, cycle, travel by tram or motor? What odds? Who cares? An aeroplane alone can render them free!</p>
        <p>The highway is thronged! Foot passengers, trams, farmers' and tradesmen's carts proceed- ing about their business! The King of the Causeway, from his elevated signal-box-cum-throne, espies a smudge of smoke upon the far horizon! Somewhere be- yond Latchford Locks, or down_ below Twelve Arches, a steamer approaches! Enough! She conies! Toot! toot! Give way, ye slaves, stand by! Too late! The gates are closed ! ! And, being closed, so shall they stay till my good pleasure; and a further melancholy toot per- mits you to resume your ways! What with the Ship Canal Bridges, level crossing on the Causeway, and single line over the new Bridge at Warrington, travel by tram- car can be utilised as a new form of rest cure. The journey is, roughly, a mile long, and the time taken is frequently twenty minutes, or an average of three miles per hour! Talk about</p>
        <p>"ROgitE4vrk</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>IRISH DIFFICULTY.</head>
        <p>THE IRISH DIFFICULTY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l crave your indulgence to permit me to protest against the assertion of "Phil Squod " in your issue, of the Bth, "that I palliate the misdeeds of anyone." I allow no one, either wilfully or ignorantly, to misinterpret me. With respect to the doings of the Manchester men, I tried to point out that Sergeant Brett was not intentionally shot. Surely that is not palliation of crime. Let "Phil Squod" carry his mind back over all 'the brutal and vicious murders he can remember being committed, and subtract from that the number convicted of the murders who were afterwards reprieVed for extenuating circumstances. Was the finding of extenuating circumstances palliation of the crime? Recently an engine driver was convicted of manslaughter, and was sentenced to a period of imprisonment, but was pardoned a few days after. Immediater3i after his sentence a wave of sympathy went over the country for him, and 'memorials on his behalf were extensively signed. When released, it is reported, he was carried shoulder high 'through the streets by his friends. Why? Had he done anything heroic? Were air those who thus showed their sym- pathies with him palliating manslaughter? I am of opinion,, were it possible for the Manches- ter men to be tried to-day, with twelve Chief Constables (English for choice) as the jury, the verdict would be manslaughter against the one only. The others would have been convicted of treason and felony only. I fully appreciate the sentiments' of "Phil Squod " when deprecating a people going about with loaded pistols, or guns, and concealed daggers, and trust he will still further use his epistolatory ability in writing a series of letterp, say, to the Marquis of Londonderry, Sir E. Carson, Galloper F. E. Smith, and Refugee Organiser Harold Smith, and express .his feel- ings of abomination at the introduction of such a tremendous number of rifles and ammunition into Belfast (I suppose they have also the bayonet attached), which are not intended for innocent church parades, or spectacular effects. It is possible his effort might have some result. If not, he will at least earn our respect for consistency. "Phil Squod's " scheme of Devo- lution and Federation, and I say this very respectfully, is not at present before the country; also it is not quite new. Mr. Alderman Bennett, of Warrington, has twice in recent years submitted a full and comprehensive scheme for discussion; and whatever faults or virtues it possesses, Nationalists look upon it as a delayal of Home Rule for Ireland. They , have been pleading,, working and urging for it for thirty years 'and more. • When . England, , Scotland and Wales ask for it Irish Nationalists will whole-heartedly help them to achieve their desires, but are not prepared to wait until they have -made up their minds. Personally, I be- lieve the disposition towards the granting of Home Rule to Ireland is growing. Contrasting my own experiences of the spirit manifested to- wards it to-day against the early days of its advocacy, the outlook is most hopeful; although I may regret I am not disturbed by the results of some by-elections. They are merely the pimples or blotches on an otherwise beautiful face; whilst they may for the time disfigure, they do not cletermipe its style of beauty._</p>
        <p>PATRICK O'BRIEN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>MAN WHO NEVER SLEPT.</head>
        <p>MAN WHO NEVER SLEPT.</p>
        <p>Insomnia, Neurasthenia and Paralysis. Best Hospital Treatment Fails and Compensation Awarded as Hopelessly Incurable. Made Strong and Healthy by</p>
        <p>DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS.</p>
        <p>Surely never in human experience has a more extraordinary story been given to the world than that of Mr. Arttur George. Felton, of 73, Portsdown-road, Maida Vale, London; N.W., the man who never slept; and it is a story of actual fact. Here it is:—" In 1905, as a result of an accident, I lost the sight of one eye. An opera- tion 'had to be performed, after which I tffered from Neurasthenia and Insomnia, and became paralysed. I was turned _ _ _</p>
        <p>out of hospital incur- able, and told F could never work again, cond- pensat ion being awarded me. I could</p>
        <p>just get about- with great difficulty, dragging my right _ foot; and _my</p>
        <p>right liand being helpless</p>
        <p>Then came sle-epless-ness. Do• what I would, or take what I would, I never</p>
