      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>FOR THE BAELSWATURV McDOUGALL'S PATENT SELF RAHNC MDR THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY,NOVENII3ER 15, 1913. IN THE COURTS. WOMEN'S QUESTIONS. FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FOR THE BAELSWATURV McDOUGALL'S PATENT SELF RAHNC MDR THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY,NOVENII3ER 15, 1913. IN THE COURTS. WOMEN'S QUESTIONS. FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>GOLD L\ THE PIGSTY. A Stor.). of Missing Cash.</p>
        <p>The story of the gold which found its way' into a pigsty was told at the Warrington Police Court on Saturday, when David Algie (18), 18, Barnard-street, and Harry Walmsley, jun. (16), 5, Hepherd-street, were charged with stealing .1.142 145., the property of Robert Smith, from a trap in Liverpool-road, on October 31. Alexan- der Algie, sen., and Alexander Algie, jun., were charged with receiving £37, knowing it to have been stolen. The Chief Constable said when the theft was reported Detective-Sergeants Reddington and Holland made inquiries, but no trace of the property could be found at that time. From what came to the knowledge of the police, Da2vid Algie and Walmsley were taxed with the theft, but both denied it. Subsequently David Algie admitted he had stolen th% bag, whilst Walmsley 'was with him. They ran along Evelyn-street and got to some fields. Each got £2 175., and it seemed Algie did not "give away" to Walmsley the fact that there were £4O in the bag. Walmsley spent 10s. He was told Algie admitted stealing the bag, and that he had implicated him (Walmsley), but the latter denied any connection with the theft. Subsequently he admitted having £2 175., and produced £3 75., which he accounted for by saying that he originally had £2 175.; he spent 10s., and that Algie gave £1 to another but, knowing it to be stolen, he (Walmsley) took it back. Person, The police went to the house of Alexander Algie, sen., and told him that some money had been stolen, and the police believed he had some in his possession. At first he denied this, but subsequently admitted he had gone to a pigsty and seen his son David burying the money, and asked him what he was doing. His son handed him £37. The father then went to the manager of a public-house, and asked him to look after the money for hindi. The manager said, "Why, what's the !matter; is it ' straight '?" Prisoner replied, "Oh! yes,” and added that it wasNnoney he had had left to him, and that he did not want his wife to know. Later, prisoner brought £l7, and the manager took possession of it. The police found the £l7, and discovered that Alexander Algie, jun., had been implicated. He at first denied knowledge of the affair, but quickly afterwards gave up £2O. nd David Algie was committed for four months' hard cabour and Walmsley for two months. Alexander Algie,, sen., was sentenced to one month's hard labour, and Alexander Algie, jun., who was given a ,good character, was bou over to keep the peace for twelve months.</p>
        <p>CARE Or THE HORSE'S FEET.</p>
        <p>In the autumn Of the year, when the roads become. muddy, and the-stable floors and stalls become . damp and dirty, the skin of the ex- tremities of horses is exposed to the irfitation of dirt, filth and cold, a condition which is apt to result in .an inflammation or cracked ulcers on a horse's foot. The most important fact to call attention to is that filth is the most active of these agents, and can be controlled by the person in charge of the animal. The coarse breeds of horses, especially those with white feet, are most subject to the disease. In regard to the stall; keep it free from, holes which collek,t urine and filth. The manure should not. be allowed to accumulate, and plenty of dry bed- ding should be provided. If the horse has been driven over muddy roads, it is advisable to allow the limbs to ,dry before cleaning. If it is convenient to wash the legs with tepid water while the mud is still fresh, and then dry them with a towel or rub-cloth, fairly good re- sults may be obtained. But a better method consists in scraping most of the mud off when fresh and allowing the rest to dryi then with a brush it may be removed thoroughly and with- out irritation. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT:</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT:</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor, Councillor G. W. Joseph (in the chair), Aldermen Smethurst, Evans, Bol- ton, Councillor Wilkinson, Messrs. W. Roberts, S. Wright, W. Furness, G. Pining</p>
        <p>and T. Locker. EVERYBODY'S FRlEND.—Charles Barlow, 21, King-street Woessti Manchester, was charged on remand ructing a L. and N.-W. Rail- way w i t h b ticket collector and refusing to show his ticket when requested.—The Chief Constable stated that when the prisoner was previously before the Court he behaved in a very strange manner, and was committed to the Workhouse to be kept under medical observation. The doctor reported that the prisoner was sane. On the morning of November Ist, as prisoner was retiring from the station he was asked to show his ticket, but, he refused and later ran back to the platform, where he locked himself in a lavatory.—Prisoner, who again behaved in a strange fashion, stated that he got a.ticket the previous night for Newton-le-Willows; but had not travelled by the trains, having stayed on the platform all night—The Chief Constable described him as a past master of strategy. He was asked where he had friends, and he replied, "I have friends everywhere; at least I hope so." —He was fined 10s. .and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. W. Richardson, Alderman Tinnion, and Mr. T. Locker. .</p>
        <p>'Gray, or Johnson, We- ant AliciaEikN OnF FuEeNkc4—, ,Tohr oa ohsnson, re charged on remand with obtaining food and lodgings by false pretences from Mrs. Hettie Twist, of 20, 'Parker-street.—The Chief Constable stated that the defendants went to Mrs. Twist's house and asked for lodgings. They frequently stated that the male prisoner was in receipt of :t2 10s. a week, in addition to £l5 a month.—Mrs. Twist stated that prisoners said they were to open a tobacconist's shop in the town, when they re- ceived the ..215 on the following Monday. On that day they were to have paid her, but on going to their room to make the fire she dis- covered they had decamped, and she informed P.C. Hawkins, who arrested them at the sta- tion; where they had taken tickets for Liver- pool. When the constable charged them they stated they were going on to Liverpool, and would have paid afterwards. The male pris- oner stated that he had not the slightest inten- tion of defrauding.—Buckley stated that she was living with the other prisoner, who had said he was going to marry her. They had a shop in Stockport, and the first week he gave her a sovereign, and this led her to believe that he had money coming to him, because nothing was coming in from the shop. It was on the Sunday night she found they had no money, and during the next morning, when she went into the room, she discovered that he had gone and taken the four bags. When she noticea this she took her hat and was off. (Laughter).— Previous convictions were proved against both prisoners, and Gray was sent to prison for three months and Buckley for fourteen days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>WEpNESDAY,</head>
        <p>WEpNESDAY,</p>
        <p>Before Mr. J. Hayward (in the, chair), Alderman Smethurst, and Mr. W. T. Williams. DRINK THE REASON.—Maria Osborne, 120, Win- wick-road, w4as charged with stealing on the 10th November a sable fur, value Bs. 6d., the proper 6; of Mary Jane Mercer, from the kitchen of the Bay Horse Hotel.—The Chief Constable said that the prosecutrix was in the room and. the fur was on the sideboEtrd. Prisoner came in and had two glasses of drink. After she had left the room the fur was missed. On a search being made the 'fur was found in a drawer in 'prisoner's house.—The husband said his wife had no .ueed to do such a thing. It was the. drink.—Prisoner was bound over for twelve months. A SERIOUS CASE.—William Fudge, Ship-yard, was charged with having assaulted Louisa Ann Singleton.—The Chief Constable said Detective- Sergeant Reddingfon saw prisoner strike plain- tiff, knock her against the wall and kick her. He asked for a remand, so that a more serious charge could be preferred against prisoner. He had, it was alleged, been living wholly,t or in part, on the immoral earnings of the woman.— Prisoner was remanded until to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. J. Rymer Young (in the chair), W. Roberts, W. T. Williams, A. J. Jolley, and</p>
        <p>Dr. Naden. PANES AND PENALTIES.—GeOrgEt Naylor, no fixed abode, was charged on remand with breaking panes, of glass, value £7, in the Raven Hotel, on November sth.—Superintendent Williamson said prisoner went into the public-house in a drunken condition. He told the landlord to come ,for an argument as publicans were 9,11 iobbers and thieves. He refused to quit the premises and was ejected. jHe waved his stick about and threatened the landlord. He smashed the glass with his stick.—Mr. Roberts: I saw the prisoner in the Morning at Afarket Gate, acting like a madman.—Naylor was sent to prison for one month. THEFT OF A SHEET.—Annie Monks, no fixed abode, was charged with stealing, from 25, Wilderspool-causeway, one bed sheet, value 35., the property of Rhoda Vernon.—Superintendent Williamson said prisoner was a married woman parted from her husband. She had lived in common lodging-houses, but was arrested the previous day at the Workhouse. She had done washing for Mrs. Vernon. When she had gone the sheet was mimed. Prisoner had pledged it for is. 3d.—She was sent to prison for 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <p>ItEGINALD CIOODWIN, FOR SILKS OF MERIT. 401 n. Cymberline, pa. 6id. 40in. Roman Satin, 3s. 6d. 40in. Enioleuse, 4s. 11d. 401 n. Printed and Plain Ninons, /a. hid. It ss. 11d. 40in. Soft Broches, 2s. Md. to 10s. 6d. Post Free. 'Phone 5883 City. Saturdays, 1 o'clock.</p>
        <p>LIDGEFIELD, KING-STREFM,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>MANCHESTER.</head>
        <p>MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>The Approaching Winter and its</p>
        <p>Effects on Fashion.</p>
        <p>WOMEN AND BOOKS.</p>
        <p>Women are peculiarly fitted. by Nature to have the, care of books; that is when they are book-lovers, for the sex is generally dowered with patience, and is neat fingered, two gifts necessary to the work. Many owners of valu- able private libraries realise that books require care and attention, just as do clothes and fur- niture. Women who have learnt, bookbinding, who can do cataloguing—a., very interesting form of labour—and can mend books neatly, may find pleasant positions as private libra- rians, although the vacancies may not be numerous. Women in public, libraries are not well paid, in consideration of the high quali- fications expected of them. Books are sadly</p>
        <p>neglected in the majority of houses, where very</p>
        <p>little care is bestowed upon them, yet a room without books can scarcely be considered fur- nished or homelike.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>LONDON'S "LITTLE SEASON."</head>
        <p>LONDON'S "LITTLE SEASON."</p>
        <p>When " drear " November arrives, the "little season" in London is generally in full swing, and this winter proves no exception., Tango teas and dinners are given every day bywarious hostesses, the theatres find many patrons, and the gayest of supper parties adjourn afterwards to, crowded restaurants. The smart world comes tip to town for week-ends now, instead of going /into the country, as in summer. Yachting is over, and the Scotch moors are far too cold and bleak to attract those who can chase the sun, and hie them to the South, when winter sets in. These migrants all foregather in London, before their flight to Cairo, the Riviera, India, Madeira, or perhaps the Colonies, and appear loth to leave the gay capital, which will continue its gaieties until the near approach of Christmas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>DISTINGUISHED IRISHWOMAN.</head>
        <p>A DISTINGUISHED IRISHWOMAN.</p>
        <p>The late Hon. Emily Lawless, the eldest daughter of the third Baron of Cloncurry, was perhaps the most distinguished literary Irish- woman of her time. ..She received an honorary degree from the University of Dublin, and was a poet, novelist, and historian. Her historical work on Ireland is exceedingly valuable, and her novels, written 'rather late in her. life, met at once with appreciation and warm admira- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>TANGO WINTER.</head>
        <p>A TANGO WINTER.</p>
        <p>The Tango, which has become something like a mad craze with society, and is rapidly oust- ing " bridge " as a popular diversion, is said to be an evolution of a Pyrric war ,dance, in- vented by Pallas Athene. In the Egyptian sec- tion of the Britrsh Museum, dancers at Thebes are represented in paintings and sculpture dancing the Tango, their only garment a girdle of gold thread. This beautiful art is religious in its origin, and symbolised the creation of the world, and the procession of the stars. Many dances produce sadness, and the mys- ticism of the Tango, with its complicated steps, suggests a. sacred origin. It was, a French professor believes, danced by the Egyptian and Chaldean priests, and represented their attempt to pqrtray the evolution of the Supreme Being.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>OLD GAME REVIVED.</head>
        <p>THE OLD GAME REVIVED.</p>
        <p>Many games now quite unknown were played by rich and poor in olden" times. Before the Rebellion every village played stoolball, and women played it as eagerly as men. It is a compromise between cricket and the once popu- lar rounders. The game has recently been re- vived in Sussex, where there are several women's Clubs, and as it is an admirable game for women and girls,,it Will probably be adopted next year iri other eountiem Te daily routine of women in villages is mono- tonoUs in the extreme, so much less being done for their recreation than for the men, but a game the middle-aged of both sexes can enjoy in the open air deserves encouragement. So many outdoor games are played under cover, tennis and badminton, for instance, and now it is the turn Of bowls to be played .indoors. Every Aar women take more interest in this old game, and several ,clubs have been formed in London and the provinces. WHITE FOR MOURNING.</p>
        <p>Despite the example set by Royalty, mourn- ing is becoming less and less "deep." Crape is Tardy used, and, grey touched with black is frequently substituted for all black. By women whose' diess allowance is elastic, white is donned, but this, especially in winter time, is a somewhat costly procedure. The materials chosen are soft satin with a dull finish, cache- mire de soie, crepe de chine, chiffon, bengaline and taffeta. No jewels of any kind are worn With white mourning. e• CONCERNING CORAL.</p>
        <p>Coral is in great request just now, and looks especially well with a black gown. There are dog collars, earrings, chains, combs, bracelets and coronets of coral, the brick red and the pretty pink catering for two very diverse com- plexions, the one for the dark beauty and the other for the fair complexioned. Good coral is somewhat expensivd, especially that of the .un- clouded order ,carved in intricate designs. The best Coral hails from Italy and Japan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>kMETHYSTS.</head>
        <p>kMETHYSTS.</p>
        <p>Chiefly owing to its reasonable cost, the amethyst has long been in the background, but quite lately some of the great jewellers have been using it with pearls, and it is not impos- sible that it may become a far more cOstly stone. Those among'us who possess a store of amethysts should therefore hold it in reserve. Queen Alexandra has a great affection for amethysts, so also has Princess Arthur of Con- naught, who received on the occasion of her marriage some exquisite pieces of jewellery containing these gems.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>• . .. . ... . . .„ .4., \\f' ',: „ '-n:All-.: • ..,10/ ,- ..,-'''..i., ”I do admire your Xmas Cakes they are so beautifully tasty and rich. I wish I could make mine as nice." ' "Well, my dear, it is the simplest thing in the world. Just try this 'recipe it's so economical." PLUM CAKE.—Flour. 1-lb: Raisins, i-lb : Currants, i-lb : Candied Peel (cut small) 1-oz: Sugar. 6-ozs : Butter, 8-ozs: .a little grated Nutmeg: new Milk, one teacupful: 1 heaped Tablespognful of Bird's Egg Substitute. , Mix in the usual way, mixing is the Egg Substitute last. . its. BIRD'S . . EGG , SUBSTITUTE , • POWDER , , that gives that richness and does away . with the use of fresh or stale eggs. Send a pogtcarrl to Alfred Bird 8c Sons, Ltd.. Birming- ham. for new illustrated "Ohle English Plum Pudding Recipe Book." • Be 102: Another Cookery flint next week</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>GRINDING TEETII IN COWS.</head>
        <p>GRINDING TEETII IN COWS.</p>
        <p>The cause of grinding the teeth is generally indigestion, or some derangement of the diges- tive apparatus, which, as you say, is some- times a forerunner, of disease. At this time of the year, the pasture has become, if old, coarse and indigestible, and, if young, sour and watery.. Give, the following drench: Epsom salt, -12 oz. ; powdered ginger, oz.;l powdered gentian root, oz.;i water or. warm gruel, 1 quart. Feed on small quantities of bran, crushed oilcake, nicely salted, giving some good old long hay, which may be steamed; or a good plan is to sprinkle some boiling water in which has been dissolved some treacle over the hay, allowing it to remain for about half an hour before feedina. Cows are very fond of food prepared in this way, and even the oats, &amp;c., may be served the same. .Care shmild tie taken not to make it 'too wet, only damping it. and as the pastures are very bleak a warm feed given while milking will he found better than other food. Do not use large quantities of barley meal or pea meal. The following powder given sprinkled over food in doses of two tablespoon- fuls for each cow, after first giving the aperient dose, will act as a useful spice and digestive, and may be used. daily. Salt, 1 part: bicar- bonate rif soda, 1 part; powdered aniseed, 1' part; powdered ginger, 1 part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>SELECTION OF BREEDING PIGS.</head>
        <p>SELECTION OF BREEDING PIGS.</p>
        <p>Before buying a fresh boar to head the herd, it is important, says a writer in ," Farm and Home," that a man should be clear in his mind as to the type of pig he wishes to raise. Sup- posing that a man is a breeder of Berkshires, Large Blacks, Yorkshires, or any other pure breed, it is no use his taking round with him a copy of the standard, and then expecting to find a pig which will come up to the ideal. Even supposing he should be successful, he must not expect that by the introduction of this pig into his herd, he will, as a result, breed ideal pigs. In breeding pigs, or any other farm animal, one has to go slow, and gain the ideal step by step. If your sows are very coarse, do not get a little short boar, -expectizig lo get a happy medium between the two extremes, but get the male'a little nearer the type you want. After two or three crosses in this way, the de- sired type may be secured. Where a bieeder uses only one boar in his herd, he should select his sows as near the type he desires, or slightly coarser and more lengthy than he de- sires, and theli secure a boar slightly more blocky or compact, with more quality and fin- ish than the sows. Some breeders have a variety of type in their herd, keeping several boars to impress the types desired, thus being able to suit a greater variety of customers. Study your animals, so as to know how to select your breeding stock for the best results, and to know what you need to add to it year by year to give you the type you need to bring your herd up to the standard'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>Pinks are not usually associated with roses, but used as an edging to the beds they are most effective. When the flowers are over, the foliage itself is pleasing. Plant narcissi in borders for a spring dis- play, leaving a Space -of eight inches between the larger bulbs, reducing the distance accord- ing to size down to three or .four inches. Let the tips of the bulbs be three inches below the surface. Put tulips Six inches apart and three inches deep in grouPs. Insert single hyacinths four inches deep and about eight inches apart. The singles/in -11 mix- ture of colours are best Mr' outdoors: Take cuttings of currants and gooseberries r . from one foot to fifteen inches long. Remove the buds below and they several Inches yienclihmedseasbuomve_ the planting surfaCe, to prevent suckers. In gt‘iNe'enlDtveegcri months'dotwiotthis so transplanting f time shrubs may safely be planted up to the end of March, but evergreens should be got in as early as possible. Trench or dig deeply, adding manure if required, and leave the soil to settle before planting. Spread out the roots naturally, and keep them as near the surface as. possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>DRESSING A LAWN.</head>
        <p>DRESSING A LAWN.</p>
        <p>Poor lawns should now be given a dressing of some description. Sand is Usually applied to groung of a heavy nature, and a mixture of fine loam and • leaf-mould for a soil of a light nature. In each case scatter the material evenly over the surface. Basic slag is useful for a moist lawn; Kainit benefits, a lawn in which worms are prevalent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>TIIE BURNING BUSII.</head>
        <p>TIIE BURNING BUSII.</p>
        <p>Dictamnus Fraxinella is commonly known as the Burning Bush. A resin or juice is exuded, the vapour from which, on a dark night, takes fire on a lighted match being applied. If the plant is gently -rubbed a lemon-peel odour is emitted, but if it is bruised it has something of a balsamic smell. It is an easy plant to grow, and does well in a light and dry soil. It is a good border plant, and is worih growirig on account of its great • beauty and fine frag- rance. It flowers during June and July. and the seeds ripen in September, which, if then sown,•soon germinate. The plants can also be propagated by root cuttings, and make plants quicker than from seedsi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>AUTUMN CHRYSANTHEMUMS.</head>
        <p>AUTUMN CHRYSANTHEMUMS.</p>
        <p>The following are the names of September flowering chrysanthemumS which were shown at the Crystal Palace. This list will be useful to those amateurs desirous of making a selec- tion :—EI Draco, rich amber; Emperor, bright crimson, golden reverse ', Alminante, chestnut- crimsop ; Hollicot Bronze, bronzy-ymlow ; Dolores, bronze terra-cotta; Golden Queen of the Earlies ; Perle Chatillonaise, creamy-white; Le Pactole, bronze-yellow ; lames Bateman, pink and white; Mrs. W. Sydenham, deep crimson; Hector, mauve-pink; Improved Masse, rosy-lilac; Polly, rich yellow; Goacher's Crim- son; Crimson Marie Masse; Hollicot Yellow; Cranford Pink, mauve=pink ; Debutante, rosy- white; Princess, pale pink; .Beth Spark, rosy- pink; Pramtield Early White; Mme. Ilene Drouett, white; La Parisienne, deep mauve; Jimmie, crimson-purple;- Cquntess, white; Golden Glow, yellow; Caledonia, white; Crimson Folly; Dolly Beeves, deep • pink; El- stob Yellow; Nina Buick, reddish-bronze; Ralph Curtis., creamy-white; Carrie, bright yellow.</p>
      </div>
