      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>LARGE ILLUSTRATED CATALOGUE</head>
        <p>LARGE ILLUSTRATED CATALOGUE</p>
        <p>will convince you of this fact. FIFTY YEARS' REPUTATION FOR VALUE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>11E1Ye.MILES,</head>
        <p>11E1Ye.MILES,</p>
        <p>NEW STOCK BIRDS</p>
        <p>34,36,38,40,42.44,46,48. LONDON 'PHONE: /2/'7.ROYBI fl/RN/51/Wl/YERAOOI. LIVERPOOL</p>
        <p>oi le pea or Lean meal, one maize (when the \\ rather, is cold), and half a part lean meat, is about as good a preparation for laying hens-- as it is possible to obtain. For afternoon feed- ing, wheat, barley, and a little maize are the most suitable. -The mixture of meals should. always be supplied warm; rather than supply cold soft food, it is preferable to feed upon- grain alone, although this plan is not, of course, recommended.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Hints of Service to Amateur Poultry</head>
        <p>Hints of Service to Amateur Poultry</p>
        <p>Keepers.</p>
        <p>EGGS IN THE WINTER.</p>
        <p>In November many are requiring new stock birds, for it is generally realised that it is much better to buy early in the breeding season. Great care should be exercised in the purchase Of new stock, since one injudicious mating may spoil the work of years. There are, however, nowadays so many reliable breeders who specialise in prolificacy and good table quali- ties .that the ditlicultS, is nothing like so great as it was a 'few years ago. When requiring any new stock birds write to a trustworthy breeder, telling him exactly what is wanteil, and the price one is willing to pay. Preferably, the breeder should be one who lives some distance away, so as to avoid even the least danger of obtaining a, related bird. Contrary to the opinion of Many poultry- keepers, the cock exerts a, considerable influ- ence upon both the laying and table qualities of the offspring, and thus, if a male 'bird from a first-class laying strain is secured he speedily improves the egg record of the flock. A new bird should be kept by himself for a few days in order to see whether he is thoroughly healthy, as many of the infectious diseases from which fowls suffer may easily be brought into a yard!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>ANOTHER LAYING COMPETITION. .. .</head>
        <p>ANOTHER LAYING COMPETITION. .. .</p>
        <p>The laying competitions at the -1-1-a-r-p-e-r.A-dams Agricultural College, Newport, Salop, and at Sedlescombe, under the management of Mr. J. N. Leigh, and for which the Development Com- missioners have made grants, both commenced last month. Entries have been received from all. parts of the .country, and although white Wyandottes and white Leghorns predominate, several breeds are represented. The breeds entered for the Sedlescombe competition, where accommodation was provided for thirty-Lwo pen i in each division, consist of the following: Twelve pens white Wyandottes, eleven pens white Leghorns, two pens buff Orpingtons, two pens white Orpingtons and, one pen each buff Plymouth Rocks, black Leghorns, . salmon Faveralles, red sussex and Rhode Island Beds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>VALUE OF STIMULANTS.</head>
        <p>THE VALUE OF STIMULANTS.</p>
        <p>I • It is ilmistake to place foci() much reliance upon stimulating powders. Some poultry- keepers are in the habit of supplying. them throughout the winter months, increasing the supply as the effects ibegin to wear off, but more harm than good is done by such means. During very cold weather the addition of a little stimulating powder to the morning mash is beneficial, as it assists in maintaininr, the terri- perature of the body; with backward pullets, too, a little hastens development somewhat, and probably causes the fowls to commence to lay _rather sooner than they would otherwise.</p>
        <p>There are some very excellent powders on the market at the present time, including mus- tard, and there is no difficulty in selecting a good one. How and in what quantity they should be used must depend upon the circum- stances. • The best method of supplying- the powder is to mix it with the morning mash, as in this manner each bird will receive its proper share. The quantity to be supplied depends upon the preparation that is used. As a general rule, full directions are sent with the powder, and these should be followed carefully.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>WINTER EGG.</head>
        <p>THE WINTER EGG.</p>
        <p>Everybody wants eggs during the winter months. Whatever branch of the poultry in- dustry is undertaken, eggs are wanted, and wanted badly, , from November to March. It, needs no elaborate calculation to prove that *it pays better to produce eggs during December and January, when they are worth 2d. or 21d. each, than during the spring i when they are not worth more than fourteen or sixteen a shilling. The.dealers on the London and other first-class Markets are complaining that eggs are extremely scarce just now, and they are offering very tempting prices for new-laids:. The laying stock requires to be generously fed upon foods that are rich in carbohydrates and fat, as these are the two elements that assist so materially in increasing. the , egg supply. Barley meal is an extremely useful food for laying hens, though it is not employed nearly so extensively as it might be. Its price at first sight- may appear rather high as compared with some other Meals, but when its weight, as well as its feeding value, is taken into consid- eration, -it is comparatively a- cheap food. Three-parts barley-meal, two middlings, and</p>
