      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>MONEY BEST UNTOUCHED.</head>
        <p>MONEY BEST UNTOUCHED.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Ogden, preachingat the Gol- borne-street Baptist Chapel on Sunday evening —Temperance Sunday—took for his address "Canon Willis and the local breweries." He said that the Rector had gone out of his way to thank the local breweries for the help they, had given to the funds of the Parish Church. Mr. Ogden quoted "The Examiner" report of the Rector's speech at the recent sale of work, which stated that the Rector "thought it was very good of the breweries to help them, and he believed that the breweries did respect the Church for its work, and further they had "reason to be pleased and proud of the connec- tion between the two:" Mr. Ogden said it would be hardly just to deal with that question without paying a tribute to the wo,rk of the Parish Church, and withbut uttering his deep reg trd for all its workers,_ and an expression of admiration for the magnificent and beautiful church they had in their midst. One might ask the question, "What purpose can be served by alluding to this utterance of the Rector's?" Had it been the utterance of some irresponsible layman or irresponsible curate, one coll.&amp; have afforded to ignore it. Free Churchmen looked upon the Rector as the official head of the Church of England in that town, and as embodying the opinion of the Church of England. Naturally then, one conceived that the utter- ances of the Rector were utterances of very serious iniportance. Whatever the local brew- eries might have done for the Parish Church„ the general effect of "The Trade" in the coun- try was to make null and void the work of the Churches, and whatever gratitude might be in the mind for an individual act of generosity to a religious organisation, that gratitude was overwhelmed by the anger it had kindled when they remembered the damage that was done by "The Trade" to the ethical and spiritual life of the nation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>" Connived at Destruction."</head>
        <p>" Connived at Destruction."</p>
        <p>If that " Trade " had connived at the destruc- tion of child life, at the destruction of domestic felicity, and at the robbing of motherhood of its glory, when, where, and how should the Church be thankful to it? it was not a temperance bigot, it was not a preacher of the Gospel who declared "that drink was responsible for a con- siderable part of the lunacy in our country. Thousands of. people were brought to a prema- ture death every year through indulgence in alcoholic liquor, and the large sum of money spent every year on drink was spent quite un- necessarily, and this traffic was responsible for crime, immorality, disease, cruelty and death." These were the words of Mr. Philip Snowden, when speaking at Leicester in October of last year.</p>
        <p>If "The Trade" was responsible .for crime, immorality, disease, cruelty and death, in what way had the Church any need to be grateful to breweries?</p>
        <p>Leavtng the wider area, and considering our own town, Mr. Ogden said that one unfortun- ately had been compelled to be a spectator of shocking scenes. A policeman on duty declared "that such madness he could understand in India, with the climate at 100 in the shade, but in this country, and with their teaching, it baffled him that such scenes should occur." Let them walk through their streets and pass into the courts and into the alleys and yards, and watch the constant frequency with which women were going to and returning from the public-houses, and see the men on Saturday night, sadly clad for the period, when the week's work was over, returning in a state that would lead them to doubt the sanity of the human mind. That could all be seen within a three Or four hundred yards radius of the Parish Church. The work of the Sunday school was reduced to a farce by the open temptation of the street, and by the open doors of the public- house. In what way Christian Workers had need to be grateful passed his comprehension.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Use and Abuse.</head>
        <p>Use and Abuse.</p>
        <p>But, perhaps, one might reply that drink was abused rather than used, and the sad, sickening spectacles of "The Trade" were evidences of human wills which were weak and frail, and that ,the business of ethical society should be to produce men and women who could know how to use and not abuse. That certainly wiks the highest ethical state, but he ventured to sug- gest that the Church *as the last society in the world to believe in the.elimination of the weak willed by self-destruction, and surely she was the first society to see to it that the weak willed or the tainted by heredity should be protected and saved from ithemSelves. That was impos- sible Until a stronger chain and a fiercer grip was placed upon "The Trade," and he, ven- tured the belief that a firm refusal to com- promise and a refusal of brewers' money would in the long run, best serve the interests of the Church towards the uplifting of humanity.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>LIBERAL WOMEN OF RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL WOMEN OF RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Women Liberal Association inaugurated their winter campaign at the Bethesda Emery Hall on Monday, when Mrs. J. T. L. Brunner, wife of the member for the division, presided. Lady Holland gave an address on the Mental Deficiency Act. Referring to rural housing, she said in some instances it was shown that the farm labourers' standard of living was not even up to that of paupers. That, to her mind, was a disgraceful state of affairs. They found that cows, pigs, and horses were housed under better circumstances than the people who .were en- gaged to look after them. Mrs. Baker referred to. the question of temper- ance, and said the Liberal Government was the friend of the temperance worker. In the United Kingdom 79,350 insane people were under care, and 27,000 of those had alcoholism as the assigned cause, of insanity. They cost the country about £772,000 per year for their up- keep.</p>
        <p>Four horses were suffocated in a filig which occurred at Heddon Town Farm, near New- castle-on-Tyne.</p>
        <p>CLOTHS PRAISED BY RUSKIN. The St. George's Guild, of which Ruskin, was master, founded at Laxey, in the ,Isle of Man, a mill for the production of good honest cloths. Any- one needing Dress Lengths, Suiting-a, Rugs, etc., as recently supplied to H.M. the King, of unim- peachable quality, and beautiful design, is invited to send for patterns to THE RUSKIN HOMESPUN, 110, St. George's Mill. Laxey, Isle of Man; or G. HOLYROYD &amp; SON 110, Station-street, Eudderadeld.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Setting Forth of Harold the Brave to the Rescue of Ulster Maidens</head>
        <p>The Setting Forth of Harold the Brave to the Rescue of Ulster Maidens</p>
        <p>FROM UNKNOWN DANGERS.</p>
        <p>Once upon a time in the ancient city of War- rington_ there dwelt a knight, named Harold the Brave. He was a great lighter; he fought with giants and dragons, and he delivered enchanted and imprisoned maidens. At the time of our story he heard of a thousand maidens in the north of the far-famed island 0f... Erin, whom •he fancied to be in imminent danger. Said he; " I. will buckle on my armour.; cross .the seas, and deliver these-maidens, their Ma's; and their little sisters and brothers. I will bring them to the safe shelter of the warm hearts, and nice homes of the town of my adoptiOn, by the crystal river." So this valiant knight called' together his friends, six thousand strong, who had in former times helped him, to don his beautiful tin suit of Tariff Reform armour. When they had all gathered together on Sonkey Green he thus addressed them: "The' adven- ture I am about to engage in is more haz- ardous, and, perchance, more glorious, than ever I have .hitherto set out upon. Igo far over the raging seas, to free a thousand maidens from danger. I wot not particularly What the danger is; but it is some danger Or Other. If I succeed in my knightly endeavour, and bring them back with me, will you receive them as sisters, and shelter them .from the storms we are going to raise, and the wars We are going tei fight in their native cities?" Five thousand voices answered back, . "We will." then said our knight: "Send in your names and location of your abodes, the number you can lodge, and your estate in the realm, to the Master of my horse, and take heed to do it right speedily."</p>
        <p>condescension if any member of the League desired to proffer any request, or ask any favour, before he set out on his hazardous endeavour. "For," said he, "It may be ye .will never more see. your hero, and my blood may stain the heather--I mean the bog." The invitation was at once taken advantage of by Motherly dame, who advanced and. asked the worthy, knight to, accept a phial containing. 4. chain' that would keep down his "vomit" (so- called in the plain speech of the early English) when crossing the. seas. . - This charm was accepted with graciousness, and tucked into a fold in his doublet.. Before the dame retired she urged him to remember to take an extra pair of hose, and a spare doublet, as. she had heard Ireland was a damp country. .And, further, that he must take great care of his coat of mail,-,as, they could-not go on buying new armour for every fresh adventure.</p>
        <p>When this dame retired, another stepped forward, and, curtseying low, asked if, in the proposed new Government of Uister, )vomen, who are Mostly home rulers, would be allowed votes? This question seemed to trouble the knight, but, recovering himself with his usual alertness, he replied " I am in principle against. Votes for Women, but I ever hold that principles are overned by pine, place, and occasion. In Ulster the women shall have votes." (Loud cheers, and a few groans.) .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <p>- "Will the august dames who keep our armoury array inc in coat of mail, for I,must forthwith speed on my hazardous adventurer S'o the Chief of the armoury, sometimes called the Dame._President, ordered the Tariff Reform armour to be taken down from the Walls, to be dusted, oiled, and cleaned with a preparation akin to that known in our own time as Monkey Soap. So they took down the 'helmet, the 'visor, the 'cuirass, the girdle, the gauntlets, the cuishes, the jambs, and the sollerets. The good knight was forthwith arrayed, and when all was finished and fitted he looked like a shining tin lobster. Then they brought him the great Tariff Reform sword, ,and the lady Of the armoury, curtseying low, presented it With fitting grace. The knight took it from the lady's hand, and looked lovingly upon it. Turning it over, he read the words engraver O n the handle and blade: "Make the foreigners Pay," "Trade will go," "Cotton will go," "Iron Will go," "Food taxes." These words :sorely Perturbed the knight's thoughts. In sudden wrath he flung the sword from him, unbuckled the armour, and told his affrighted attendants to' Sell these much-patched, worn-out, accursed lobster shells." But, recovering himself, he said more gently: "Stay—keep the sword, dinted and twisted though it be, as an emblem Of my—l mean our—great victory. Hang it high upon the wall, so that all may see the rusty blade, but none the errgraving thereon." Said he: "Should I. ever again require this Style of armour the fashion and the pattern Will surely again have changed, so keep only he sword. Instead thereof bring forth the gay ulster Orange armour I have of late worn When addressing the citizens; hi this alone will 'Ago forth." Be it here observed this was not the Znti-Home Rule armour which did service at he last conflict on wall, in hall, and in can- vass, but a suit of new design and pattern, blade in the ancient town of Belfast. So the la-riff Reform dames brought forth the'fine new rnour, harnessed the bold knight, gave to his s and, and buckled on his thigh the great bWerd of the rebellious covenanters. Then eing duly arrayed, he arose with a mighty uPrising. Twice, six thousand eyes gaze dwith rv,tt nitration- upon his stately form, noble bear- and shining armour. After fitting pause, raised his mailed right, hand on high, and hwere a mighty oath, and this was the swear ue Swore:— •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>OATH.</head>
        <p>THE OATH.</p>
        <p>"I, Harold the Smith; fitly -called the Brave, political champion of Warrington, belted knight and free-born Englishman, swear that I will do my devoir as Ulster's Orange Champion. Knightly, truly and Manfully I will do battle in this quarrel With any man whom the enemy may list against me, for the glory, or, if it may be, the death, of my political reputation, which. , 'boldly stake upon this hazard. May the Spirit of these six thousand women, and of MY great brother, Galloping Fred, le my Witnesses, and inspire and win for me .the. victory." This oath waS uttered, and sworn to, with Filetit solemnity and in awful silence. Then the call of the President the thousands of 41,4r,riff Reform women lifted their right hands, -A-li' responded together,.," We are witnesses." reneir a brief hush, a great burst of cheering air--.-and the band began to play. So were the goodly dames by this imposing the-111,°nY that they forgot their, sorrow about Avhi Phr°Posed sale of their favourite armour, to `e" had so often led them to victory, and thetienies. They consulted together , whether the-,:airle of their League should not be altered ao and then to "The 'Ulster Amazons," or s.uch title. __ "en the belted I:ni,rht. inquired with great</p>
        <p>When this dame retired there hesitatingly advanced a young Plamsel. who, bending low, seemed to have difficulty in proffertng her re- quest. ,The kin*• knight, to encourage her, patted her on the head with his gauntletted hand, sometimes called Mailed fist. The charm seemed to act, and the damsel began: " Worthy and valiant knight, of your courtesy prithee list to one who comes from the maidens of the town. They think it would be wiser, instead of taking women and children away from their homes, to bring over from Belfast one thousand goodly young gentlemen of high birth•and renown. Warrington is not a worthy place for women and children; the town is not clean, and 'a river flows through it into which the children might fall. Then women,, are always in the way, when you want 'to get on with housework, and men, especially the youth- ful, are not so, because they could in the day- time play at bowls and practise archery. Of an evening, before curfew, we might occasion- ally extend to them the courtesy of taking them to see the river, and the old bridge, and the Parish Church, and the moon, and -" This damsel was brought to a sudden halt by the uprising of Sir Harold, whose brow was dark, and whose look was Wm. In a-voice of \pas- sion, speaking, not to one, but to .all, he said: "I wish all to know that this is a serious mat- ter, and not one for joking. Will ye all please to remember-"" "The Fifth of November" piped in a youthful voice. At this there was an evident inclination to mizth amongst the younger portion of the crowd, that could scarce be restrained by their ,more serious elders.</p>
        <p>The knight, percpiving that no useful end would be served by further parley, cried: "To horse, ye brave." He mounted his coal-black steed of Orange breed, and matchless speed, and set forth, accoppanied by various knights, squires, the men and dames of quality in the town, and with them the citizens and their wives, a goodly crowd, and all companied him to the town's boundary, the Bridge over Sonkey Brook. Here -there was a triumphant parting scene. As he set out there sounded a great blast of trumpets, and the huge concourse cheered the knight, as, digging his spurs 'Mc his steed's flanks, he galloped away. But some of the women wept, for they said "Our Harold may be slain, and we may never see him more." And some said: "If , one thousand Ulster maidens get hold of our knight it will be the same to us as though he were slain in the tournament," and they too, wept sore. This is all I have to tell you of the arming for the Great Adventure of Harold the Btave.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>DOCTOR'S FAMOUS STOMACH</head>
        <p>A DOCTOR'S FAMOUS STOMACH</p>
        <p>PRESCRIPTION.</p>
        <p>A real lasting cure for digestive disturbances such as Indigestion, Flatulence, Aeldity, Con- stipation, Liver Trouble, &amp;C., is onred in the prescription of ,Dr. Meier, the famous stomach specialist. Dr. Meier says that if sufficient ozone can be supplied to the digestive organs its disinfecting and purifying effects makes diges- tive disturbances impossible. Dr. Meier offers all sufferers from stomach troubles the benefit of this prescription in the new Ozone prepara- tion, Stomoxygen, which can be obtained from all chemists, or a 2:0. for 2/9 to the Stomoxygen Co., Norwich-street, London E.C., will bring a package with full directions at once, pest free. Dr. Meier says further: "I cannot too strongly deprecate the practice of using the many -in purgatives, &amp;c., which are in such common use." ,</p>
        <p>COURT SCENE AT ST. HELENS. Francis Martin, a St. Helens collier, who made his eighty-first appearance before the St. Helens magistrates, on a charge of drtlnkenness, created a scene by addressing the Court in a violent manner. He was fined, and then thanked the Bench, saying that he was anxious to do what the governor of Snutsford Gaol had told him to do. The Chief Constable said that pri- soner had been examined by a doctor, but they could make nothing of him.</p>
        <p>Drink Makes Poverty.. Drink unmistakably makes poverty: Homes by the hundred could be found, into which vastly more than a living wage went, which were no longer homes, not even hovels, as the result of drink. It was "The Trade" that had allowed the woman, dressed in rags, with an infant at her breast, to stand at the bar,and silence her baby's cries by the gin from her glass. If the Church had reason to be thankful to "The Trade," one would believe that "The Trade." would combine to prevent young people entering licensed premises. Whtn a Bill was introduced to prevent children of tender years accompanying their parents into public-houses, did "The Trade" at once acquiesce, and con- sider it as beneficial? They opposed it with all the might and wealth they possessed. They resisted to the last ditch, and even when de- feated and the Bill became an Act, their com- missionaires were employed to take charge of the children at the doors whilst the mothers drank themselves insensible within, and even now "The Trade" was so willing to entice mothers to their premises that they undertook to find a nursery room and a nurse to attend to the children whilst the women were drink- ing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>e – A</head>
        <p>e - A</p>
        <p>Opened by Mrs. John Crosfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>PRETTY CHAPEL THAT WILL COST £2,400.</head>
        <p>A PRETTY CHAPEL THAT WILL COST £2,400.</p>
        <p>Independent Methodism has made many strides, and not the least is that at Grappen- hall, where, last Saturday, with due reverence, a new church .was declared- open. This cere- mony was the outcome of a decision arrived at three years ago, when the Methodist officials in the district were faced,with the problem which must come sooner or later to thriving denomina- tions, of inadequate accommodation; The pro- blem was attacked spiritedly, and now, on the Knutsford-road, stands the beautiful monument of their labour of love—a church capable of seating 250 worshippers. The original church was ecinimenced in 1855 in a• very small room adjoining Mr. Carter's shop, and the chapel just vacated was erected in 1880. The building of the new church was commenced early this year, and it is. estimated to cost about £2,400. The erection consists of a pretty- chapel, with minister's vestry and all necessary offices, a large assembly hall to seat 220, and four separ- ate class-rooms, in addition to a kitchen, and. the Yorkshire stone finish to the buildings gives it , a very noble appearance. Mrs. John Cros- field attended on Saturday to perform the offi- cial opening. ceremony, and she was supported by Councillor R. Henshall, who presided, the Mayor and Mayoress (C,ouncillor Dr. -G. W. Joseph and Mrs. Nield) and Aldermen Burton and Evans.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Crosfield, and alluded to the valuable ser- vice that lady had rendered them in coming from Colwyn Bay. Her remarks about the .Quakers were quite correct. If they had not been Methodists, in all probability they would have been Quakers. (Applause.) Alderman BURTON, in seconding, referred eulogisticallYr to Mrs. Crosfield's kindly disposi- tion. He also recalled his connection with the Grappenhall Methodists of 40 or 50 years ago, when they met in an upper room, and the prayers uttered there he believed were _respon- sible for the good they were doing. • He hoped and prayed that they would continue .to work, and the amount of good they would accomplish no one could tell. (Applause.) The motion was carried with much heartiness, and Mrs. CROSFIEI.D briefly responded. THE DANGER OF BEING TOO ' COMFORTABLE.</p>
        <p>The sc.hoolroom was packed in the evening, when several speeches were made, and an excel- lent concert given, over which -Mr. W. Beesley, of/Prescot, who is president of the Warrington Circuit. presided.</p>
        <p>The CHMRuA.V.. saidahey all put a high eSti- mhF3 upon the efforts of their friends at Grap- penhall. He had some. slight recollections 'of the old Grappenhall Church; and they all hoped that those dark days had gone by. He had noted that there was always a certain amount of fear- on the part of the older portion of a church, who had not been accustomed to the comfort that the younger members sought; and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>OPENING.</head>
        <p>THE OPENING.</p>
        <p>The weather was not ideal for such an occa- sion, but there was a very good attendance when Mr. P. SILCOCK, the architect, handed the gold</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Photograph by Burrell.</head>
        <p>Photograph by Burrell.</p>
        <p>AFTER THE OPENING. Reading from left to right :—Mr. E. Ralphs, Mr. P. Silcock, the Mayoress (Mrs. Nield), Mrs. John Crosiield, the Mayor (Councillor G. W. Joseph), and Councillor R. Henshall.</p>
        <p>'''.. -,Wfil.;tz-,;.41‘.1-.4p,',:,,;:e.:t4-f:'5;::, .- ik,•-. - :-.-'.•), , ;_, , ...: ,t 4. a ,e,.. ..,.. ~,,,,,,A,`...,-,",,,,,,,..„, ,„ ..,-;,':-';',.',.`•-„, ;.. • • -,''.:,,,,;''''''''',r ''.,..z. ,:, '' ,',',-- '‘,.' ''' . t.,;•-',, -'•&lt;.-!''' •: ', , - .•. , , , • ' la, • . ~., ‘46140i lwr , &gt; \ , '1 4 • u. ?-...-...., -.- ... .- . II? P .• . . ~ , - I . . . s ‘ 111 -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <p>key to Mrs. Crosfield, and requested her to declare the church open. It was, he said, the third tithe within a short period he had had this pleasing duty to perform,. and he could only say again that he was quite sure they could not find a more gracious lady to perform the cere- mony. (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>Mrs. CROSFIELD inserted the key in the lock and said: I now declare this church open in the Name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost, and I hope for its continued success." The key bore the inscription : "Independent Methodist Church, Grappenhall. Presented to Mrs. John Crosfield, viii. Nov. '13." Those assembled then proceeded into the church; where</p>
        <p>Mr. E. RALPHS, of Westholighton, the evangel- istic secretary, preached from I. Corinthians ii. 2.: "For I determine not to know anything among you, save Jesus Christ and Him cruci- fied." In concluding, he said that he .had a message for them. He rejoiced with them on the new life and wider service opening out to them. He was thankful that he had taken part in the Opening of that beautiful building for the worship and service of God, but they would accomplish little 'except in the name, and by the power of the Cross. They would have pro- blems, burdens, weakness, sin, indifference and antagonism, and to meet all these things would bring grief to their hearts. They were there to witness to the truth, and there were many people who did not want it and would turn upon them. The work would sometimes be monotonous and trying, but it would not always be so.</p>
        <p>MRS. CROSFIELD FAVOURS BRIGHT ANC</p>
        <p>CHEERY CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>After the service, tea was provided in, the schoolroom, and later, prior to Mrs. Crosfield's departure, she addressed the gathering in the church. She had great pleasure in being with them, she Wald, because she was delighted to be able to help them in their efforts to see that beautiful church and schoolroom opened. She understood that not many members of that congregation were, comparatively speaking, "well off," and a great many must have prac- tised self-denial in order to build the church. They. would, however, have the satisfaction and happiness of knowing they had done it for the furtherance of Christ's kingdom on earth. With. such an admirable site she thought they were well advised to erect such a handsome build- ing, which was a pleasing addition to the neigh- bourhood—instead of a plain and ordinary church. Bare and ugly chapels did not appeal to most people. Perhaps they did in the days of the Covenanters, bilt now people liked to see churches comfortable, bright and cheery. It was always a pleasure to look at good architec- ture, and it was best that they should make the House of God as perfect and beautiful as pos- sible. The young people had not been forgotten, for there was ample accommodation for them in the fine assembly hall. The schoolrooms were also very suitable for the purposes of a Sunday school, and for the various branches of church work. She was sure' that the church would be a great blessing to the many attracted within its walls. She was not acquainted with the Society of Friends, but she believed that. the Independent Methodists came nearer' than any other denomination to that society for the one _reason that. the , Independent Methodists had no paid ministers. ,(Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Claim Upon the Crosfield Family.</head>
        <p>Claim Upon the Crosfield Family.</p>
        <p>That neighbourhood had a claim upon the Crosfield family to a certain. extent:: It might interest them to know that the late Mr. George Crosfield, her husband's eldest brother, once lived at' "Fir Grove," where he went to live after his marriage in 1852, his wife being one of 'the daughters of the late Mr. Henry Ashworth. Some of those • present would no doubt remem- ber his name; and it was of interest to recall that he had a great deal. to do 4 with the repeal of the Corn Laws, and was a -friend of both Cobden and John Bright. A further reason why they had a claim upon her . was that the Grappenhall Garden City Suburb Wftg not far from that church, and they (her family) were the first to build two houses 'there. She ex- pressed the wish that others would be inclined to build there, and she Wag pleased that the pew church and schools were so handy. • She honed that the dc.iu would soon be cleared off. (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>THANKS TO MRS. CROSFIELD. Alderman Evyo: proposed a vote of thanks to</p>
        <p>he himself had often thought and felt that great danger lay in the places where worshippers were made too comfortable. He hoped that Would never occur to that church, and he trusted that the:true spirit would always be in them. He did not think there was any fear when they recognised the leaders in that church. He did hope that they might realise as Christians the necessity Of helping churches with which they were particularly connected. He wished every success to the friends at Grappenhall and the bold effort they had Made. (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>Councillor Roberts for: Bolton, James and John Evans, Forshaw and Smethurst. Councillor Birtles for: Bolton; James and John Evans, Forshaw and Smethurst.</p>
        <p>Councillor Broadhurst for: For Shaw, Archer, Chrimes, Wilkinson and Wilson. The Mayor for: Bolton, James and John Evans, Forshaw and Smethurst. THE ALDERMEN RE-ELECTED.</p>
        <p>It was evident before the actual figures were announced that the majority would be re- elected, and there was some speculation as to whether Councillor Wilkinson had gained suffi- cient votes to make him one of the five.</p>
        <p>After a few minutes the Town Clerk officially announced the result of the ballot as 'follows: _ _</p>
        <p>Ele-cted : James Evans, 13 votes; Forshaw, 13; Smethurst, 12; and John Evans, 11. For the fifth place there was a tie of ten votes each between Messrs. Bolton and Wil- kinson. .</p>
        <p>The MAYon : I must support our old colleagues and therefore I give my casting vote in favour .of William .Bolton. • (Applause, in which Coun- cillor Wilkinson joined.) Alderman Bolton was thus declared elected, and the unsuccessful candidates and the votes given werj as follows :</p>
        <p>Messrs. Wilkinson, 10; Joseph, 8; Wilson, 8; Chantler, 7; Cunnington, 5; Pemberton, 1; Chrimes, 1; and Archer, 1. •</p>
        <p>ONE HUNDRED YEARS SERVICE.</p>
        <p>'ln a few words the successful candidates ex- wessocl their thanks.</p>
        <p>Alderman JAMES EVANS said he had looked ,up the records of, the five Aldermen and found that between them they had given 100 years' of ser- vice to the Corporation, and he thought that a very creditable thing for five men to be able to .40. (Hear; hear.) If they had been com- pelled to retire they would have done it with- out disgrace. They had served their day and, generation to the best of their ability. (Ap- plause.)</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON, returning thanks, said he would always remember the action of the new Mayor in giving a casting yote in his (the speaker's) favour. (Applause.) ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>ALDERMAN JAMES EVANS'S COMPARISONS.</head>
        <p>ALDERMAN JAMES EVANS'S COMPARISONS.</p>
        <p>Alderman EVANS announced, amid laughter, that he would speak of the time when he was a juvenile. So many things seemed to cross one's mind on occasions of that kind that it seemed to create in his Mind, at any rate, a feeling that he wished he had been horn 40 years later— (laughter)—especially when he remembered some of the old conditions of things, and when he made -comparisons with the new. He re- membered very early when he was associated with the old barn, Sunday school. They had forms without backs. (Laughter.) Fortunately in, those days there was a very excellent minis- ter; he referred to the man of sainted memory— Joseph Williams. (Hear, hear.) He must have been a boy, for lads did not like long and dry sermons. (Laughter.) If they were dry they. could not go to sleep; there were no backs to the forms—(laughter)—and nothing to lean -upon but the 'next lad. (More laughter.) These things struck him when he went into the new church. He sat in a pew with a soft cushion in it, and he thought perhaps there was the danger that it might be too easy. It mattered not from what standpoint tkey were looking if the young men and women were to carry .on the work in that ney- house, and to excel and exceed all that. the old friends did in the old house it would be by the Cross; "Those soft cushions," he said, "will. not save you from the Cross, and if I can say just one word- that would give inspira- tion to the young men and women who are coin- ing on, that would 'help them in those days that our .friend, Councillor Henshall, referred to in the prayer—dark and misty days in connection with the Church—they will need as standard- bearers men and women who are not moved. easily." He wanted to encourage the young people, because things would not always be just as they wanted the, and if they were to reach the higher walks of spiritual life and experienet it would be by the- .Cross. He remembered the old 'house, and had been there on many a hot day. They had not been told that there was a bakehouse under it—(laughter)—but there was, and very often he had had his coat off. (Laugh- ter.) It was hot enough to take the waistcoat off, too. (More laughter.) But he had not for- gotten the old spiritual condition that obtained, and Up sweetness of the singing, although they were very much out,of tune—(laughter)—ah ! • there was something in it. They were not only trying to sing for it was possible to feel the spiritual . sweetness. If some of them could have seen many of the changes in the home life and the effect of Christianity in the churches ,1 they would be mere than satisfied. The old people such as he recalled to mind were pioneers of what they had in their churches. " Perhaps," remarked the speaker, "they did not do as excellent as we are doing in some directions, yet in others I am inclined to think they excelled, and whatever else we may say, we may say they (lid their best." - He recently told a friend in Bolton that he thought of goin,, to preach Some Sunday in his bakehouse clothes. His friend said -"What for?" and he replied :"" It I would disturb the monotony." (Laughter.) The Church had a good many problems to'solve, and it had the. material with which to solve them. They had a beautiful house with, a heautiful finish, and they would need more .beautiful money to pay for it—(laughter)—but he had always been a believer in systematically apPlyt ing what belonged to God to Him. He did not, want the young folk to think they could have pleasure land. do just as they liked, or the burden would fall upon their -shoulders for the carrying out (Applause.) DEVELOPMENT.</p>
        <p>• Mr. W. MORT said that he remembered going to the first service which was held in the chapel they had just vacated, lie had been suifering from toothache—(laughter)—and further it: was the day upon Which he had his first pair of cuffs. (More laughter.) As time went on, by some chance or other, be crept upon the plan. and was asked to take the service there, and here he was always able to find a few faithful old members. He had been thinking about the building development, and bow it arose out of the old place, and one thought which seemed to be rather impressive to him was that with the development of those buildings there must have been hearts growing larger and sympa- thies' wider. No structure could grow with- out the people becoming richer; he felt quite (Continued is next column.)</p>
        <p>NEW INDEPENDENT METHODIST CHURCH FOR GRAPPENHALL—cont.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ALDERMANIC SCRAMBLE.</head>
        <p>ALDERMANIC SCRAMBLE.</p>
        <p>Three Candidates who Received One Vote Apiece.</p>
        <p>AN EXCITING ELECTION.</p>
        <p>An exciting scramble ensued at the meeting of the Warrington Town Council on Monday for seats on the aidermanic bench. - There were five vacancies, and for these thirteen candidates were voted upon, three at the tail end of the list securing one vote apiece. The five retiring Aldermen were Messrs. W. Bolton, James Evans, .1o)in Evans, W. .1. For- shaw and J. Smethurst, and they offered them- selves again for election. Meanwhile rumour had been busy. that a section of thetouncil had decided to " mit the old gang," and speculation was rife as to whether they would succeed. The voting, however, showed that a well-pre- pared plot had been concocted, but this, like many schemes of mice and men, went " agley." When the new Mayor, Councillor P. Peacock, announced that Inc vole for aldermen would be taken, quite a buzz of excitement passed through the Council Chamber. The members finished filling in the voting papers, and handed them to the Mayor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>TIIE PUBLIC MUCH AMUSED.</head>
        <p>TIIE PUBLIC MUCH AMUSED.</p>
        <p>It was evident some scheme was on foot, and scarce had the Mayor started ,to read the names on the paper ere it became apparent that quite a number of the lesser lights in the Council had aspired to the. dignity of aldermen. .N‘Then some of the name S were disclosed the public at the back of the hall indulged in langliter:. The papers-slioive'd that the following thir- teen had been voted upon: Messrs. W. J. For- shaw, James Evans, J. Smethurst, John Evans, W. Bolton, J. Wilkinson, Dr. Joseph, W. J. Wilson, T. Charmer, E. Cunnington, J. Pember- ton, J. Chrimes and G. Archer. The MAYOR intimated that he would read out the names of the candidates each councillor had voted for. THE VOTERS' SELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Mayor did so, and this was the result : Councillor Wilkinson voted for: Bolton, John Evans, James Evans, Forshaw and Smethurst. Councillor Craik for Bolton, John and James Evans, Forshaw and Smethurst.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gough for: Chantler, Cunnington, Joseph, Wilkinson and Wilson. , Councillor Cunnington for :• Cha-htler, Cun- nington, Joseph, Wilkinson and Wilson. Councillor Pemberton for: Forshaw, Chant- ler, Joseph, Wilkinson and WilsOn.</p>
        <p>Councillor Lashford for: Chantler, Cunning- on, Joseph, Wilkinson and Wilson.</p>
        <p>Councillor. Chrimes for: Chantler, Cunning- on, Joseph, Wilkinson and Wilson. Councillor Archer for: Wilkinson, Chantler, CunningtOn, Wilson and Joseph. Councillor llalliwçll for: James Evans, foseph, Chantler, Wilkinson and Wilson.</p>
        <p>Councillor Lindley for: James Evans, John Evans, Smethurst, Pemberton and Wilkinson. Councillor Shaw for: James Evans, Forshaw, Smethprst, Joseph and Wilkinson. Councillor Henshall for: Bolton, James and John Evans, Forshaw and Smethurst. Councillor Locker for: Bolton, James and John Evans, Forshaw and Smethurst.</p>
        <p>Councillor Taylor for: Bolton, 'James and John Evans, Forshaw and Smethurst. Councillor Joseph for: Bolton, James and John Evans, Forshaw and Smethurst. Councillor Jenkins for: Bolton, James and John Evans, Forshaw and Sinetfiurst.</p>
      </div>
