      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>SMOKE AND DOMESTIC FIRES.</head>
        <p>SMOKE AND DOMESTIC FIRES.</p>
        <p>Open Fire Places Still the Popular Method of Heating our Homes.</p>
        <p>MAKE SMOKE rOD WASTE HEAT.</p>
        <p>While manufacturers are being asked to con- sume, as far as possible, their smoke, the domestic fireplace should not be forgotten. In towns like London, Manchester and Glasgow, it is calculated that 75 per cent. of the smoke comes from domestic fireplaces, and 25 per cent. from works' chimneys. For Warrington it is probable this ratio will be about reversed. Com- paratively speaking, Warrington is ,not densely populated, and the inhabited areas stretch out into the country in irregular directions. The number of works is comparatively large, and their special character necessitates a consider- able amount of smoke, although a good deal more is made than is at all necessary. Hence in Warrington the works' smoke may amount to 75 per cent. and domestic smoke to 25 per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Wasteful Abominations.</head>
        <p>Wasteful Abominations.</p>
        <p>The domestic open fireplace: in spite of nunr i rous rivals, is still n Warrington, as else- whe e, the popular method of heating rooms. Its cheerful appearance and the comfort of direct heat radiation, are generally recognised. A further point in favour of the open fireplace i is the effectual change of the air n the room by the chimney draught. Great improvements in recent years have been made in gas fires. Instead of the heat being given out by con- vection, it is now largely given off by radia- tion, a very great improvement, and likely to increase the popularity of gas fires. The essen- tial difference between i heat given off by con- vection and radiation s that the latter heats the solid objects it impinges upon, while in the case of convection, it is the adjacent air that is heated. This difference may be recognised when in the sunshine on a winter day we feel warm, although the surrounding air may be below freezing point. Place two thermometers, the one where the mercury is exposed to the sun's rays, and one in the shade, and although the i air which surrounds both s equally cold, the ' one receiving the sun's radiated heat will stand far higher than the ohe In the shade. This explains why we feel so much more comfortable in a room warmed by heat radiated from a fire, than one warmed by hot water pipes and "radiators"; this is the air temperature in both may be alike. It s curious why radia- tors should be so called: they scarcely radiate o by radiation, is an at all, but give off their heat by convection, i that is, the air n i contact conveys the heat. The improvement h gas fires, as stated, in in- creasing gimprovementen eral utshe. e heatß whichut gt open fireplace will long ren off is bringing them into more hold its own. Hence the need of an intelligent study of fireplaces and fires, both in relation to smoke and cost. Great progress has been made in recent years in the form of.flreplaces, but it is not yet recognised by many what a difference i the class of grates makes n the cost of heating a room. Of the improvements made may be Mentioned the introduction of angular covings' fire-brick backs and cheeks, glazed tiles sides and hearths; all these make the fire more effec- tive. We still see in many houses these waste- ful cast-iron abominations, with their high set barred grates, which were so common in the early Victorian time, and which have, so to i speak, .eaten their heads off n cost many and many a time. The back and sides of a grate ought to be made entirely of fire-brick. This being a bad conductor, retains the heat and acts as a source of radiation, whereas iron con- ducts the heat which escapes by the flue. The back of the grate should slope towards the hearth, which increases the power of radiation and converges the flame and smoke, bringing about ignition. The fire-bricks not only in- crease the radiation into the room, but the heat so radiated impinges on the gaseous and solid matters as they leave the fuel, and helps to heat these to the point of ignition. The absence of i fire-bars in modern grates s a distinct advan- tage, as ahything in front of the fire is an im- pediment to radiation: After all that can be said in its favour, we must recognise that the open fireplace is a most wasteful way of heat- ing a house. As much as 6per cent. of the solid portion of the coal escapes in the form of smoke, while a much larger portion goes up the chim- ney in the form of combustible gas.</p>
        <p>How Gas, Soot and Tar Are Formed. We now come to the question of the fire it- self. Let us suppose the fire has burnt down, and requires coals; we heap a •shovelful on the top of .the red-hot fire. ,The heat of the fire soon acts upon the new coal, beginning with the coals most closely in contact. A milky-like form of smoke is given off, this consists of tar vapour, coal gas and steam, the same as in the distillation of coal in a gas works. Indeed, in the ordinary fireplace, we distil a considerable portion of the gas out of the coal before we can get it to burn. ;The gas is too cool for ignition, and escapes up the chimney. During this period no soot is formed, but a considerable amount of the heating power of the coal is lost because there is no combustion. The tar vapour in this form of smoke mainly consists of minute vesicles or globules, which are in a state of violent motion. When they emerge from the chimney they ascend, being lighter than air, they gradu- ally cool and descend, when they strike any solid surface the tiny envelope bursts, the gas it con- tained is liberated, and its microscopic skin of tar adheres to the surface on which it strikes. This surface may be a stone all, the leaf of a plant, a child's pinafore, or an alderman's nose. It is this sticky adhesion quality that makes domestic smoke so objectionable. • This form of smoke does not occur to any great extent in furnaces, where the heat should always be suffi- cient to burn it up. It may occur to some of our readers that prevention of this waste could ; be effected by reversing the draught, making it a down-take draught instead of an upcast. This would only be prafficable by erecting a tall chimney to serve a group of houses, and which from its height would .suck the draught down into an underground flue to the chimney. By this means the gases given off by the fresh coal on top would be drawn through the red-hot coal below and burned. This plan has been thought of, but it must be apparent to all that the coals would take longer to ignite because of the wrong direction of the draught. Appliances have been devised for feeding to coal from below, a kind of coalbox has been built behind' the fire and coal fed in from above. This coal gets partially heated in the receptacle, and is brought into the fire from below at the back. I do not know what success this form of grate has achieved.</p>
        <p>To Prevent Smoke and Waste. We have described the first stage in the com- bustion of coal in fire grates; let us now pass on to the second. After a time, varying with the amount of coal that has been put on, sufficient heat is generated to ignite the escaping gas, and flame is formed on the surface of the fire. If this flame is observed, it will be seen topped with a dull lurid red, which terminates in a swirling *wreath of dark • smoke. This smoke is almost entirely carbon or soot. It is not free from tar vapour, but the quantity is not large. As time goes on the fire burns clear, the hydro- carbons having been all driven off, the flames become small, and finally die away. 'A practic- ally smokeless combustion is the result, the coal has practically become coke. This is the third stage. The gas then given off is chiefly carbon monoxide unaccompanied by smoke. At this point most heat is radiated into the room. If this could be Maintained economic firing would be the result. But the fire burns down, fresh coal must be put on, and the same waste- ful round ,is repeated.</p>
        <p>So far we have said little definite about smoke reduction, but economical combustion; is but another word .for smoke reduction, the two go</p>
        <p>hand in hand. If smoke is consumed heat is generated, if smoke is sent into the atmosphere heat is lost. This is as true of a kit Chen fire as a factory. furnace, and the householder has no more right to pollute the atmosphere than has the manufacturer.</p>
        <p>In regaord to the fuel itself, the most helpful suggestion we can offer is that the coal be mixed With a proportion Of ,coke. When fuel BO mixed is put on the fire, the coke ignites more rapidly than the coal, and the wasteful period of dis- tillation of the •coal gases without ignition is considerably curtailed. Coke is specially useful in kitchen fires, when brightness is not so much a consideration.</p>
        <p>_ We repeat that an economical grate is of first importance. If householders had the opportu- nity, and would take the trouble to test the coal -cc-, ;umption in its relation to the heat given 0.1 in the best forth of grate, and com- pare it with an inferior one, the difference would astonish many. We have before us a test made in London of two fire grates which shows that between a good and bad design of grate there is a possible difference in fuel cost for effective heat of above 40 per cent.</p>
        <p>• B. Th. U. Percentaqe Source of produced entering Relative Method, heat. for id. room. cost. Open fireplace Coal at bad design 26s.per ton 110,000 20 1.0 Open fireplace Coal at - - • -- --</p>
        <p>good denign 26s.per ton 110,000 35 0.57</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>BEAUTY AND PROGRESS</head>
        <p>BEAUTY AND PROGRESS</p>
        <p>Courts Where the Sun Never Rises in Empire Where it Near Sets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>SLUM CONTRASTS.</head>
        <p>SLUM CONTRASTS.</p>
        <p>Continuing his lectures on "Venice," undo• the auspices of the Museum Committee, Mr. J. E. Phythian spoke on "The Growth of Venice," at the meeting in the Co-operative Hall on Saturday night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>ALDERMAN BENNETT'S CONSUMING PASSION.</head>
        <p>ALDERMAN BENNETT'S CONSUMING PASSION.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT presided, and said that by the large audience he was beginning to think that they were gradually waking up in War- rington, and were really interested in the beau- tiful, artistic, and higher things of life. He had. thought sometimes that in their zeal for mak- ing money they had lost touch with the artistic, but the large and increasing attendances at such lectures as those was encouraging, and spoke well for the efforts of the committee. (Ap- plause.) He used to think—but during the last few years he had learned many lessons—that England was the only civilised country, and that people of outside countries were more or less barbarians, but he had been on the Con- tinent, and, when compared with Belgium, Germany, and other countries, he thought that in our zeal for industrial progress we were losing our love of things beautiful. Even Continental industrial centres were made more attractive and more beautiful than our own. As a patriotic Englishman and a .patriotic Warring- tonian it grieved him to realise that that was so, and if he had' a consuming passion in his public life it was to try to make the people of England realise that there were other things than Wealth which counted more in the welfare of a nation. Their endeavour should be to make Warrington attractive. He did not see why beauty and progress should not go hand in hand together. He did not suppose Venice was ever a manufacturing town in our sense of the word, but having regard to the changed circum- stances-he did not see why England should not mix up beauty with her industries. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>MANUFACTURERS SHOULD LIVE NEAR THEIR OWN 'CHIMNEYS.</p>
        <p>By the aid of lantern slides, Mr. PHYTRIAN showed the causes and effects which resulted in the rise of Venice from an almost bleak and desolate series of sand-banks to the position of mistress of the seas, which she held in the Mediterranean until the end of the sixteenth century. In characteristic fashion he told the story of an electioneering incident in 'London. The canvasser had approached the mistress of the house as to her vote for his candidate, who, he said, represented the Empire on which the sun never sets. "What do I care for that?" said the woman, "I live in a court where the sun never rises." (Laughter a4nd cheers.) He spoke very strongly about the prevalence of our smoke evil, and the awful conditions under which some people dwell. "Could not elec- tricity,' he said, "be produced by using the elements more by making windmills, than by those means which result in our inhaling smoke hour by hour all the days of our lives?" Venice, it is true, being in comparison a Liver- pool or trading centre, compared to Manchester as an industrial centre, had more chance of keeping herself clean and beautiful. In Venice the great men had to live in the city. (Cheers.) The Chairman knew what it meant, for at pre- sent the slums were in the centre of the town, and the rich lived outside in the suburbs in the fresh air. In the old days the slums were out- side the city walls, and the rich dwelt iuside in safety. He considered Parliament ought to make a law, causing the manufacturer to live under the shadow of his own chimney. (Cheers.) "We have no civic pride, he said, "but look upon the cities only as places where we can 1119.1Ke money, and then leave to spend it." Places such as Venice were now show places. For centuries they had the carrying, trade of the world, and now England had it, and whether some revolution, some great change, like the making of the Panama Canal, or the fact that the Chinese and Japanese were doing things for themselves, would gradually wear down our .supremacy, were questions for us to take up in time. We could get abundant warnings from Venice. He was dealing with a romantic city, but it was well at the same time to bear these facts in mind.</p>
        <p>• The beauties of St. Mark's Cathedral were shown on the screen, and the lecturer dealt in an interesting manner with his subject. "The Great Days of Venice" is the subject for to-night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>GARDENER'S DIFFICULTIES.</head>
        <p>A GARDENER'S DIFFICULTIES.</p>
        <p>Never a Bud But It was Spoilt Before</p>
        <p>it Burst.</p>
        <p>For some five years I lived in the very shadow of the 'forge. Every dawn was darkened and every day dimmed by it. - As for the sunsets, the glory of those was lost to me alsd through those long years, but that I do not lay to the charge of Pearson and Knowles. It was the buildings that did that. I was always an en- thusiastic gardener. but all my gardening there was vexation to the soul. Never a bud but was spoilt long before it burst. One tended one's chrysanthemums half the year. and was rewarded with blooms so begrimed that not a solitary one was worth the cutting.' As for the evergreens, long before winter 'every trace of green or of sheens had departed, and the only trace of colour on holly or laurel was the coal- tar iridescence on the sooty leaves. The boughs and twigs were of the same lugubrious aspect, and nothing short of soap and water discovered the beautiful tints beneath. You will easily conceive then that I accumu- lated during those five years a considerable amount of bile. Even now, though it is two years or more since I left, I boil ivhpn I think of it. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <p>THE BEST PROOF of genuine value is an increasing sale. Mothers, daughters and grand- daughters have used Steedman's Powders, which still stand as an unrivalled medicine for children. Observe the EE in Steedman. These powders contain no ,poison.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>MR. J. BLECKLY'S " PINPRICKS "</head>
        <p>MR. J. BLECKLY'S " PINPRICKS "</p>
        <p>The Suggestion to Remove Dalldm and Bewail.</p>
        <p>SOME COMMENTS FROM THE SOUTH</p>
        <p>A few weeks ago Mr. J. J. Bleckly, of the firm of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., wrote to the Press saying that there was a serious proposal at present under considera- tion of the directors of his company for the gradual removal of the Dallam and Bewsey forges from their present site to one immedi- ately adjacent to the new Partington Steel Works, abutting on the Shill Canal. , There were, he said, "many inducements for making such a change," among which he stated: (1) Experts held the Partington site to be the very best situation for works of this character in the kingdom; (2) The mere saving of rates would in a few years cover the whole cost of removal. "It may be some consolation to Alderman Bennett," wrote Mr. Bleckly, "to know that his yearnings for the abatement of what he is pleased to call the smoke nuisance' in War- rington, may possibly be—l go no further—in part at any rata—nearer Of accomplishment than lie supposes, but not on lines which he lays down. . • • , _</p>
        <p>"A movement of this kind would Unques- tionably very much reduce the smuttiness of the town,. \but while the clearing of Warring- ton atmosphere might be compensation enough from Alderman Bennett's standpoint, and might further the objects of the Beautiful Warrington' agitators, I venture to think he and they would find very few townsmen to acquiesce in this view of the case. . . . "I do not wish it to be understood that Alder- man Bennett and his anti-smoke crusade has anything to do with this scheme, but the mul- tiplication of local 'pin pricks' has some effect even in these matters."</p>
        <p>Mr. Meekly then entered upon the merits of the smoke question, balancing the then Mayor's opinion against that of other.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett's reply, to that remarkable letter has already appeared in " The •Examiner." This week we have received a letter, which we produce below, from a gentleman formerly a resident in Warrington, and an employe at Crosfield's works, where he. took a keen and active interest in the question of smoke aboli- tion. His contribution to this discussion will be read with great interest, for the writer knows his subject well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>REPLY TO MR. BLECKIN.</head>
        <p>REPLY TO MR. BLECKIN.</p>
        <p>TO THE EDITOR OF "THE WARRINGTON EXAMINER?'</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will you allow me, as one whom the exigencies of employment condemned for some years to live in Bewsey, to make a few remarks on the amazing manifesto above the name of Mr. Bleckly, which appeared in Warrington? I should like, in the first place, to know what Mr. Bleckly means by speaking of what Alder- man Bennett is pleased to call the "Smoke nuisance." Not even he would, I suppose, main- tain against all common or statute law that no amount of smoke can, in any circumstances, be a nuisance? Does he mean that he does not consider the Bewsey smoke as coming in this category? Surely that would be an extraordin- ary position to take up at this time of day. His own eyes must have daily seen the prodigious havoc made of every form of property, both indoors and out of doors, the dirt, dinginess, and decay everywhere around. I do not ask him to imagine the unending trouble, •ex- pense and discouragement for all those, whether householders or housewives, who still struggle to maintain a decent standard of life in such dismal surroundings, their privation of sunlight and all the joy it brings, the wear and tear of human life, and destruction of health and hap- piness, for I am sure he would regard ,all that order of ideas as mere sentiment, unworthy of a business man. I would put my appeal to Mr. Bleckly on the' lowest grounds and say, "Is it not notorious that the yearly rentals (and, of course, rateable values) of some of the proper- ties in the immediate vicinity of his works have been considerably reduced by their operations, and almost every property within sight of them has felt in some degree this depressing influ- ence? And, if this is so—and if we rightly define the legal term " nuisance "/ as "that which incommodes, or annoys, or causes incon- venience, or damage "—what does Mr. Bleckly find strange in Mr.-Alderman Bennett speaking of the smoke nuisance? And will Mr. Bleckly tell us, speaking as an inhabitant of Altrinc- ham, at what point he would consider the Bew- sey and Dallam smoke to have become a public nuisance? But, of course, all this is elaborate trifling, for, after all, Mr. Bleckly does himself clearly admit twice in his letter not only that there is a smoke nuisance, but that a very considerable/ e part of it is due to their works. I do not for the moment enter into the ques- tions how far at Bewsey and other such places they go, or try to go, in the direction of avoid- ing all the smoke they can, or to what extent they rely on the partial immunity the law gives to certain of their processes; but if they do not abuse, nor intend to abuse, this immunity, and if they have no intention of encroaching on the legal rights of the community, then why these gibes, whence all this animus, against Alderman Bennett and all who, like him, are concerned only to protect within the law, the public health, wealth and happiness? The inference can only be that Mr. Bleckly and his friends are impatient even of the present inadequate restraints of law. They must have nothing less than carte blanche to make all the smoke they please. I see Dr. Joseph is against any efficient control by the central authorities (which every reformer is agreed in regarding as the only hope of the suffering public), while Mr. Bleckly very warmly resents any interfer- ence by private persons.</p>
        <p>'I have often made observations of the Bewsey chimneys from my house or garden, and never realised the moral turpitude of my action till I read that last paragraph of Mr. Bleckly's. Thus, according to these two authorities, there is to be no right of action independent of the Coun- cil, which is to be the final Court of Appeal and sole guarditn of the public, interest. This is an arrangement which would no doubt suit Mr. Bleckly admirably. I suspect the Council still contains a majority of members who regard smoke as the venerable symbol of prosperity, any interference with which would be- a ii4nton tempting of Provi- dence. All that remains, therefore, to secure complete freedom of control is to rid the Coun- cil of these pestilential "faddists." To achieve , this Mr. Bleckly abandons higher grounds and chooses this, the psychological ,moment, for an appeal to the pockets of the poorer people. He no doubt imagines, by raising the spectre of unemployment, to concentrate public odium on all those who are fighting the great ,fight for a wholesome life.</p>
        <p>It is to be hoped that the Warrington electors are shrewd enough to suspect his purpose. If so, they will examine very closely the wording of this truly Delphic utterance. They will note the careful reservations and be duly impressed by the awful sense of responsibility under which Mr. Bleckly evidently labour S in making his momentous declaration. • The arrogance of the letter will amuse them as much as its condescen- sions. They will probably also, like myself, if they have lived in Bewsey, derive a quiet enjoy- ment from that passage about "the hygienic effects of smoke," the stupendous complacency with which Mr. Bleckly lays to his soul the flattering unction administered by Dr. Joseph, and "the most eminent members of the medical 'profession," and the deliciously patronising way in which he puts the junior practitioner in his place ! Poor junior! Still fresh, I suppose, from his medical studies which are as yet un- tarnished by Warrington smoke. But what will, perhaps, most, amuse the average Warringtonian is the presumption underlying . the whole 'Of the letter, that the prosperity of Warrington is so indissolubly</p>
        <p>bound up with that of the Bewsey and Dallam forges that the removal of these latter will in- evitably involve the irretrievable ruin of the entire town, and that therefore the mere sug- gestion of such a catastrophe will be quite suffi- cient to bring the Warrington people to their senses. I hope we are not so unfeeling as to make light of all the suffering that such a dis- location would mean for many poor people, but before the electors yield either to fear or to sympathy and suffer themselves to be, cheated of their elementary right, they should ask them- selves many things, e.g., whether there is much probability that the threatened step will be taken at all; whether, if Pearson and Knowles do decide to move, they will not do So purely for their own interests, and for advantages quite unconnected with the smoke question; whether it is likely that a site like that at Bewsey would be abandoned or would. remain unutilised ; whether Pearson .and Knowles have not, on the other hand, a very direct interest in retaining so valuable an industrial site or in maintaining it in profitable occupation, if not by themselves, then by others; whether, in short, the interests of the firm and those of the employes do not largely converge in this respect. The average ratepayer, too, will note for the same, reason, that whatever reduces the , rateable value of the site and what is upon it, will also be felt very directly in the firm's capital account. On the other hand, he will not fail to note that, after a certain interval at any rate, the removal of such an industry (or the more objectionable parts of it), or its replacement by one less objectionable, must very considerably enhance the value of all that quarter of the town. Then, ,as for the lass to the town of income and business, that would probably be distributed'in any case over a con-,, siderable period, and would,' in all likelihood, be pan i passu made up- from other sources. Warrington is a tovfn of many resources.</p>
        <p>In any case, against any such losses tiler° would be set, so far as the townspeople gen,e,,j; ally are concerned, an enormous yearly savni in the upkeep or renewal of property of sorts—not to speak of health and many othe' things that are not to be counted in money'. • it Who knows? We may yet live to see a fled Bewsey like the old one I have so often heart described, with fair-growing trees and fragl" flowers. It is but for Mr. Bleckly to give word, "and the 'desert shall blossom aS rose." This is something to inspire Mr. nett, for then, too, would a new generation 01' to call him blessed. W. A. R. DARLINGTON. Rectory House, Ealing, W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
