      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>LKEIWILLY Cias &amp; Oil IMPERIAL WORKS, Showrooms : LONDON, NOTTINGHAM, CARDIFF.</head>
        <p>LKEIWILLY Cias &amp; Oil IMPERIAL WORKS, Showrooms : LONDON, NOTTINGHAM, CARDIFF.</p>
        <p>A SMUT MISTAKE.</p>
        <p>_ Lord was the guest of a country squire a few weeks ago, and much merrhnent followed an observation by little Ethel; • the youngest daughter, who seemed completely over-awed by the presence of the distinguished visitor. At table, during dinner, the little girl heard her mother and father now and then say: "My lord this and my lord that," or "Will you have some of this, my lord, or some of that?" .</p>
        <p>Finally when Ethel's mother was interested in the converBatton of another guest, Ethel noticed that milord was gazing at a dish ot relish quite °fit of is reach. Ethel, child though she was, thought she saw a chance to please his lordship, and in aJirm, clear voice exclaimed: "Mamma, God wants 'some pickles." "</p>
        <p>ENGINE Co„ Ltd., KEIGHLEY. Yorks.</p>
        <p>DR. ORMANDY'S OPINION.</p>
        <p>Oil of Vitriol in his Grounds.</p>
        <p>Dr. Ormandy, in the course of his lecture on liquid fuels before the Literary and Philo- sophical Society on Monday night, said: "We havesheard that the removal of smoke would he a terrible thing, but for my part I refuse to believe it. I live in the 'shadow of a wire works and we have a large iron works on the other side. I made some experiments in winter and found on one little acre of ground which I have the pleasure of inhabiting there was produced or deposited—in six winter months '—one ton of pure oil'of -vitriol." Dr. Ormandy concluded by hinting that he intended to Migrate to the South, where he hoped "to die a lingering death in the sunshine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>r. BURNING ITCHING PIMPLES.</head>
