      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY,NOVEMBER 15, 1913. 5 ~., . . 4 – i . . , %... s .„ . „. . .• ,•,,,, ~_,Di.,.. . A:, .. .... . ~ .. _..„.., ,• E.,::::, . _.,•„ , .. . T,. . _ ... _. •.: .. ~ .. ....., ... 1 • . . •, : , . :4 .... s, 4. zz..r. . • . , ,it:. . ::,. • • .. 1 – ,- ~,...., T .. .. ..., .. t. . . ... . .., . . , ; . *„. ~. lit. , . ... -. , . iRRI. , . i N•</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY,NOVEMBER 15, 1913. 5 ~., . . 4 - i . . , %... s .„ . „. . .• ,•,,,, ~_,Di.,.. . A:, .. .... . ~ .. _..„.., ,• E.,::::, . _.,•„ , .. . T,. . _ ... _. •.: .. ~ .. ....., ... 1 • . . •, : , . :4 .... s, 4. zz..r. . • . , ,it:. . ::,. • • .. 1 - ,- ~,...., T .. .. ..., .. t. . . ... . .., . . , ; . *„. ~. lit. , . ... -. , . iRRI. , . i N•</p>
        <p>Bath Heaters, Coffee Roasters, Smoothing Irons and Heaters, Wash Boilers, Clothes Dryers, &amp;c. In the industital section the firm's reputation is world-wide, and they specialise in gas or oil heated furnaces for every trade process. In installing such apparatus, the consumer is not only assured of all absence of waste of fuel, but he is in the unique position of having absolute control of the temperature necessary in his particular process, and can rely on obtaining uniform results.</p>
        <p>Fletcher, Russell and Co. also make many appliances for use in experimental and chemical laboratories and for research work, where the use of gas for many purposes is necessary, as, besides being more convenient, it enables abso- lute precision to be obtained, without which experimental work is of little value. These advantages are over and above that of the complete absence of smoke.</p>
        <p>THE BRITISH COMMERCIAL GAS ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The solution of the smoke nuisance problem is summed up. in two words—gas fuel. Gas can be used for 'every purpose that is now effected by coal fires and is cheaper, cleaner, and more convenient.</p>
        <p>Houses warmed with gas call be kept at a pleasant temperature without any of the work that a coal fire makes in cleaning fireplaces, laying paper, wood and coal, coaxing the fire to burn or replenishing it when it gets low. Gas fires need no sweeping up of ashes and dust, no carrying of heavy coal scuttles. Cooking by gas gives better and surer results than the old-fashioned method, liecause more dishes can be treated at once and the right heat given to each. A supply of hot water is always, available where a gas water-heater is used, and this appliance is independent of the kitchen range.</p>
        <p>Gas lighting is more restful to the eyes than electricity, and, by modern methods, as simply and easily controlled.</p>
        <p>Cominer-cial work also comes within the scope of gas as a heating agent. Gas furnaces—whether regenerative, harden- ing, annealing, muffle, oven, or crucible—are most efficient and economical in working. Gas engines have an established claim to be the most inexpensive form of power both in up, keep, labour and floor space. As long as there are coal fires there, must be smoke. By using gas fuel the smoke nuisanCe ceases to exist. the work is done better and both money and labour are saved.</p>
      </div>
