      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>ATTACK ON SMOKE.</head>
        <p>THE ATTACK ON SMOKE.</p>
        <p>Mechanical Devices for Abatement and Fuel Saving.</p>
        <p>MAKERS' ANNOUNCEMENTS. • A RECORD IN MECHANICAL STOKING. Messrs. James Proctor, Ltd., Hamilton-street Iron Works; Burnley, have issued a pamphlet, "A Record in Mechanical Stoking." , This con- tains, in the front p'ortion, the namessof a large number of users, some of whom have had twenty to thirty years' experience with the firm's stoker, and a list is given of the repeat orders sent during that period. At the end of the book is a list of a large number of users in the textile trades of Lancashire and Yorkshire. The fact that the firm are able to publish these lists as open references is more .valuable testimony' to the efficiency of their stoker than short period tests, which are usually made under special conditions. They are always prepared to guar- antee the highest efficiency from boilers fitted with their stoker and bars, and, at the same time, the wear and tear is so slight that the cost of upkeep is fully 75 per cent. less than on any other type of stoker in the market. SMOKELESS COKING STOKER;</p>
        <p>which shouldpatent, There commend are kwe 11.1' smokeless coking stokers. There is smokeless themselvesanspe , w features hi t t a combuitiOn, no grit or smuts, and the highest efficiency is attained at 50 per cent. less labour. The upkeep is stated to be the very lowest. Other special features include the following: The coking plate is made on an entirely new principle, being inclined instead of being placed in a horizontal position, thus causing the coal to ignite more quickly, which is absolutely es- sential for a coking stoker. .The grate is abso- lutely self-cleaning, all clinker being deposited into a closed chamber at the back of the bars, and is withdrawn underneath the grate by a special shovel at intervals.. Owing to the method of construction, the furnace is consider- ably lighter, yet quite 100 per cent. stronger, than any other furnace made wholly of cast iron. All the wearing parts are provided with renewable hardened cast-steel pieces, thus en- suring a long life and simple and cheap re- newals. The firm of Whittaker, Yorkshire- street Iron Works, Burnley, is highly recom- mended, and has a good reputation. DESTRUCTION OF BLACK SMOKE. Regarding Messrs. Brunton and Co.'s appar- atus, which they claim will stop the smoke, re- duce the coal bill, and give increased working; power, the following extract from a conteinpo- rary, we think, fully bears out their statements: An interesting experiment was made at the premises of Mr. Thomas, Wood-street, Burnley, yesterday morning, Mr. R. Brunton, M.R.S.A., a well-known Burnley man, giving n demonstration of his invention' for the destruc- tion of black smoke. The patent, which is par- tially described below, fulfilled all expectations. The main intentions of , the invention are for the consumptibn of smoke and the saving of heat and fuel. The invention can be' fitted to any style of boiler. By means of steel tubes air is introduced to an air chamber behind the bridge, from which if Is 'discharged by patent nozzles and intermixed with the smoke, and gas from the furnaces thus causing a combustion. After the gas and smoke unconsumed• have passed along the bottom and side flues, they are again caught by an induced draught and forced back to a second hot-air chamber and again forced out at great force behind the furnace, thus causing a thorough destruction or combus- tion of the smoke, and at the same time creat- ing an intense heat. This.. part of the appar- atus needs no attention when once set, as it works automatically.</p>
        <p>"The inventor claims to reduce black smoke to a, minimum in froth 15 to 30 seconds, and yesterday miming Mr: Brunton and-flie others present had the satisfaction of seeing the smoke entirely disposed of in the short space of be-- tween eighteen and thirty seconds. The demon- stration was highly successful to all present." SMOKE CONSUMERS.</p>
        <p>• The Smoke Consumer made by the Thornton Smoke Consumer, Ltd., Huddersfield, is fixed entirely on approval for one month's free trial. This shows that the firm have every faith in the stability Of their production. The consumer has been fixed to the largest works in Lanca- shire and Yorkshire, and is giving every satis- faction—and manufacturers have nothing to lose, but everything to gain, by giving it a trial. It is claimed that the consumer will last as long as the boiler if instructions are adhered to. All fixing could be done at the week-end, so that they would not interfere with work; no boring or anything that would injure the boiler is necessary. The firm send an expert consulting engineer to check the tests and give advice, free of charge. Increased draught is attained without touching the chimney, cheaper fuel may be burned, and great savings in outlay may be made.</p>
        <p>SMOKELESS AUTOMATIC STOKERS. Messrs. James Hodgkinson, Pendleton, Man- chester, guarantee smokeless and gritless com- bustion with the maximum efficiency. Their stoker is automatic in working. The grates being self-cleaning, there is no necessity to open fire doors during Working hours. The stoker is proved absolutely smokeless in working, as may be seen at a large soap works, where a typical installation of 10 Lancashire boilers is fitted throughout, the chimney being only 40 feet high, situated close to a model village. There are over 70 boilers fitted at this firm's works. At a Widnes soap works the whole of the boilers, 26 in number, are fitted. with these stokers, and a St. Helens glass works have 44 boilers fitted, after making careful tests as to smokeless work- ing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>CURE BY GAS. THE RICHMOND GAS STOVE AND METER CO.</head>
        <p>THE CURE BY GAS. THE RICHMOND GAS STOVE AND METER CO.</p>
        <p>In these go-ahead times,- we are all anxious to learn how to save time and money. To the manufacturer in his works this is essential; to the housewife in her home it will add materi- ally to the comfort of the family. We must therefore be up-to-dae, and one cannot neglect to consider the labour-saving devices which are now being largely used in the households of 'England. At the recent National Gas Exhibition in London, no doubt many of those living in War- rington had the opportunity of seeing gas ap- paratus of all descriptions being used in the thousand and one ways in which it is possible. In the old days many objections were raised to using gas stoves, owing oftentime to the smell and unpleasant sensation of dryness caused on the body. Great strides and improve- ments however, have been made by such firms as the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., War- rington, and nowadays no one need hesitate to use gas cookers, grillers, or hotplates, for deal- ing, with the whole of the cooking in any sized kitchen, or gas fires and radiators for conifort- ably and hygienically warming sitting-rooms. bedrooms, passages- and halls, or gas-heated water heaters of all descriptions for an ample supply of hot water to any ized household. In addition to this, there is the highly im- portant factor of the saving in dirt and dust, caused by the use of doal, to say.nothing of time and labour spent in cleaning and renewing, from the ravages of the ordinary domestic smoke, so much of which could be saved by the use of gas apparatus, which, with gas at 2s. I.d. per thousand, is cheaper than coal, when all circumstances are considered.</p>
        <p>FLETCHER, RUSSELL'S SPECIALITIES. The well-known local firm of Fletcher, Russell and Co., Ltd., may be considered to be amongst the pioneers of the Smoke Abatement Move- ment, as they have been manufacturing gas appliances for the last 40 years, and have the. satisfaction of knowing that their specialitie§ have been the means of reducing the output of smoke in almost every large town in the king- dom.</p>
        <p>As showing the numerous purposes for which gaseous fuel is now replacing solid fuel in the domestic section, and thus doing away with smoke, we give a short list of this firm's mann- factures':—Gas- Cookers, Gas Fires, Radiators, Grillers, Boiling Tables, -Large Hotel Appara- tus, Fish Friers, Baking Ovens, Water Boilers,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>SMOKE ABATEMENT HISTORY. what Our Fcreilthers Thought of "Smoak."</head>
        <p>SMOKE ABATEMENT HISTORY. what Our Fcreilthers Thought of "Smoak."</p>
        <p>ROYAL PROHIBITiONS.</p>
        <p>"The great majority of men," said Emerson, "unable to judge of any principle until its light falls on a fact, are not aware of the evil that is around them until. they see it in some gross form." This truth has its limitations. If the evil has surrounded them all their lives, grown imperceptibly with them •in mstfy cases, even the gross forms which the facts take do not arouse. any desire to abolish the evil. The light may fall on the facts, tke facts may take the grossest form, as in the 'case of the smoke, but to the Present generation of town dwellers this grossness having been, the environment to their life, they accept the con- ditions with resignation, if not indiftercnce. We can get accoStOlned to almost anything. It is said that eels get so accustomed to being skinned, they- actually enjoy . it. The inhabit- ants of .Warrington have got so accustomed to the crepe-like veil of smoke, which so. often (when the kindly wind does not blow it away) shuts out the. stun light tiltt many look ivon it as the inevitable, nut and, aad proper condition' of life. Some vagrants'are said to get so fond of their dirt that what they most dread is the workhouse bath.</p>
        <p>It may be interesting in this connection to see what our forefathers (who are supposed to be far behind the present generation in sanitary matters) thought of the smoke nuisance when in its infancy, before people had Come to accept a smoke-burdened atmosphere as natural. The first coal mined- in England would seem to have been dug from the seashore, where the carboniferous Strata were exposed by the wash- ing of the waves and tides; it is believed that. this is the reason, why such fuel was for some centuries known as " sea-cols," to distinguish it from charcoal.</p>
        <p>It is on—lec'O'rd-that in the year 1257 Eleanor, Queen of Henry 111., was constrained to leave Nottingham for Tutbury Castle, in order to escape the smoke of the " sea-coles."</p>
        <p>Tlie " nobles,-pieltites and others" going to London to attend Parliament, greatly annoyed by the increasing smoke, took the lead in get- ting up demonstrations against the use of this fuel. In consequence of the agitation, a Royal Proclamation was issued in 1306 prohibiting artificers from using coal in their furnaces. This step, proving ineffectual, was followed in 1307 by the appointment of a Commission, which - was instructed "to inquire of all such who burnt ` sea-cole ' in the city, or parts adjoin- ing, and to punish them for the first offence with great fines and ransoms, and upon the second offence to demolish their furnaces." Those early advocates of smoke abatement, how- ever, had no doubt in their minds as to the need for a definite programme, and as Ao the import- ance of carrying it out in a drastic fashion. It is recorded that one man who disobeyed the King's command was tried, condemned and executed for burning coal in London. A proclamation was issued in the time of Edward I. and another in Elizabeth's reign, prohibiting the use of stone coal, as it was sometimes called, during the time that Parlia- ment was sitting, for the reason that the health of the knights of the shire might not suffer from this cause, while they were in London at- tending to their Parliamentary duties! The gradual destruction of the forests, which once so largely covered our land, naturally led to increased use of coal, as is shown by a peti- tion presented in 1578 to the Council by the Com- pany of Brewers, offering to use wood only in the breweries near Westminster Palace, as they understood that the Queen " findeth hersealfe greately greyed and anoyed with the taste and smoke of the sea-coles."</p>
        <p>In a pamphlet written by Evelyn, in the reign of Charles 1., it is said "it is this horrid smoke which obscures our churches and makes our palaces look old; which fouls our clothes and corrupts the waters so that the very rain and refreshing dews, which fall in the several seasons, precipitate this impure vapour, which, with its black and tenacious quality, spots and contaminates whatsoever is exposed to it." "It is this which scatters and strews about these black and smutty atoms upon all things where it comes, insinuating itself into our very secret cabinets and most precious repositories. Finally, it is this which diffuses and spreads a yellowness upon our choicest pictures and hang- ings; which is like Avernus to fowl, and kills our bees and flowers abroad, suffering nothing in our gardens to bud, display themselves or ripen; so that our anemones and many other choicest flowers will by no industry be made to blow in London or the precincts of it, unless they be raised on a hot bed, and governed with extraordinary artifice to accelerate their spring- ing."</p>
        <p>-The same John Evelyn, in his " Fumifugium," written in 1670, puts on record a vigorous in- dictment of that "infernal smoak." He writes of London smoke as "That hellish and dismall clowd of sea-coal which is not only perpetually imminent over her head, but so universally mixed with the otherwise wholesome and exceel- nt Aer, that her Inhabitants breath nothing but an impure and thick Mist, accompanied with a fuliginous and filthy vapour, which render them obnoxious corrupt in the lungs, and disordering the entire habits of the Bodies; so that .Catharrs, Phthusicks, Coughs and Con- sumptions rage more in this City than in the whole earth besides."</p>
        <p>It is clear whatever differences may now exist amongst medical men about the hygienic effect of smoke, John Evelyn had no doubt about the matter, and goes for the "Smoke devil" in right gallant style. What our modern apologists for smoke would say if we went for the swarthy demon in anything like the same vigorous lan- guage is difficult to imagine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>OIL VERSUS COAL.</head>
        <p>OIL VERSUS COAL.</p>
        <p>Dr. Ormandy Discourses Learnedly</p>
        <p>on Liquid Fuels.</p>
        <p>~...,tie,-,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>GOOD USE FOR ALCOHOL.</head>
        <p>GOOD USE FOR ALCOHOL.</p>
        <p>"Liquid 'fuels" was the title of an address given by Dr. W. R. Ormandy, formerly of War- rington, to members of the Warrington Literary and Philosophical Society in the Cairo-street Hall on Monday evening. One of the lecturer's principal suggestions was the use of alcohol as a substitute for coal. This substitution would, to a great extent, reduce the evil of black smoke, which has been discussed so much of ,late.</p>
        <p>Mr, 0. Lorrnouss, who 'presided oN-Tei::-large attendance of members, alluded to the change in headquarters. They had, he said, at last found a suitable habitation for the society., and one which, he hoped, would be permanent. Dr. OEMANDY said his interest in the subject would probably never have been kept alive if it had not bean for his connection with a local arm, ,Messrs., Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd. During the periOil he was• connected with the firm one portion Of his duty was to gather in- formation regarding the uses of alcohel for in- dustrial purposes in this and other countries. In the course of that inquiry it soon became evident that if any alteration of the laws relat- ing to the use of industrial alcohol was brought. about in this country, it would be owing to thei pressure exerted on the part of those who de- sired to use that material for the production of light, heat and power. It was obvious that that aspect of the Question was not likely to interest , the Warrington firm in any very direct manner, but with a commendable public spiritedness they allowed him to devote a considerable amount of time, at considerable expense, to the continued investigation of the eubject. He wished to pay a public tribute to them for their broad-mindedness.</p>
        <p>The question of fuel was one of paramount importance to humanity. It was of greater im- portance in this country than in any other, since the bulk of the inhabitants owed their very ex- istence to industries, the persistence and success of which depended on fuel, and particularly cheap fuel. The bountiful provision by Nature of vast coal deposits in this country had had muclt to do with our ,industrial development, -and it was to countries where Nature had not been so bountiful in the provision of solid fuel that we must look for the early risdland incep- tion of the liquid fuel industry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Oil Resources.</head>
        <p>Oil Resources.</p>
        <p>Dealing with the discovery, of liquid fuels, the lecturer said it was in 1859 that the first large amount of oil was obtained in America. which culminated in the great American oil boom. Further exhaustive details having been given with regard to the discovery and improve- ments in the treating of liquid fuels,- the lec- turer said it was an extraordinary thing that England, which had no oil resources of her own, should be the first country to build a war vessel entirely driven by oil. The danger of such a policy had rendered itself evident, and the future tendency would be to use coal-fired ves- sels, with oil as a reserve, which might be used to gain a slightly greater power or which would be available in case the coal supplies were de- pleted. This, hov(ever, pointed to the absolute necessity for this country making arrangements for the provision of oil-fields under her own control. Although the natural conformation of the country prohibited the possibility of such supplies of oil being obtained here, there wag no reason Why the oil resources of the Empire should net be tapped and 'certain proportions ear-marked by the Government as reserve for times of war. The great convenience of oil as a fuel in marine work was owing, not only to tire much greater heat value of oil; compared with coal, per unit of weight, but to the much greater ease with which the oil could be stored and the greater convenience of handling it in the stokehold. The radius of action of a war vessel fired with oil was much greater than that of a coal-fired vessel, and the boiler-room staff was :enormously reduced. Clearly to talk of the displacement of coal by oil would be 'absurd. Although new oilfields were being continuously discovered, the increased supply barely sufficed to meet the diminished, yields of the older fields, and it behoved us, therefore, to direct our at- tention to all possible sources of supply. There was no doubt that in the past the utilisation of our natural fuel resources had been carried out in a criminally wasteful manner, and it was sincerely to be desired that earnest efforts should be set on .foot for the introduction of more economical methods.</p>
        <p>Can Alcohol Be Used?</p>
        <p>The use of low-temperature distilled coke in the household would diminish the smoke nuis- ance. It was possible also that in the none too distant future useful application would be made of the millions of tons of fine coal at present left in the pits, where it formed an ever present danger to those who work among it. Import- ant as was the proper utilisation of those re- sources, stored up in the early history of the earth, we must rather look in the future to some form of energy which could be produced from day to day to meet the world's. demand. Just as coal and oil might be regarded as the production of a stored-up solar energy, so stareii, gugar and cellulose were the daily pro- duction of the same creative power.</p>
        <p>It had been estimated that the total require- ments of petrol in America amounted to 1,500,000,000 gallons per 'annum, and it was interesting to learn that five per cent. of the American grain crop converted into alcohol would be capable of displacing the whole of this as a source of energy. We might look forward to chemical science enabling us, at no distant date, to convert waste cellulose products, such as sawdust, straw and the like, into alcohol, and indeed this conversion had been carried out on an industrial scale, though as yet with- out financial success. Without waiting the arrival of such improved processes, it was pos- sible to produce alcohol in enormous quanti- ties from starch and sugar-containing materials. which could be produced at an exceedingly low price in our colonies and in countries where Nature was more generous in the distribution of sunlight than in our own grey islands. That alcohol suitably denatured—that was, rendered nauseous and undrinkable—could be used suc- cessfully in internal combustion engines there was no doubt, for such engines had been em- ployed successfully in Germany for many years. Undoubtedly a considerable amount of work was required before the proper conditions for the best application of that material were thoroughly understood. It is fortunate that alcoliol and benzine are miscible in all propor- tions because, although the yield of the product of coal distillation was not likely to amount to anything like the quantity which the home market would require, used conjointly with a denatured alcohol produced in our own Colonies, it might serve to solve the problem which is growing even more preloing. There was a time not long ago when the man in the street re- garded the motor car as an evil, and merely as a luxury of the rich, but to-day the production of high speed internal combustion engines was the greatest engineering industry in this coun- try. The extraordinary apathy of those who had built up this huge industry regarding the fuel, which was the very essence of their exist- ence, was remarkable. Several committees had been formed at various times to inquire into the whole subject of motor fuels, but for the most part they had ended in the passing of resolu- tions which had never been acted upon. , Dur- ing the past summer an important conference was held in London, the Imperial Motor Trans- port Conference, attended by representatives from all quarters of the British Empire, and it was interesting to learn that the deliberations of that conference might be summed up in the one sentence—we must study alcohol. He was pleased to be able to say that the holding of that conference had resulted in a permanent council being formed, having representatives from almost all our Colonies, and that this council had decided to form a Fuel Committee to in- quire into the whole subject, both statistically, theoretically and practically. In being asked to become a member of the committee he felt that</p>
        <p>a certain compliment was being paid to the firm who realised the importance of this subject at the time when others regarded it as of no moment, if they granted it any consideration or attention at all. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
