      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>WE WILL GIVE</head>
        <p>WE WILL GIVE</p>
        <p>ONE HUNDRED FO</p>
        <p>i.._</p>
        <p>;</p>
        <p>COMMON SENSE IN EATING.</p>
        <p>Ihese are the days of the "Food Expert," '• e ingenious person who is so great a source .„c —',Perplexity to anyone who takes an inter- -4e4 3E in matters of health. There is a good deal • be said in favour of the suggestion that e"rY "Food Expert" shall be condemned to take a vow of perpetual silence; for then Should we be left to feed ourselves according to our individual fancy, without misgiving, and, let us hope, without indigestion. As it is, the "Food Experts" are permitted to frighten us to the utmost of their bent, until -the more nervous of us are dri'ven to the con- clusion that safety is to be found in nothing 141ort of continual starvation; and, however nervous we may be, we cannot help feeling lhat starvation is an ,unduly high price to Pay for mere safety. 130 NOT BE FUSSY ABOUT FOOD.</p>
        <p>, The frank, if brutal, truth is, that the Peed Expert," is often a misguided nuis- ance, He quarrels with all his brethren of tile craft, whom he regards as fools, and in 11,0 Way helps us, average men and women. lie talks as though his particular food fad were the one important thing in life, for- getting that we must consider other matters, besides food, if we wish to be healthy and happy. , A. very sound Tuie to go on is, .-"Do not be fussy about food. Eat what you find suits •3r011; and be sure that it is sound and of good quality." For consider the varying forms of diet Arhich may be enjoyed by Men with impun- ,itYo and,indeed, with great benefit. The `Pood Expert" talks as though the human bc)dY were no more than a delicately con- ructed chemical laboratory, very easily _thrown out of gear; whereas the human uocty is a living organism which is capable of adant44z., itself, to the most _ varying condi-</p>
        <p>To any Charitable institution that you I don't consume the smoke In any ha provided the draught Is 7501 t.</p>
        <p>If it is only 400! t. we can double it for yoi 25 per cent. more steaming capacity on 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>rIKS,</head>
        <p>rIKS,</p>
        <p>0</p>
        <p>lELD.</p>
        <p>UNDS</p>
        <p>Lay name If we 1;2 d-fired boiler,Ui</p>
        <p>lu and give you the same plant.</p>
        <p>tions. The eative or the Arctic regions gets very well upon a diet consisting almost entirely of fat animal food. The native of India is largely vegetarian, living mainly. on rice and other forms of grain. The African black eats meat in large quantities when he can get it, and at other times keeps well up- on grain and fruit. All these people—not being blessed with "Food Experts "—take what- they can get and are thankful, and, on the -whole, healthy. We, members of a dominant race, consider carefully what we shall eat, 'and—suffer from dyspepsia. We listen to one expert who asserts that flesh food is poisonous, and the eating of it im- moral: to another who pins his faith to un- cooked fruit and nuts: to a third -who preaches that health is to be obtained only by a daily consumption of great quantities of underdone beef and dry toast. One says that death lurks in cheese: another that cheese should be the main feature of the dining table.</p>
        <p>HOW THE BODY IS BUILT UP. Now, it is well to remember that the body is made up of various constituents, of which the principal are carbon, hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen, and that these constituents are being constantly used up and got rid of. It is necessary that they shall be replaced by a fresh supply; in other words, that food shall contain carbon, hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen is suitable proportions. Foods are divided into three classes, proteids, carbo- hydrates and fats, all three containing car- bon, hydrogen and oxygen, but only pro- teida containing nitrogen. It follows that proteids are necessary; and in that they con- tain all the four important elements it is the- oretically possible for the body to keep going on a diet consisting exclusively of proteids. In practice, however, it is found that an en- tirely proteid diet produces certain aisorders of digestion and so on, and that a healthy iiet should consist of a mixture of all three )1 the •above classes, together with salts and water. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>ALCOHOL AND THE GOVERNMENT.</head>
        <p>ALCOHOL AND THE GOVERNMENT.</p>
        <p>In proposing a vote of thanks .to the lecturer, Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said the address had been in- tensely interesting. Mr. W. SILOOCK seconded, and said Dr. Ormandy was master of the subject he had in- troduced. (Hear, hear.) Dr. MANsoN said that the coal and oil sup- plies would in time be used up, and unless we had built up resources on the lines suggested by Dr. Ormandy, the production of energy would be a very serious matter. The use of alcohol for the purpose seemed to be the solu- tion of the matter. There seemed to be some relation between the question of the use of alcohol and the temperance question. It would be much better if the money spent on liquor in the country were turned from the breweries and distilleries and put into engines. Mr, J. G. WITHINSHAW asked if alcohol was being used in the way suggested in any coun- try. Mr. W. H. SHERBERN also joined in the dis- cussion. The CHAIRMAN said the prospect of a shortage of fuel supply was a question for every patriotic Englishman to consider.</p>
        <p>Dr. ORMANDY jocularly remarked that in the presence of a good bottle of Rhine wine he would not like it to go to a motor car. One obstacle to the use of alcohol for industrial pur- poses was the fact that the Government derived</p>
        <p>enormous revenue from its taxation. The great task would be to convince the Government that alcohol was a fit substance to be placed in the hands of the general public, and that it could be used in such a manner as not to deprive the Government of the revenue. Engines were run by alcohol in Germany, but the tax on it waff too great as to warrant its general use.</p>
