      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 15, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 15, 1913.</p>
        <p>THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S DAY SENSATION.</p>
        <p>• Public opinion has for weeks declared that Councillor Peacock would make a fearless and energetic Mayor, and everybody will admit that he soon got to work on Monday to prove the truth of the prophecies of his many friends. He was elected by the unanimous vote of the Council as it stood at the opening of the pro- ceedings—that is, there were five Aldermen waiting about their own fate, to be revealed by the voting papers later on; and there were several Councillors away, but near to, evidently arranging for a surprise division that would sweep away some of the retiring Aldermen. Everything had been said that could be said by Alderman Tinnion and Alderman Burton, the proposer and seconder of Councillor Peacock, and several Conservatives added their meed of praise for the man who had had an extensive business career, and. had performed unforget- table rervices in the Friendly Society World. Alderman Tinnion mentioned that Councillor Peacock had made "some considerable sacrifice to enable him to okupy the position" in which the Council proposed to place him. That was somewhat vague, but Councillor Peacock, re- turning thanks for his election in a speech which, for matter and, style of delivery, was equal to anything that has come from the chair for two decades, let drop a bomb which caused a report that has resounded , through the whole country for the remainder of the week. His speech must be read to be properly under- stood; but, .briefly, the point that created the sensation was thtit upon his accepting the Mayoralty he received notice to quit the service of Messrs. Joseph C.rosfield and Sons, for whom he had acted as traffic manager for fourteen years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>WAS IT ADVISABLE?</head>
        <p>WAS IT ADVISABLE?</p>
        <p>People have asked whether such a step as that taken by the new Mayor was advisable. We think it was, so that the air might be cleared. There had been rumours in circulation for weeks, and it was essential that a Mayor should not begin his work with a cloud about his head. He explained his position, and Coun- cillor Henry Roberts, a director of Crosfield's, in one of the letters in which he has been re- plying to the Mayor's statements, says he is glad "for the opportunity afforded him of re- plying to certain rumours, and insinuations." There has been 'a clearing of the air by the exertions of the parties on both sides, and we auggest now that the dignity of the town de- Mrals that both sides should lay down their arms and combine for the peace and well- being of the town. At the end of his speech-- after he had explained how it was that he would be able to be a whole-time Mayor—his Worship extended to each and every member, words uttered slowly and with emphasis, "of this Council my right hand of fellowship, hop- ing that friendship may 'continue from this day and that we may be blessed.in our work for the good old town of Warrington." That state- ment, made while the sensational explanation was still hot, called forth loud and sustained applause from an astonished and highly sym- pathetic. audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>SUBSEQUENT SQUABBLE.</head>
        <p>THE SUBSEQUENT SQUABBLE.</p>
        <p>The statements, both by means of letters and interviews, will speak for themselves, but an explanation is due to the public from the Press in one particular. At the Council, Councillor Peacock said he had been "approached and pressed by Councillor Roberts, who was a can- didate for the position of Mayor, and, through him, by Mr. John Crosfield, the chairman, who told him that, holding the position of Mayor of the borougri was incompatible with his (Mr. Peacock) holding the position Of traffic manager at Crostield's." Mr. Roberts has characteried this statement as untrue, .and Mr. Peacock has withdrawn the word " approached," but stands by the remainder. It is only fair to state that the written notes from which Coun- cillor Peacock spoke contained only the word " pressed " ; these notes were handed to the Press, but the spoken words were followed by the reporters, and not the notes. whether this part of the controversy is a quibble with words, as the Mayor states, or not, 'the man in the street," that important personage and final arbiter in such matters, says that the fact re- mains that Mr. Peacock is Mayor and out of his job; and that if he had not become Mayor he would still be following his ordinary employ- ment as a highly competent traffic manager. The same highly sensitive man in the street thinks it a thousand pities that one of the town's most honoured firms did not stand by the chosen head of the town for one short year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>MR. ARTHUR CROSFIELD'S POSITION.</head>
        <p>MR. ARTHUR CROSFIELD'S POSITION.</p>
        <p>Mr. *Arthur Crosfield, the prospective Liberal candidate, has, to a small extent, been in- volved in the rumours that have been " laid " by the new Mayor and Councillor Hy. Roberts this week. Some have thought him to have a common, if not an active responsibility, for what has happened. This is an entirely erro- neous idea. Mr. Arthur Crosfield has, for some time, ceased to he connected with the firm of J. Crosfield and Sons.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Crosfield has sent a letter to the Mayor, in which he says : "it is a matter of great satisfaction to me to know that in conferring upon you this signal mark of honour and esteem, your fellow towns- men and mine, have but recognised character, capacity and first-rate business ability, and have therefore paid honour where it is due,. "It is, on the other hand, a matter of the deepest regret to me that the firm with which I had the, honour to be actively connected for very many years, should have incurred the censure of All right-thinking "men by acting in a manner so wholly at variance with—so utterly, opposed to—the character of its traditions, established through many generations."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>OLIVE BRANCH: A GOOD START.</head>
        <p>THE OLIVE BRANCH: A GOOD START.</p>
        <p>In the attempt to oust the retiring Aldermen, or some of them, the members who had the matter in hand came desperately near success in the case of Alderman Bolton, who tied, for the fifth and last place with Councillor Wilkin- son, with ten votes. Any other Mayor would have been in a trying position, but Councillor Peacock. was 'prompt and decisive. Alderman Bolton is, or has been, accounted head; of the Conservatives in the Council. Councillor .Wilkinson succeeds councillor Peacock as Chairman of the Liberal Association, the Mayor having retired from politics for his year of 'office. The Mayor had said he would know no politics, and his first act was by his casting vote against his political friend .and successor, to 'confirm Alderman Bolton in his old position, an act which stood in. strange contrast to the action of the intriguers, largely members of his own party, who had nearly cast him out. Well might Alderman Bolton, who had been savdd from his friends, say the Mayor's act was one he woUld always remember.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>PLOT THAT FAILED.</head>
        <p>THE PLOT THAT FAILED.</p>
        <p>It coulcl.--e;ily ,be seen at the Council on Monday that there was something unusual in the wind, for there were whispered 'conversa- tions taking place between a certain set of Councillors, and the usual calm tranquility of the Aldermen, whose fate hung in the balance, for once seemed to have deserted them. The object of the conspirators was not as is gener- ally the case in other towns, the mere gaining of a party advantage. At the critical juncture —the time for casting the votes—there was a rift in the lute, and4it is alleged that some of those who had sworn allegiance to the scheme had not the courage of their convictions, and either failed to record their votes, or exercised (like a woman) the privilege of changing their mind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>SMOKE.</head>
        <p>SMOKE.</p>
        <p>"The Examiner" to-day publisnes, on Pages 4 and 5, several special articles on smoke, and many advertisements of manufacturers, who undertake to supply apparatus for stnoke abate- ment. We draw 'special attention to the article on household smoke, and invite our readers to let us know their opinion on the subject.</p>
        <p>A BEAUTIFUL GRAPPENHALL GARDEN. "T.A.C.," the well-known Cheshire Natural- ist, writing to the "Manchester Guardian," a fortnight ago, said: A Gatley correspondent tells me that a castor oil plant in his garden has this season flowered and fruited for the &amp;lit time; the spike of bloom is about 14 inches long and the berry clusters are not unlike those of the ivy. He wonders if this is usual, out of doors, in so northerly a county. We suggest that Mr. Coward's correspondent has. made the common mistake of confounding the castor oil plant with the Aralia Sieboldii. The castor oil plant is of a succulent nature, and will not stand even our mild winter in this ,neighbourhood. The Aralia is to be found in the nursery men's catalogues described as a half-hardy plant. It is not generally known that the Aralia will stand the climate here, but we are informed that Mr. John Fairhurst, the borough treasurer, has an excellent specimen growing in his garden at Grappenhall. The plant has been out in the open for the last 27 years, and has been moved on two occasions to different gardens. At the moment it is 9ft. 6in. through and Oft. 6in, high, exclusive of the flower spikes, which are as long as 20 inches. Many. gardeners have seen the plant and have declared that there is not another such speci- men within fifty miles. Mr. Fairhurst, as everyone knows, does not keep the glories of his, fine garden to himself. He is sending some leaves and flower spikes to the flower table at , the Museum, so that those desirous of studying the Aralia may do so. Mr. Fairhurst asks us to make it known that he would welcome callers who would be inter- ested not only in this out-of-the-way plant, but in a garden which, we know, is at its best, laccording to the season, all the year round. He has, for example, a Hydrangea Peniculala, which last year bore 82 blooms at one time. He also grows Magnolias in the open, which flower well. In the early spring there may be found here the snowy Mespilus, flowering cherries, guelder rose, the laburnum and the almond; and all Warrington knows the love Air. -Fairhurst has for roses, and how suc- cessfully he grows them. If he has any greater pleasure in life than growing, it is showing others the results of his labours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>SCEPTICAL GARDENER</head>
        <p>THE SCEPTICAL GARDENER</p>
        <p>Two years ago Mr. Fairhurst visited Hathp- ton Court, and, seeing a gardener planting out an Aralia, which had been under glass all winter, told him that he .grew the plant outside altogether in the .North. The gardener gave the Visitor a look which plainly indicated scepticism of the pronounced order. • On. reach- ing home, Mr. Fairhurst sent him a photograph of his plant in situ, but no acknowledgment of conversion has ever yet reached Grappenhall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WARRINGTONIANS "DOWN SOUTH."</head>
        <p>WARRINGTONIANS "DOWN SOUTH."</p>
        <p>Many of his friends will be pleased to hear that Mr. Walter Gleave, son of Mr. William Gleave, of Paul-street, Warring- ton, was elected a member of the Portsmouth Town Gouncil at the recent municipal elections. Mr. Gleave,, who stood in the Liberal interest, contested the St. Mary's Ward, and had the substantial majority of 320. Mr. Gleave, who served his apprenticeship with Messrs. J. and W. Dutton, drapers, Sankey-street, is in busi- ness in Portsmouth, where he has resided for about seven years. His elder ,brother—Mr. W. A. Gleave—is engaged in journalistic Work in the neighbouring seaport of Southampton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>BABBLING.</head>
        <p>BABBLING.</p>
        <p>A correspondent sends us a quotation from a report of a recent speech by Mr. Harold Smith, M.P. The Member, among other things, said: They read that Falstaff, when sick, babbled of green fields, and when the Liberal party were sick they put. up the Chancellor to babble of turnip fields. We don't know what this means, but, coming from the source it does, we suppose it is all right, especially as the sentence finishes with (Laughter.).</p>
        <p>LOST—AND NOT WANTED. It is strange that so many things are lost and are allowed to remain lost through sheer idle- ness, indifference, ,or ignorance. It is to be feared that we have so little faith in the hon- esty of our fellows that when we lose anything, especially money, we invbluntarily come to the conclusion that the finder will stick to his find. There are, however, some honest folk in the world, and it would not take up much time, or cause much trouble, to make at least an effort to recover the missing treasure. In to-day's " Examiner " the Borough Treasurer advertises the fact that so long ago as the middle of August a purse containing a fair sum of money was found in the Latchford car. No inquiry has been made through the usual channels—the carmen, the tram office, or the police office— and the money, remaining unclaimed, the find is publicly advertised. It is not long since a large parcel containing a considerable quantity of dress materiallvas found. It lay for months at the Police Station among other " finds," but was never claimed. This is hardly fair. •The honest man who finds anything that does not belong to him feels hurt,when the loser refuses to allow him to do the proper thing. We hope the men—and women—who are careless or un- fortunate enough to lose their belongings will be sufficiently thoughtful as to do their duty by the finders, and take steps to relieve them of their undesired property. It is so easy to inquire, and the chances are that an inquiry will lead to success.</p>
        <p>REFLECTIONS. In passing along life's journey, We could find many ports for a call, If we only remembered, when learning, The message the Cross has for all. Would so many hearts have been broken, And lives sunk in bitterest shame, That a look or a word—left unspoken— Could have saved in the great Master's name?</p>
        <p>Would the cry then be heard in their anguish, Of "My soul—save my soul—take my gains," /When the final on earth comes to banish, And disease holds them coiled in her chains? Alas! in the greed of the nation, To heap up their treasures of gold, All unmindful of others' privation, Till the grave claims them victims as toll.</p>
        <p>Where now are our men with the courage To love intellectual sweet chimes? They prefer to pay doctors demurrage, For a motor and "good, jolly times." Is this education's progression, Our spiritual training outshone, All churches and prayers a profession, And the Godlike within us undone ? E. A. B.</p>
        <p>Three Bohes for Teachers. E. M. Rapp, school superintendent of Berks County, Pa., says some pointed things in his latest monthly letter to the teachers: " There's a difference between being prepared to teach and only thinking so. Sourness is all right in vinegar, but it is a very poor quality in a schoolroom. "It is better to live 10 years than to exist 50. "Wishbone, jawbone, backbone, these three; but the greatest of all is backbone."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>BY-ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE BY-ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The by-elections have not altered the situa- tion very \much, though before Keighley the ' Unionists had another attack of election fever.</p>
        <p>11 they had' really persuaded themselves tha</p>
        <p>the results of the Reading and Linlithgowshire by-elections were the symtoms of a revolt in the constituencies against Home Rule, Keighley speedily has undeceived them. sir S. 0. BUCKMASTER was re-elected by an increased majority. In a three-cornered contest the</p>
        <p>majority of the Liberal over the Tory vote was</p>
        <p>Increased by 53 since the last contest in 19</p>
        <p>and the combined Liberal and Labour vote, which is, of course, solidly Home Rule, in- creased its majority over the Tory vote ioS7 247. In fact, the voting in the Keighley Division gave a plurality of 4,524 for Home Rule. That the Liberal majority at Linlithgow should have been lowered, and Reading lost by a compara- tively small turnover, though regrettable, is not surprising, since these seats had to be contested by candidates unknown to the electors against</p>
        <p>opponents who had long been, nursing their constituencies. After the result was known, the</p>
        <p>Tory Press "endeavoured to claim all the* votes</p>
        <p>which the Socialist candidate at Beading cap- tured from the Liberal party. But before the</p>
        <p>election "The Standard" had proclaimed tha</p>
        <p>"every vote given for-the Socialist would be a</p>
        <p>vote for Home Rule." On this showing, all tha</p>
        <p>happened at Beading was the conversion of a Home Rule majority of 99 into a Unionist major- ity of 68. It is not a very tremendous affair, but we forgive our opponents for making the most of it, There is nothing like making hay while the sun shines. They will not have much</p>
        <p>cause to rejoice when the plural voter, the Unionist strength at by-elections, has been eliminated from electoral calculations.</p>
        <p>UNIONISTS AND FREE. TRADE. .kt a Free Trade meeting in Liverpool on November 4th, at \which Sir John Simon waS the principal speaker, a letter was read from Sir William B. Forward, who said:</p>
        <p>I think the time has arrived when we Unionists who hold Free Trade opinions hhould express those opinion q with more ,boldness and candour than hitherto, for I am persuaded that our party, will not be returned to power so long as they hang on to Tariff Reform. Placing party politics on one side, and considering what is best for the country, can there be a doubt that our great prosperity is due to Free Trade, and that under Free Trade alone can it be main- tained?</p>
        <p>The old hope is "hardest to be lost," but Unionists will inevitably coOe to disclaiming the most deadly policy they'ever espoused.</p>
        <p>FREE.—A choice Coloured Picture Almanac with "Liverpool Weekly Post" of Saturday, November 22—now enlarged and the brightest and• best Saturday paper. Hall Caate's Greatest Story; Cash Prizes for the Zag 'Zig Game, and many other good things. Big demattd certain. Order your Almanac copy now.—(Advt.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>MAY QUEEN DEAD.</head>
        <p>MAY QUEEN DEAD.</p>
        <p>A Promising Young Life is Cut Short.</p>
        <p>IMPRESSIVE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The May Queen of Warrington is dead. Miss Maud Kirkland, who only a few months ago was crowned the Queen of the May inlthe pres- ence of a packed assembly in the Parr Hall, passed peacefully away in a nursing home at Nthv Brighton, whence she had gone to recuper- ate her health some ten weeks ago. She died young, for she had scarce completed her twen- tieth year, but in her short career she accom- plished much useful work in connection with the Band of Hope, the Wesley Guild, and the -choir at Stockton Heath Wesleyan Church, and in other directions. The news of her demise came as a severe blow to her friends, both young and old, and her genial nature made her always surrounded by a bevy of earnest friends.</p>
        <p>A Most Popular Queen. At the May Queen ceremony Miss Maud Kirk- land, as the central figure in an exquisitely pretty function, bore herself with grace and</p>
        <p>titotograph by Burrell.</p>
        <p>dignity. Her" address' from the throne was a charming piece of composition, and she went through tile evening's proceedings with almost life-burn regal grace: At the after concert Miss Kirkland was the recipient of numerous pres- ents, including a gold erooch from Mrs. James Dodds. On behalf of the Hill Cliffe and Stock- ton Heath Band of }Tope, she was presented with Tennyson's poems, which; by a 'tragic co- incidence, contains the •pathetic, poetical story of the death of a young May Queen. The Brit- ish Women's Temperance Association, through Mrs. Tanner, gifted her a charming bouquet. Congratulations showered upon the young Queen. _</p>
        <p>The news of her death became known early on Sunday; and at the. Stockton Heath Wes- leyan .Church touching references were made to the sad event by Mr. Cox at the morning ser- vice, and Mr. Peake in the evening. The pulpit was also draped in black. The most heartfelt sympathy was expressed on every hand with her parents and relatives, who reside in Pike- street, Stockton Heath, to which house the body was removed during the .week-end.</p>
        <p>An Impressive Funeral. The interment took place in St. Thomas's Churchyard on Wednesday afternoon, and it was one of the most impressive and one of the largest ever seen in Stockton Heath. All along the route blinds were drawn Out of respect to the dead Queen, and the Wesleyan Chiirch..was -filled with sympathising friends, amongst whom was Mrs. James Dodds, president of the War- rington Band of Hope Union. A touching service was conducted by ,the Rev. W. A. Beckett, superintendent of the Warring- ton Wesleyan Circuit, and the Rev. George Curtis, of Hill Cliffe Baptist Church. Mr. Tom Tanner, organist to the Band of Hope, played the organ. In the course of a short address, the Rev. W. A. Beckett said: Maud Kirkland's sun will shine in heaven, and that is the great part of our consolation here this afternoon. She made her influence felt for good. As a Sunday school scholar she yielded herself to Christ in this place, and she united herself to the membership of this church. From very early days she was associated with the Band of Hope, and for several years she has taken a very active part in the work of the Band of Hope, a work, which we are quite sure, she very greatly loved, and a work that was so greatly prized by you, that it was acknowledged in May Of this year, when the Band of Hope showed its appreciation of her -work by crown- ing her the May Queen., She has not lived long to bear the honour here, but she has entered into a more glorious honour with Christ.</p>
        <p>Endeared Herself to AU. As assistant secretary to the Band of Hope in connection with the local churches, she rendered splendid service, and she will be greatly missed in that work. She was also the secretary of the Young Abstainers' League, an institution con- nected particularly with our own Wesleyan Church, and in the service she did in connection with that she did it with all her heart. She also filled a fitting place on the Social Com- mittee of the Wesleyan Guild in connection with this church; and also in the Guild Sun- shine Band, for she herself was always bright and sunny.</p>
        <p>For about nine or ten weeks she was a happy- inmate of the nursing home in New Brighton, and from thence on Saturday last she passed into the presence of Christ. It is a- great joy to us to know that there she became a great favourite; she endeared herself not only to those who were fbllow sufferers, but she endeared her- Self to the doctor and Ole nurses, and all of them. speak Most highly of her. In my own visitations to her, on two or three occasions, I found her most cheerful and very hopeful of returning home again and spending Christmas in the home circle with her loved ones. In the land of lasting pleasures. and with Christ she will keep her Christmas. - The home is the poorer, the church is the poorer, the world is the poorer, because Christ has taken her to be with Him. We could not want her hack. She is in, much better keeping than ours, and she will be able to serve her Master there even' better than she could serve Him here.</p>
        <p>' . ...L...., ...,..06:1K,,, . • yks.- .-."•-•*::.:!:'::: , ... ,1c.,.,"`.2.4.'::.,....?:,..,,,-:., ''' ' •,:•::..i.fat:.;,-. : , .'.."4•,,..: ~,... iffY4'..-"'" ..,%0' ,',O ....N.:.,,..,•:.;....,,?:,,, -,....,../- : - -' . ;.....' .-.i0i.,.4i#:;,-'' -.....",-E.l .12i.:::..;:,:,-.•.,, , , ~ . ~ ._. •v...?'..., .....,.:gfike:.....?:::',::::.',. . vf..--,:-.,:z.::,,,tA,,:.,,...• .....,ni.i.::i0m..:i:,,,,,,.:..,::e. %' &gt;555 \/,\,......, ... ..,... i......., - ~ , ~.. , ~...,,, ~ , .. :P. .. .., ~.., .-........</p>
        <p>THE LATE MISS MA CD KIRKLAND</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Funeral Procession.</head>
        <p>The Funeral Procession.</p>
        <p>The procession from the church to the place of interment was headed by the late Queen's Maids of Honour at the May festival: Misses Doris Thompson, Edith 4ollings, May Ellis, Jessie Davies, Edith Warhurst, Eliza Williams, Dollie Cain, and Elsie Whitfield. Each \of the maids carried white flowers. Then came the Fairy Queen, Miss Eva Tanner, and the Crown. bearer, Master Reggie Grounds, who carried a floral crown bearing the inscription "From the Warrington Band of . Hope Union, in loving memory of their May Queen, who has exchanged her earthly crown for one that fadeth not. 'away!'</p>
        <p>Following were former May Queens, namely,' Mrs. T. W. Durant (nee Jessie Ellison), and Misses Minnie Whitmore, May Isherwood, Amy Spencer and Amy Littlewood. The mourners were: Mr. and Mrs. F. Kirk- land, father and mother; Miss E. Kirkland, sister; Mr. Fred Kirkland, brother; Miss Evelyn Todd, Mr. and Mrs—G. Whitford, Mr. and Mrs. F Grounds, Mrs. S. Appleton• and Mrs. A. Grounds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Other Deputations.</head>
        <p>Other Deputations.</p>
        <p>The following deputations attended: Warrington Band of Hope Union: Mrs. James Dodds, president, and Mrs. F. Gandy, on behalf of the hon. secretary. Executive and Festival Committee: Mr. T. Dale, Mr. G. Whitfield, Mr. C. S. Fellows, Mr. J. Hamblett, Mr. J. Whitmore, Mr. W. Watkins, Mr. T. Tanner, Mrs Whitmore, Mrs. G. Shaw, Mrs. Hamblett, Mrs. Fellows, Mrs. Spruce, Mrs., Durant and Miss Isherwood.</p>
        <p>Hill Cliffs and Stockton Heath United Band of Hope, of which deceased was assistant secre- tary : Rev., G. Curtis, president; Mr. G. Whit- (Continued in veal column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS, "ONLY A MILL GIRL."</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS, "ONLY A MILL GIRL."</p>
        <p>Royal Court Attractlou.</p>
        <p>This popular drama, dealing with life in anti around the weaving shed, has attracted large audiences to the Royal Court Theatre during the week. There is plenty of sensation ta stimulate interest, and a sprinkling of whole- Tha some ,humour to relieve the tragic. scenery is most realistic, a weaving-shed will!, running looms being one of the princiPar achievements. Miss Vera Grafton playsl,ll6 part of Mary Raymond in a clever manner. 'lle role of Constance Daryll is in the able hallu,s of Miss Edith Giddings, whilst Miss Eunice Dorris is a capital Martha Ellen Simpson. Mr; Henry Scatchard's delineation of the part "al Raymond, the mill overlooker, is indeed Praise; worthy. Mr. Leonard Tremaine scores a sucras::, as Jack Ainsleigh. A typical moneylender presented by Air. 11. S. Carlton and Mr. Jonty-llewliurst also plays his part in a caP"" manner.</p>
        <p>"BABY MINE" ARRIVES ON NIONDAY.I "Baby Mine" will be at the Royal' Gau Theatre, next week, presented by Mr. Herber Jay's West End company. This screaminglY farcical comedy in three acts creates one lorg laugh from the rise to the fall of the curtelliti Mr. Jay travels the entire scenery, Sze., 811c1 there are some beautiful dresses by the be° \Vest End firms, and some hats from Paris.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>SPLENDID HIPPODROME PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>SPLENDID HIPPODROME PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>For sheer ilaring and intricacy of exechti°ll the feats performed by Merza Golem's trotT°o of acrobats and tumblers excel any we .11ac„if witnessed- for a long time. Under the title "The Slave Dealers" this spectacular Asiaul_ novelty is a success in all directions, the !°rg geous, coloured .tapestries and carpets of value -being admired by .all. 'the vall,st u., tricks are carried out .with a rapidity and sics tainty which is little short of marvelle.,%: "Backing the Gee-gees" is an intensely ahl,„('' ing playlet, presented by Renaud LockW' Miss Clarice Warner and Miss Gertie MOe Richard Finberg gives many Character in a pleasing manner. , His delineation of Rat," from "The Breed of the Treshams, exceedingly .good. Kisbey, the call-boy eerti., dian. has many new gags and witty stort which convulse the audience, whilst 1,1111,,,1e Nelson is as popular as ever with his "Oh! crinoline" song. Lily Hill, the Lance° fa Nightingale' uses a sweet voice to advanteglelit, pleasing songs. Dorma Morgan- is a caPill Mg comedienne, with a cha:rming style.</p>
        <p>ct Comedy holds sway at the Hippodrome week Topping Topping the bill is the sketch 'TI;4S of a Kind," presented by George Abel anticci boznpany. Bert. Terrell should supply •8, g turn, as also should the American KyasYBs; jar eluding Miss Kyasya, whose hair is insul'ev,„o, £l,OOO. • Amongst the other items are Ida 70, in a dancing scena; Nava , and Winfr; in a 'comedy sketch,." A Leap Year Propos,.'fa, the Raymond Trio, in a dancing act Dpt ji- comedienne, and the Warrington Trio. eatiaiD. brists, in a combination of grace. and strell''</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>THEATRE ROYAL.</head>
        <p>THEATRE ROYAL.</p>
        <p>A story of the days of the struggles beillithis the Royalists and Roundheads is given c'ire- week in "The Striking of the Hour." ',;(15 match finds a capable rendering in the rift-,r, of-the popular Mr. IL Val Vousden. and Mr" 15 Healey is equally successful as Sir Fr 1111( Whittan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>PARK PICTURE PALACE.</head>
        <p>PARK PICTURE PALACE.</p>
        <p>greatest cinematographi trent§ e - The Park Picture Palace promises One: 'in Vt' the 'Warrington next week. " Macho traction, and then will be presented Picturna. of the full drama by Mr. Arthur Bourcbier Miss Violet Vanbrugh. The scenes and dress:id' will be true to the period of the should prove one of the great" drawilie mediums ever seen in this theatre. ilet;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>NEW PICTURE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>THE NEW PICTURE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>A drama full of intense human interest ".11't be exhibited here for the firs 1 three days of nel•ci week, under the, title of "The Great DianWieg Mystery"; whilst for the latter part of the We a very comical film will provide the chief a;ttrif.ipe Lion, defiling with "Wild BeaSts at Large:,' . other pictures also appear to be very good</p>
        <p>STRIKE AT RYLANDS' WOO</p>
      </div>
