      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>Late Mrs. Hollingvvorth.</head>
        <p>The Late Mrs. Hollingvvorth.</p>
        <p>A memorial service to the late Mrs. lolling- worth, mother of the Rev. J. Hollingworth, was conducted in St. Barnabas' Church on Sunday by the Rev. J. W. Glendenning, St. Helens, and a. former curate of St. Barnabas'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>Freak of the Season.</head>
        <p>A Freak of the Season.</p>
        <p>The fine season has deceived the plant life. A pear tree in the garden of Mr. Baldwin, Pad- gate-lane, under the impression that spring has arrived, is once more in bloom. Several labur- num trees have also been in flower in different parts of the district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Music Unearthly and Spiritual.</head>
        <p>Music Unearthly and Spiritual.</p>
        <p>"Music is the most unearthly and spiritual and the purest of all the arts," said Canon Morley Stevenson, preaching on behalf of the choir fund at the Parish Church on Sunday. Canon Willis preached in the evening. The choir rendered special music, Mr. J. B. Poole sang a pleasant solo, and Mr. F. M. Darby gave an organ recital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Gifts of Clothing.</head>
        <p>Gifts of Clothing.</p>
        <p>The Bold-street Wesleyan Church has received a gift of a parcel of clothing from the South Lancashire Division of the Needlework Guild. Mr. Laws, the Police Court Missioner, has also received articles of clothing from the South Lancashire Needlework Guild and a parcel from the Cheshire Guild, per Mrs. Lyon, of Appleton</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Saved the House Wreckers.</head>
        <p>Saved the House Wreckers.</p>
        <p>The house wreckers engaged pulling down the old property in Buttermarket-street found an unexpected ally. Through, it is supposed, the vibration caused by the heavy motor traffic, an outside wall of an old shop suddenly collapsed. Two men employed on the work had a narrow escape. One labourer, William Brown, received a slight cut on the hand.</p>
        <p>A Surprise for the Youngster. An amusing scene was witnessed in Sankey- street. A venturesome boy stole a ride on the rear of a motor van. He was alarmed, and, in seeking to dismount, his jersey caught on a hook upon the van, and the youngster hung in the air for a moment.. Then he fell off and did " Gaby glide" through the mud. He was a muddier boy when he got up.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>King's Liverpool Regiment.</head>
        <p>The King's Liverpool Regiment.</p>
        <p>Arrangements are now complete for the traas- fer of the depot of the King's Liverpool Regi- ment from Warrington to Seaforth Barracks, Liverpool. An advance party has arrived from Warrington, and the rest of the troops will be transferred early in the New Year.</p>
        <p>Women Who Do Not Want Votes. There was an open meeting of the Wycliffe Church Y.P.S. on Thursday night, when a debate on "Should women have the vote?" was held. About 75 were present. The .Rev. T. T James presided. Miss W. Corney took the affir• mative, and explained the main motives of the Suffragist movements. Miss E. Brown, who opposed, maintained that women's work was in spheres other than political. A division showed 33 for the affirmative and 37 against.</p>
        <p>50,000 Dutch Bulbs. Mr. R. Lowe will sell by auction on Wednes- day next at 11 o'clock 50,000 Dutch bulbs.</p>
        <p>Presentation to a Curate.</p>
        <p>At St. Paul's Bible Class last evening the Rev. E. E. Peters, who is leaving, was presented with an illuminated address and a purse of gold.</p>
        <p>Temperance Sunday. Temperance Sunday was celebrated in War- rington and district last week-end, and in nearly all the Nonconformist Churches special reference was made to the subject.</p>
        <p>The Mayor "Knighted." The Mayor, Bro. P. Peacock, was enrolled a Knight of the Golden Horn at Alexandra En. campinent on Thursday evening. - The ceremony was performed by Knight J. Wolfenden.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>Mayor's First Engagement._</head>
        <p>The Mayor's First Engagement._</p>
        <p>The Mayor and Mayoress, Councillor and Mrs. P. Peacock, fulfilled their first public engage- ment on Monday evening, when they attended the ball of the Sacred Heart Church in the Parr Hall. There were 300 present,' and amongst the company to welcome the civic guests was T..ady Acton. The Rev. Father Rockliffe said he did not think they could have found a better man to fill the office of Mayor than Councillor Pea- cock. The Mayor, in reply, said the Catholic denomination had always been very friendly with him. Many of theta had been his friends since childhood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Retiring Mayor Praised.</head>
        <p>Retiring Mayor Praised.</p>
        <p>Tributes were paid to the work of the retiring Mayor, Dr. Joseph, at the Warrington Police Court on Monday. Mr. S. Wright congratulated Dr. Joseph upon the success which attended his three years in office. If he had erred at all it was in respect to the leniency •displayed to un- fortunate people brought before the Bench. Alderman Smethurst said to those people Dr. Joseph had shown a kind and tender heart. Mr. W. Roberts said it was satisfactory to know that Dr. Joseph would remain with them as a brother magistrate. Mr. G. F. White, the Magistrates' Clerk, associated himself with thcs remarks. Dr. Joseph briefly expressed his thanks.</p>
        <p>Successes at Wood's Tutorial College. The following students have been declared successful as the result of examinations con- ducted under the auspices of the National Short- hand Association in shorthand and typewriting Typewriting Certificates have been awarded te Misses N. Duffey, H. Weir, E. Rogers, G. Ains- worth, A. Gilbert, D. Swinton, E. Percival, E Hayes, E. Podmore, Mr. A. Taker, and Miss M Wilde. Shorthand Certificates: Mr. A. Taker 60 words per minute; Mr. W. Such, 60 words pei minute; Miss Doris Bond, 60 words per minute: Miss Rogers, 60 words per minute; Miss A. Bar- low, 60 words per minute; Miss M. Berry, 66 words per minute; Miss B. Grounds, 60 worth per minute; Miss B. Weir, 60 words per minute; Miss Gertrude Ainsworth, 90 words per minute; Miss M. Wilde, 80 words per minute; and the undermentioned have been awarded certificate of proficiency for theoretical knowledge of short- hand : Miss D. 21.loorfield (distinction), Mr. F. W Bradshaw, Misses E. Ball, I. Cunnington. Tooner, J. Kilduff.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>National Health Insurance: Change of</head>
        <p>National Health Insurance: Change of</p>
        <p>Doctor.</p>
        <p>The attention of insured persons residing in the borough is directed to the advertisement, which appears in another column, giving parti- culars as to the course to be adopted in case an insured person wishes to select a Panel Doctor, other than the one on whose list he or he has been during the present year. Persons desiing to change will require to obtain the necessary form (No. 35) at the Head Post Office, Spring- field-street, or at the Customs anet Excise Office, Winmarleigh-street, or by forwarding a stamped addressed envelope to the Clerk of the Warring- ton Insurance Committee, Trinity Chambers, Market Gate. Having filled in the form, the insured person will take or send it to the doctor from whom he wishes to receive treatment dur- tug 1914. The latest date on which applications ean be entertained is the 11th December. T ',floured persons not making application by that nate will remain on the list of the doctor from whom he has been entitled to receive treatment during the present year.</p>
        <p>Mayor Attends a Concert.</p>
        <p>The new Mayor and Mayoress have so far had a busy week, and amongst the engagements car- ried through was the attendance at a concert organised by the Bowling Club connected with the 'Bank Quay Working Men's Mission, and held in Thewl is-street School on Thursday even- ing. Councillor Osborne presided and was sup- ported by the Mayor, wearing .his chain of Office, the Mayoress, Miss Wilkinson and Mrs. Osborne. The Mayor and the Chairman briefly addressed the meeting. His Worship expressed s his pleasure at being able so early in his term of office to visit his old friends at Bank Quay. He urged upon the young people to have high ideals, to cultivate a dauntless spirit and a determina- tion, which, joined with perseverance, would en- able them to attain to positions which would be Otherwise impossible of attainment.—Solos were excellently rendered by Miss Wilkinson, Miss L. Jones and Miss Scarisbrick. Mr. Lee's Juvenile Choir did well in the choruses, and other inter- esting items. An original and clever recitation , on "The King's Visit" was greatly appreci- ated. One of the triumphs of the evening was the humorous sketch "The Old. Stagers' Club," hY the Men's Class. • It was original and was the talented work of a member of the Mission. It Was a quaint and clever production, the per- formers playing the parts of " old staffs' to Perfection.</p>
        <p>alrrettrs RANGES, FIRE GR.ATE3, TlLES.—Large *tack, reasonable nrices.—l. S. GARLAND. Iron- *longer, 51 to 55, Itridge-etreet, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <p>. , - - .</p>
        <p>An M.P.'s Visit.</p>
        <p>The Junior Liberals hold their great demon- stration in the Co-operative Hall on Monday at 7.30. Speakers, Mr. W. A. Barton, M.P. for Old- ham, and Mr. J. Davis, Liberal candidate for Crewe. Councillor H. Roberts will be chairman,</p>
        <p>The Ten Bishops. The great Missionary Exhibition takes place in the Empire Rink next week, and each day it will be opened‘by a Bishop. Thus ten Bishops are due to visit Warrington in as many days. The exhibition will be so interesting that large crowds, are likely to attend daily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Their Jewel in Heaven.</head>
        <p>Their Jewel in Heaven.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Insurance Committee on Thursday the part they would play in ,the Mayoral procession was mentioned. "Shall we have our jewels?" asked a member. The Deputy Mayor: "We shall have our jewels in the next state."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Bold-street Wesleyan Band of Hope.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Band of Hope.</p>
        <p>Mr. Reginald Parry ddressed the meeting on Wednesday and gave a graphic description of a drinking saloon. Songs were rendered by John Mitton. Miss Elsie Hatch and Miss Minnie Stockton played two pianoforte solos with ability. Recitations were given by Gladys Bon- nel, Dorothy Ball and Elsie Hannan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>Platelayers Threat to Strike.</head>
        <p>Platelayers Threat to Strike.</p>
        <p>A meeting of platelayers of the Warrington district was held last evening, when it Wits un- animously decided to support the Liverpool men in their struggle and to demand the reinstate- ment of the four men suspended as a prelimin- ary to a settlement of the dispute on the Cheshire Lines.</p>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Society. An interesting lecture on a trip up the Rhine was delivered by Mr. W. R. Jackson on Tues- day. He left the beaten track and found many pleasure spots, which he showed to his audience by means of his excellent photographs. For a maiden effort the lecture was brilliantly suc- cessful.</p>
        <p>A Lucky Offender. Councillor Peacock took his seat as Mayor at the Police Court yesterday. Ile was welcomed by Dr. Joseph, the deputy mayor, Mr. White magistrates' clerk, and Mr. Arthur Browne, on behalf of the Bar. A woman charged in eon. nection with a slot-meter theft case was dis- charged, it being the first case before hi:. Worship.</p>
        <p>. . Dolls for Poor Children. The Mayoress has made arrangements for an exhibition of dolls, to be distributed.' amongst Warrington poor children, to be held in the Town Hall on Friday next from 3 to 4 p.m. About 220 dolls are being dressed by Warring- ton ladies, and they are requested to return them to the Town Hall not later than Tuesday next.</p>
        <p>Aiding the finfirmary.</p>
        <p>The Matron of the Infirmary E-L cknowledges, with grateful thanks, the receipt of 20 articles of clothing from the South Lancashire Needle- work Guild, and 45 articles of clothing from the West Cheshire Needlework Guild.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, . SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 15, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>Medical Referees.</head>
        <p>Medical Referees.</p>
        <p>The question of medical referees was raised. at the meeting of the Warrington Insurance</p>
        <p>Committee on Thursday evening</p>
        <p>Mr. Fostei</p>
        <p>said most societies were appointing their own referees. Mr. Bibby thought it was not a ques- tion for that committee. Mr. Delaney stated that people would be frightened to death by such referees. Mr. Hall thought such an appointment would be premature. It was decided, however, that the appointment of a referee was necessary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>Law Students' Debate.</head>
        <p>Law Students' Debate.</p>
        <p>•At a meeting of the Law Students' Society at the Warrington Academy on Wednesday, Mr. J. Longland, solicitor, in the chair, Mr. F. 0 Stansfield moved the following resolution : "That this house strongly disapproves of the Government of Ireland Bill, 1912, and of the policy of the present Government with regard thereto." This was opposed by Mr. N. Browne. and the debate was continued by Messrs. Dut- ton, Farrow, Guest, Unsworth and Bairstow The motion was eventually carried by a major- ity of three.</p>
        <p>New Chairmen of Committees: Alder-</p>
        <p>man Pierpoint's Qualification. Dr. Joseph has been appointed chairman of the Electricity and Tramways Committee, in succession to Alderman Smethurst, resigned. Alderman William Pierpoint, who lost his qualification some time ago, and was succeeded as chairman of the Gas Committee by Alder- man John Evans, has now regained his quali- fication, has been re-appointed chairman in accordance with that undcrstanding, and Alder- man Evans becomes deputy-chairman. Coun- cillor Chantler has been appointed chairman of the Markets Committee, in place of Alder- man James Evans, resigned.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Mr. R. B. Roberts. At the Education Office on Saturday Mr. RI B. Roberts, the Assistant Director of Education, who is leaving Warrington to take up the posi- tion of secretary to the Woking Education Com- mittee, was presented with an entree dish and a pair of candlesticks by his colleagues. Mr. J. Moore Murray, Director of Education, spat. in high terms of the work accomplished by Mr Roberts during his ten years in Warrington, and conveyed to him, on behalf of the staff, besi wishes for the future.—Mr. A. M. Ker, borough surveyor, Mr. A. T. Holloway, deputy town clerk, Mr. A. S. Elliott, who is succeeding Mr Roberts its Assistant Director, and Mr. W Farrar, on behalf of the School Attendance De- partment, offered further testimony as to Mr Roberts's worth both as a worker and as a friend.—Mr. Roberts, in a very neat reminiscent speech, thanked the staff sincerely:</p>
        <p>An Airship's Adventures. During one of its recent voyages, the Bovril airship, which has lately been seen in varioua parts of the country, met with an unpleasant adventure, of which no explanation is at present forthcoming. On the fifth day cf the cruise, which started from Henclon not long ago, thc. airship, while passing over St. Ives, in Hunting- donshire, encountered a strong headwind and the pilots decided to effect a landing. But it was found impossible for a time to bring the machine to earth. The pilots are absolutely at a loss to account for the behaviour of theft craft, for the entire machine was found to be in perfect order. They set it down to some extraordinary atmospheric phenomena which cannot be explained. The airship is the pro- perty of the proprietors of the famous Bovril beef preparation, and is the first airship in history to be utilised for commercial purposes.</p>
        <p>Mayor and the Children. the Mayor presided yesterday afternoon at the annual meeting of the local branch of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children. His Worship said such a society deserved the best wishes of every good citizen The society belonged to the same party as him. self. It had no religious or political feelings. (Applause.) It had to be absolutely impartial just as he had to be as Mayor. It pleased him to learn that the society had such a number of willing helpers and collectors to carry forward that grand movement, for without it the chil- dren would suffer very much. It was not a society that aimed solely at prosecution, but it, through its vigilant inspectors, endeavoured to better the condition of the children.—The re- port, submitted by Mr. Jeans, the secretary showed that 249 complaints, affecting the wel- fare of 691 children, had been dealt with. There was a balance of £2O, compared with £4B last year. The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Tramway Profits.</head>
        <p>Tramway Profits.</p>
        <p>The tramway profits !or the half-year ended September 30, 1913, amounted to £1,439 net, against £1,288 in the corresponding half of last year. The reserve fund stands at £lO,OOO, and the appropriation fund at £3,2.58.</p>
        <p>Cemetery Hotel Bowling Club. Mr. A. H. Crosfield presented the prizes for this year's Cemetery Hotel closing bowling main in the clubroom last night. That, he said, was the 20th time he had performed that pleasant duty. The prize-winners were: 1, R. Young; 2, T. Ti. Fielding; 3, A. Forster; 4, C. Jordan; 5, 6,7, 8, equal prizes, won by A. Pennington, W. Drury, G. Ball, jun., and F. Dale. Mr. Antro- bus won the prize for the best average in the playing competitions. An extended report of the proceedings, in- cluding Mr. Crosfield's speech, will appear in next Saturday's "Examiner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>Alderman Smethurst Retires from</head>
        <p>Alderman Smethurst Retires from</p>
        <p>----- Electricity and Tramways Committee. Alderman Smethurst this week retired from the chairmanship of the Electricity and Tram- ways Committee, a position he has held since 1903. He has brought both departments up to a high state of efficiency and made them paying concerns. This admirable work for the town, along with the arduous labours of Alderman Smethurst, which have largely contributed to- wards providing the town with a splendid new Warrington bridge, will form the subject of a special. article in "The Examiner" next week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>"Tea Tango."</head>
        <p>"Tea Tango."</p>
        <p>A Tango tea was held in the Pari Hall on Thursday afternoon, under the direction of Miss Ethel Spinks, who is well known in Warring- ton in the dancing line, and her lessons include this latest dance, the Tango. The exhibition was by Mr. and Mrs. Griffin, Wavertree, and was refined and graceful in every way. .The programme was varied, and Mr. H. Brew6.ton gave some pianoforte solos, which he rendered with skill and finish. Miss Coxen sang beauti- fully; her sweet contralto voice was fully appre- ciated by the audience. The children, Misses o..Balmford, Freda Davies, W. Knox. G. Pen- dlabury, B. Taylor and Master R. Partridge, were extremely pretty, and they danced like little fairies.</p>
        <p>Preachers for To-morrow. Wycliffe Congregational—Morning, Rev. T. T. James; evening, Rev. A. N. Johnson. Bold-street Wesleyan=-Morning, Rev. J. Gun- nell; evening, Rev. 11. Williamson. Cairo-street Chapel--Morning and evening, Dr. Mellor.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Independent Methodist—Morn- ing and evening, Mr. T. Worthington, Wigan. Elmwood-avenue Congregational Morning, Rev. A. N. Johnson. Latchford Baptist Church—Morning and evening, Mr. Fielding, Manchester Baptist Col- lege.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild Rally, Bewsey-road Church— Morning and evening, Rev. Theodore Bishop. Buckley-street Methodist, Anniversary—Morn. ing and evening, Mr. B. Richardson, Liverpool St. John's Presbyterian Church—Morning and evening, Rev. W. Reid.</p>
        <p>Golcor'ne-st-re-ei Baptist—Morning and even- ing, Rev. H. Ogden. Society of Friends—Evening, Dr. E. Viponi Brown, Manchester.</p>
        <p>Christadelphians, Toft's Assembly Rooms— Evening, Mr. A. Morris, Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>MAYOR'S SUNDAY.</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>The Largest Procession Eve': Seen in Warrington.</p>
        <p>A REMARKABLE GATHERING.</p>
        <p>To-morrow is Mayor's Sunday, and Warring- ton is to be presented with a processional spec- tacle never before witnessed in the ancient borough.- The number in the procession is ex- pected to exceed all previous records, and with the regalias of the Friendly Societies, the neces- sary dash of colour will be -added to-the dis- play. The procession starts from the Town Hall soon after ten o'clock, and will proceed to the Parish Church, where the Mayor's Chaplain, Canon Willis, will preach.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>Order.</head>
        <p>The Order.</p>
        <p>The order of the procession will be : Regimental and Bugle Bands, members of the 4th Battalion (Prince of Wales's Volunteers) South Lancashire Regiment, Army Service Corps, National Reserve, Church Lads' Brigade, Latchford Cadet Corps, Boy Scouts, Legion of Frontiersmen, St. John Ambulance Brigade, Fire Brigade, Police Force, Sword Bearer, Mace Bearer, The Mayor's Chaplain, The Mayor, The Town Clerk, The Deputy-Mayor, The Member for the Borough, Freemen, Ex-Mayors, Ex-Member for the Borough, Members of the Council (in accordance with</p>
        <p>order of precedence), Officials, Magistrates, Education Committee, Education Committee Officials, Non-Members of the Council, Board of Guardians, Board of Man- agement of the Infirmary, Insurance Committee, Townsmen.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES. The Friendly Societies will be in the following order :</p>
        <p>Ancient Order of Foresters, Buffalo Band, Buffaloes, Independent Oddfellows., Free Gardeners, ,Druids„ Shepherds, United Oddfel- lows, Rechabites, Oddfellows (Manchester Unity), Hearts of Oak, Rational Friendly Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>PEOPLE WHO KNOW.</head>
        <p>PEOPLE WHO KNOW.</p>
        <p>Peps Used Regularly by Distinguished Members of Society, Church, Law &amp; Stage.</p>
        <p>THE INVALUABLE FAMILY MEDICINE.</p>
        <p>When ladies and gentlemen of discrimination, regularly take Peps for their coughs, colds, and bronchial ailments, it is a sure sign that Peps possess curative properties that make them the incomparable throat and chest remedy. LORD ROSSMORE writes from The Stud House, The Home Park, Hampton Court, Middlesex :—" For colds and coughs, and eveyy- thing of that sort, there is, to my mina, nothing in the world like Peps. They have done my old winter cough a great deal of good. I wish I had known of Peps' long ago." THE HON. ALEXA,NDRINA PECKOVER, Bank House, Wisbech, England, writes: " I am liable to take cold as the Winter comes on, and two or three times during the past Autumn I felt that I might be about to have an attack. Each time, however, I took one or two Peps, and the cold soon came to an end."</p>
        <p>THE REV. ARTHUR E. FLEMING, Pre- centor of Gloucester Cathedral, writes from Worcester Lawn, Gloucester: "I find Peps most soothing to the throat, and very helpful in clearing the voice and sustaining the tone during singing or intoning." THE REV. JAMES GRINNELL, Alexandra- road; Sible-Hedingham; Essex, rite 4 : "Both I and my parishioners have found Peps parti- cularly beneficial for bronchitis and asthma. Peps do my throat a lot of good, and are parti- cularly soothing and strengthening. Members of my congregation suffering from colds, bronchitis, and asthma, have found Peps the best remedy for loosening and raising phlegm and giving relief generally." MADAME CLARA BUTT and Mr. Kenneriey Rumford, the famous singers, write: "We have formed the very highest opinion of Peps, after using them regularly for some considerable, time. They are quite unique in their invigorat- ing effect on the throat and chest, and in their freedom from any irritating or relaxing effect on the vocal chords."</p>
        <p>--M-r.-E..MARSHALL HALL, K.C., M.P., writes: "I have found Peps most useful, and my chauf- feur tells me he has derived great benefit from them on foggy nights." MAJOR H. HENDERSON (late 10th Regi- ment), writing from Villa Nova, Elhain, Canter- bury, says: " Having served in India some years. I found the bitter north-east winds in the old ' country ' affected my throat, and brought on a very persistent cough. I tried Peps, and found them very excellent and pro- tective. Peps banished my cough and did real good to my throat."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>Councillor Peter Peacock the Unanimous Choice of the Council.</head>
        <p>Councillor Peter Peacock the Unanimous Choice of the Council.</p>
        <p>RECEIVED " NOTICE " ON ACCEPTING THE HONOUR.</p>
        <p>The New Mayor's Dignified Explanation and Offer of Friendship.</p>
        <p>Councillor Peter Peacock was installed Mayor ' of Warrington with the usual formality on I Monday—" Mayor's Day." I</p>
        <p>Alderman Tinnion, in moving his installation, paid a tribute to the Mayor's high rank in the Friendly Society world.</p>
        <p>Alderman Burton voiced the appreciation of the Council at the appointment, and others added their testimony to Councillor Peacock's capabilities for the office.</p>
        <p>The Mayor, in his reply, created a sensation by disclosing the fact that when he consented to stand as Mayor his services had been dispensed with by Messrs. J. Crostield and Sons.</p>
        <p>The retiring Aldermen were re-elected. Several other names appeared in the running. Coun- cillors Pemberton, Chrimes and Archer got one vote each. Messrs. Bolton and J. Wilkin- son tied for the last place with ten votes, but the Mayor gave his casting vote for Alderman Bolton, who was re-elected.</p>
        <p>In discussing the Standing Orders, Councillor Pemberton attacked the Aldermen who held so many chairmanships of committees, and several of the Aldermen indignantly replied to the insinuations they alleged had been thrown out by Councillor Pemberton and some other members of the Council.</p>
        <p>•:-.:-:•</p>
        <p>,+•+•:•.:••:-:-:-:-.:•-)+.:•-:••)•:...:•,..</p>
        <p>Alderman Tinnion concluded with an appeal to every member of the Council and every rate- payer to extend to Councillor Peacock their neartiest help and co-operation. He felt sure that would be a very popular election, and would be supported by most people in the town. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>ALDERMAN BURTON SECONDS.</head>
        <p>ALDERMAN BURTON SECONDS.</p>
        <p>Alderman BURTON found it a pleasure to sec- ond the resolution, and he also paid a tribute to the work performed by the retiring Mayor and Mayoress. As a member of the Council for seventeen years he had every confidence that Councillor Peacock would worthily uphold the dignity of the office and discharge his duties to the satisfaction of the Council and the public. ..Applause.) lie earnestly hoped Councillor Peacock would have the unanimous support of the Council, so that there would be nothing to mar his pleasuro in the office. (Applroise.)</p>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE SUPPORT. Councillor Pemberton's Confidence.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON, in supporting, thought too much had been made about the matter that Councillor Peacock had not much municipal experience. In his opinion, Councillor Peacock had had a fairly good experience. They had to consider his vast experience in friendly society work and his tremendous capacity as a business man. He had no doubt but that Councillor Peacock would be able to fill the chair with as</p>
        <p>'.:•••:-:-:-...:..x.•:•+•••++••:•+•:-:-:•••:.</p>
        <p>Reform Act of 1832 and granted a Municipal Charter in 184.7.</p>
        <p>I do not need to apologise at not being known to nearly everyone in Warrington. I have been here since one year of age—l could not have come much earlier. (Laughter.) I have been in business the whole of my life in this grand old town, of which I am so proud, and I have had a unique experience in fighting four elections in three different wards in this ancient borough. I was returned successfully at the head of the poll in the three wards. I feel quite confident that if I make a few remarks about my position y'bu will not con- sider them misplaced. During the past fourteen years—it is my duty to explain—l held the office as a manager for that most honour- able and well-respected firm, Messrs. Crosfield and Sons, and during these fourteen years I have strenuously served them faithfully and well—(hear, hear)—so much so that from time to time I have received flattering testimonials from different members of the firm for my ser- vices. I feel that as lam now out of an active position I shall be able to serve this good old town with more freedom, and I shall be able to give the whole of my time to forwarding the interests of this town. "INCOMPATIBLE" POSITIONS.</p>
        <p>When you first mentioned my name as a candidate for this honourable position, I was approached and pressed by Councillor Roberts, who was also a candidate, and through him by Mr. John Crosfield, the' chairman of the firm, who told me that the position of Mayor of this ancient borough was incompatible with my position at Crosfield's. (" Shame.") Well, gentlemen, I considered it very seriously, and, personally, from my own knowledge of what the duties were as to whether it would in the slight- e3t degree affect my position and duties at Cros- field's, and I ennui definitely to. the. conclusion that it would not. (Hear, hear.) I made various suggestions to satisfy the firm, but they were not entertained. You will therefore judge me surprise when I received a letter, signed by Mr. J. Cromfield, from Mr. Roberts's depart- ment, as follows :</p>
        <p>Oct. Ist., 1913.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—ln pursuance of the terms of the agreement, dated April 5, 1900, and made be- tween the Company and yourself, we hereby dye you notice of our intention to determine the agreement at the expiration of one calendar month from this day. We are, dear sir, yours faithfully. (Sinned) JOSEPH CROSFIELD &amp; SONS, LTD. (J. J. Crosfield, Director). (" Shame.")</p>
        <p>Well, gentlemen, my notice has now expired, and whilst regretting, as I most sincerely do.</p>
        <p>,•:-:•• 4.,:.++.:.+.:..:.-:..:.•.:..).:..:.•.:.++•:.</p>
        <p>THE AFTERMATH.</p>
        <p>A Medley of Statements, Interviews and Explanations.</p>
        <p>MESSRS. CROSFIELD'S POSITION.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., handed us the following statement on Tuesday: We have read with surprise and regret Colin.. cillor Peacock's reference, published in to-day's papers, to the determination.of his agreement with his company. . . Though his statement is grossly misleading, we have no intention of entering into any con- troversy on the subject. We are confident that our reputation for fair dealing with our managers and employes is well known.</p>
        <p>It will, we think, be readily recognised that the duties our traffic manager owes to our com- pany do not permit of his time being occupied in the many onerous and honourable duties which must be undertaken by the Mayor. JOSEPH CROSFIELD &amp; SONS, LTD. 11th November, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <p>Photographs by Birtles.</p>
        <p>HIS WORSHIP THE MAYOR AND MRS. PEACOCK.</p>
        <p>**:***.•.'+'..'••:.•:.•-:- +• ••+••+</p>
        <p>With the customary imposing ritual, Coun- cillor Peter Peacock was installed as Mayor of Warrington on Monday. The ceremony is always an attractive one, and there was a large attendance of interested spectators. The retiring Mayor, Dr. Joseph, presided at the opening of the Council, and after announc- ing the results of the elections, invited Alderman Tinnion to nominate the now Mayor.</p>
        <p>much/ credit to the town and himself as any Mayor who ever sat in the chair. (Hear, hear.) lie was sure Councillor Peacock would so con- duct the deliberations of the Council that they would have that friendly feeling, which all members of the Council should have, for the gentleman who sat in the Mayoral chair, and he was also certain the new Mayor would give ample scope for discussion, such as they had experienced during the past three years to an extent he (Councillor Pemberton) had never before experienced in that Council. Be would have the support and confidence of everyone. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>that the gentlemen named should ha4e come to t4e conclusion that a manager of theirs, elected by the Council to the exalted position of Mayor, in their own town, would shed no lustre on their firm, and whilst they apparently measure in pounds, shillings and pence, the loss of such portion of my well-earned salary as would be represented by the time I would have to spend in this chair serving the good old town, I asked myself the question where my labours would be best employed, and in accepting your invita- tion to become Mayor, I am called upon to make a sacrifice which is not usually demanded from the occupant of this Mayoral chair.</p>
        <p>Gentlemen, I now turn from this unpleasant and sordid episode and express the gratitude I feel to you all. I have been congratulated from all quarters and from all parts of the globe. I have just received a letter from an old War- ringtonian now in Portugal. I do not under- estimate the importance of presiding over this great and honourable borough, with assets amounting to two millions sterling. I feel, as chairman, that there are one or two things I would like to see completed as speedily as possible. alear, hear.)</p>
        <p>‘.‘ `" • • ••• ' • ... • • • • . „ . • • "i .„ • . • • 3... Tt/ ' At, •47 • ti • • 4 • • , r " I I • , • t 4 . . - , tt,</p>
        <p>.., 4:,.;.......:4t4: s. ~.....,„..fl;t,:..,,trski.i.t,:t:tl:A'. ~,, ~,- , . , '..., ...5,.‘.....:.:,::::.,.7'.: , 555; 44 ~.,.:,:.„,..„.,„.... ........;,,,;,,.. ,z...i,,....„..0...z.:i,i,....1.0:4;i4.. ~..i,.,6 .-...:......,:t....,.....„.,:,..,.....„,.., ;4444S SS ..,..,.,A, -..,., '1:3;:‘..5,,,...-:::‘...::::-;:i' '..• -.,:.;7::- ..,,,,,,i..::.,;.:::..")::',1.i..'ii:;,;iii-,....,-,A ~...."...:,..:i....,.;;.,,......,...,.....,.. . . ..,..,;.:.,,.x55:,...:7,1.44::?:!,,.::....,:,„,, 5 ~.......‘&lt;:,......,..,..f,,,,-1.5:,,..t:;,.444A..17,,,,,R ~':.,.,.'.5,4,,.; - e w A.,,',.. ..:,.,...-.4);;i:V;i1-1:,•i.tviti.',.::-..;i4,,..ik:f:::'.i.; ...:;,:: ::,,.... '—-,,. ,;,.;,•,,,,,,i';s' , ...h'i.','''..::i;:Ait....: ~‘ . A 7,,,„,.„. ~,,-,:,.-4•.,:,-.1 . ~.,...z; - 1 ' e,,,-,..,0,..,...k'-4:,*.i's 5,,, • 2 ~,..,A.,....,,, .... . ~. . ..... „. ..:. .. ~..,,,,,i1) ..,,•..,,pi,:i.1,.. ......... 1, , . , 1 s: ..,,....,..., ....,,,..:...:....*.t.f...4.:.,.: . , , i-r.:-,- i -4,7., ',4-: '',•.,.',''' ~..4.,'..,‘,,' . ::..::' ' -.1v,i..•.'.,.:2c''.:&lt;- .„ ' S 4 ..:.) i ...:J'Lliek ~,, .7, ?...,,:3:, 4:-..,...,,,i.„ s ~,,,,...v•ts.'„'q','„ . ~.,...1„, ~...:.,1k1,.. * .A: A.--- ' ~ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>ALDERMAN TINNION PROPOSES</head>
        <p>ALDERMAN TINNION PROPOSES</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PEACOCK.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION said it gave him great pleasure to propose Councillor Peacock for the position of Mayor. Before doing 30, he thought it only fitting and proper to congratulate Dr. Joseph on the excellent manner in which he had for the past three years performed the duties of Mayor. He associated the Mayoress with the remarks. Her charming manner had endeared her to everybody in Warrington, and she had been of great assistance to the Mayor in carrying out the duties of the office. (Applause.) ' Alderman Tinnion proposed:</p>
        <p>"That Mr. Peter Peacock be and he is herebyi elected Mayor for the ensuing year, and until the acceptance of office by his successor."</p>
        <p>A MAN OF GFI!T" AND CAPACITY. Well, said the Alderman, there has been some criticism as to the choice the Council have made. Personally, I am a party to the choice, and, so far as I am concerned, I am perfectly satisfied. True, you might say Mr. Peacock has not had as great a share in the municipal part of the work as have many members of this Council, but what lie has done he has done exceedingly well, and,he has done it with only one object in view, and that is the best interests of the town of Warrington. (Applause.) If he has not had a large municipal experience, he has had an excellent business training, and, after all, that is a good qualification in anyone endeavouring to fulfil the duties of Mayor. He ought to, and should he, a business man. Councillor Peacock had already made some considerable sacrifice to enable him to occupy that position, and if some criterion of what they might expect was wanted, they had ample proof that they had got a man with some grit, and a man who would do his duty fearlessly and in such a way that at the end of twelve months the Council would, be proud they had elected him. NOTED FRIENDLY SOCIETY WORKER.</p>
        <p>He would like to say a word about Councillor Peacock's Friendly Society work. As a brother Forester, he (the Alderman) could speak with very considerable pleasure and authority on the excellent position Councillor Peacock held as a member of the Friendly Societies. He under- stood Councillor Peacock was also a member of the Buffaloes, Shepherds, and Hearts of Oak. He had held almost every office in the Foresters Society, from the bottom rung of the ladder to the very top, and Councillor Peacock was Past Chief Ranger. He was at the present time the treasurer of the Warrington district, and he had had the unique distinction of attending the High Court for seven years in succession. No other man in the district could claim such an honour. Councillor Peacock was also a member of the Board Of Management of the Warrington and District Burial Society, and all that amply proved that he had given much time to forward- ing objects. which many of .them had at heart. He (Alderman Tinnion) thought the Friendly Society movement was one of the greatest and most beneficial movements the workers of the country had ever known. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>MAYORESS.,</head>
        <p>THE MAYORESS.,</p>
        <p>Of the Mayoress they must not expect too much, as she was of a quiet and retiring dis- position, but her heart was right, and he (the Alderman) had every confidence in saying that she would make an excellent Mayoress, and give satisfaction to everyone with whom she came into eontact. (Hear, hear.) With reference to Councillor Peacock's work, gentlemen, few men, added the Alderman, have done more to strengthen the ties of friendship and brotherhood, which unite the members of the friendly societies, and which have been the cause of a common inspiration, and the highest example to the whole world. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>Councillor Broadhurst Retracts.</head>
        <p>Councillor Broadhurst Retracts.</p>
        <p>Councillor BROADHURST, also supporting, said one speaker had referred to the criticism that had been passed. As one of the greatest critics when Councillor Peacock's name was mentioned, he must now say the time for criticism was past. They had passed that stage to the position which meant work. Every member must do his utmost to make the period of his office a brilliant success--(hear, hear)—and' they must remember they were not there for the purpose of putting obstacles in the way of any gentleman who occupied the Mayoral chair. (Applause.) As one who had been a critic of Councillor Peacock in the past, he hoped every member and every person in Warrington who could assist, would help Councillor Peacock in the fulfilling of the duties of the high office which had been conforred upon him, and that they would do their utmost to make his term of office a complete success. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Councillor Chrimes' Tribute. \</p>
        <p>Councillor CREMES said, as a friendly society worker, he could vouch for all that Alderman Tinnion had said about Councillor Peacock. They in the Foresters had always found him straightforward and willing to do all he .pos- sibly could do. He was sure that the experi- ence Councillor Peacock had gained from friendly society work would enable him to carry out the duties of Mayor in a very creditable manner, and in a manner which would meet with the approval of the Council and the town in general. (Applause.) The retiring MAYOR, Dr. Joseph, expressed his thanks to Aldermen Tinnion and Burton for the tribute they had paid to the Mayoress and himself. At the last meeting he had been .guilty of an error of omission, namely, to thank the officials for the assistance he had received from them during his tern: of office.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>ELECTED UNANIMOUSLY.</head>
        <p>ELECTED UNANIMOUSLY.</p>
        <p>The resolution was then put and carried unanimously, and the new Mayor, in the official robes, was escorted into the Council Chamber by Dr. Joseph and Aldermen Tinnion and Bur- ton. Ire was loudly cheered on entering. The Retiring MAYOR: In declaring Councillor Peter Peacock Mayor of the Borough, I can only say I shall have the utmost pleasure in assist- ing him in the performance of his duties as far as I possibly can. I feel sure he will 'make an excellent Mayor, and I think lie understands I will thoroughly help him to the best of my ability. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>\THE MAYORESS'S THANKS.</head>
        <p>\THE MAYORESS'S THANKS.</p>
        <p>The new MAYORESS at this stage entered, and in response to the Council's and public greet- ing said: I must thank you heartily for the great honour you have conferred upon my hus- band. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>SENS XTIONAL DEVELOPMENT.</head>
        <p>SENS XTIONAL DEVELOPMENT.</p>
        <p>A Whole Time Mayor.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR, after taking the various oaths, said: If there is a moment in one's life which touches one's feelings it is the moment when any individual is elected to this great and exalted office of Mayor of this ancient borough. I thank you from my heart very sincerely for the great honour you have conferred upon me by electing me Mayor of Warrington, a place of great antiquity, frequented by the Romans and mentioned, in the Doomsday Book by the name of Wallintune. It is an honour which does not come to all men, and I indeed appreci- ate it very highly. This is a very ancient borough, made a Parliamentary 'borough by the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>RIGHT HAND OF FELLOWSHIP.</head>
        <p>THE RIGHT HAND OF FELLOWSHIP.</p>
        <p>One is the Baths extension, which has been drawn out for so 'long a time, twhich I hope will be settled within the next feiv months. Then there is the very important question of the War- rington Bridge. . The terms of the. contract were, entered into before I entered this Council last, in November of last year, for the third time, and I do not know the contract time, Nut we have the assurance of the Chairman of the Bridges Committee, Alderman Smethurst, that,. in his opinion, the Bridge will be finished dur- ing the next twelve months. I hope there will be no more putting off of the day of completion. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>I desire to express my personal appreciation of the tacit understanding which has been come to with regard to the relics of the older bridges of 1837 and 1495, and the earlier oak bridge, and I am pleased that through the instrumentality of my friend, Alderman Bennett, a suitable place near the new bridge will be allocated to commemorate the various bridges. (Hear, hear.) I can assure you, gentlemen, I will use my best efforts to fulfil the duties as worthily as did my predecessors. I shall know no politics during my Mayoralty. (Hear, hear.) I hope, however, you will bear with me in my imper- fec_tions, which we all have. I BEG TO EXTEND TO EACH AND EVERY MEMBER OF THE COUNCIL MY RIGHT HAND OF FELLOWSHIP, hoping that friendship may continue from this day, and that we. may be blessed in our work for the good old town of Warrington. (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>DEPUTY-MAYOR.</head>
        <p>THE DEPUTY-MAYOR.</p>
        <p>The Mayor then announced that be had selected Dr. Joseph as Deputy-Mayor, and Canon Willis, Rector of Warrington, as his Chaplain. The Council approved of the ap- pointments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>MAYOR'S SUNDAY.</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>Later the Mayor extended an invitation to the Council and public to attend with him at the Parish Church on Mayor's Sunday, to-morrow morning.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Since writing my letter of the 11th inst., Councillor Peacock has again been interviewed by the Press and now states that "he was under the impression that he used only the word 'pressed,' and not the words 'approached and pressed,' but being assured-by several reporters that both words were used he wishes to state frankly that the word 'approached'. is mis- leading "—or, in other words, untrue. Councillor Peacock therefore now admits that I have never " approached " him on the sub- ject of the Mayoralty, and consequently the abominable insinuation of motive, which he attributed to me, proves equally baseless. Coun- cillor Peacock's further statement "that pres- sure was brought to bear on him by Councillor Roberts, and, through Councillar Roberts, by. Mr. John Crosfield, ,to withdraw from his can- didature for the Mayoralty," is as untrue as both his previous statements. Not the slightest effort was made at any time to induce Mr. Pea- cock either to remain in the firm'e-service or to retire from the Mayoralty. For six weeks past I have been aware that insinuations and rumours reflecting upon my reputation as an honourable and fair-minded man have been diligently circulated, both among our own workpeople and others of my fellow- townsmen, and I have had to endure this in silence; but while strongly deprecating the gross abuse of privilege which enabled Councillor Peacock to attack both our firm and myself from his safe entrenchment in the Mayor' chair, knowing, as he did, that I could not there reply to him, I am 'glad on other grounds for the opportunity afforded to me of disproving his allegations, and at the same time expressing my regret that a man whom I have many times befriended should prove himself so incapable of gratitude.—Yours truly, HENRY IZOBEitTS. Warrington, November 12th, 1913.</p>
      </div>
