      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS'S DENIAL.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS'S DENIAL.</p>
        <p>Councillor Henry Roberts, who is, of course, a director of Crosfield's, handed us the following personal letter at the same time :</p>
        <p>TO THE EDITOR OF "THE EXAMINER."</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln his speech of yesterday Councillor Peacock stated that "lie was approached by Councillor Roberts, who was also a candidate for the position (of Mayor), and through him by Mr. John Crosfield, the chairman of the firm of J. Crosfield and Sons, who told him that holding the position of •Mayor of the Borough was incompatible with his (Mr. Peacock) hold- ing the position of traffic manager at Crosfield's. This statement of Councillor Peacock's is untrue in both particulars. I have never at any time approached Mr. Peacock on the ques- tion of the Mayoralty, and prior to the time of the interview which took place on September 10th last, I had withdrawn absolutely as a can- didate for the office, which fact was known to Mr. Peacock, because I told him so in Mr. Crosfield's presence.—' ant yours truly, HENRY ROBERTS. Warrington, November 11th, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>MAYOR EXPLAINS.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR EXPLAINS.</p>
        <p>The Mayor was interviewed by a representa- tive of "The Examiner" on Tuesday night, with reference to the letters from Messrs. Cros- field and Sons and Councillor Henry Roberts. The answer of the firm, he said, called for no reply. With regard to Councillor Roberts he said:</p>
        <p>"I am under the impression that I did not use the words approached and pressed,' but only the word 'pressed'; but, having been assured by several reporters that both words were used, I wish to state frankly that the word approached' is misleading. What I in- tended to say, and what I had written on the notes from which I spoke, was pressed.' What I wished to convey was that pressure was brought to bear by Councillor. Roberts, and, through him, by Mr. John Crosfield, to with- draw from my candidature for the Mayoralty."</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS'S REJOINDER. TO THE EDITOR OF THE EXAMINER?'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>MAYOR DECLARES FOR PEACE, BUT</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR DECLARES FOR PEACE, BUT</p>
        <p>CANNOT RETRACT.</p>
        <p>The Mayor, on being interviewed once again, said:</p>
        <p>"Councillor Roberts evidently tries to com- plicate matters when he speaks of having be- friended me in the past. Anything he has done for me has been well earned. "The question at issue now is that of the Mayoralty and my disraissal. What Councillor Roberts says on that subject is merely a quibble with words in my speech. I cannot retract from the position I have taken up, and I do not wish to go further into the matter.</p>
        <p>"-So far as I am concerned the controversy may rest now, and all soreness be .buried."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>FINAL REMARKS.</head>
        <p>FINAL REMARKS.</p>
        <p>Yesterday Councillor Roberts sent us he fol- lowing letter : Sir,—ln reply to Councillor Peacock's last statement, I desire to say that the issue between us is not a mere "quibble about words," but an infinitely more serious matter. The honour of our firm and my personal character were both made the subjects of an unwarrantable attack by Councillor Peacock, and the only satisfaction which .has been derived from his. abuse of his Mayoral privilege is the fact that we have been thereby afforded a public opportunity of clear- ing the air from the baseless and wicked rumours which have been in circulation for many weeks past. Councillor Peacock says "he cannot retract from the position he has taken up." In his own interests this is unfortunate, especially in view of the serious admissions he has been compelled to make of the "misleading" character 6r-tire most serious of his allegations;\ and the conse- quent dubiety of all the rest.- . If Councillor Peacock is sincere in his desire "that this controversy may -rest now, and all soreness be buried," I am prepared to accept "the hand of fellowship," which he somewhat inconsistently offered at the close Of his un- fortunate speech on Monday last, provided be agrees to " bury " everything finally and beyond all possible resuscitation. On these coAditions, it will be my pleasure to give him all the help I can to make his Mayoralty a pleasant and successful one.</p>
        <p>Yours faithfully, U. ROBERTS. Warrington, 13th Nov., 1913.</p>
        <p>LIFE TOO SHORT TO QUARREL. The Mayor, seen by our representative in reference to the above, said:</p>
        <p>, "I cannot, consistently with the dignity of the high office of Mayor, continue the bandying of words.</p>
        <p>"I adhere emphatically to the gist of my re- marks. Life is too short to continue to quarrel indefinitely with anyone. I accept Councillor Roberts's offer to bury the past completely, and I hope, in the interests of the town, we shall be able to work together arnicably4 There will be no difficulty now Councillor Roberts and I close the controversy." Ready-made.</p>
        <p>These are the days of ready-mades. Men have reach-me-down ,opinions, but you can generally recognise them. Like reach-me-.down suits, they don't fit quite snugly.</p>
