      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <p>E.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>TOINNSIIIP TITTLE-TATTLE.</p>
        <p>lli, 'it? . , , 1</p>
        <p>t</p>
        <p>The Parochial Committee had a new member. It consisted of a tdy terrier--La dawg! • • All the debates were sO weighty that The doggie slept peacefukly by the fire. A cerium n Councillor . mentioned the word ".rats." Then doggie turned over and growled. • At the tip 'Lis said a larger rat population exists</p>
        <p>Than there is a human population in the Heath. Fortunately they keep to one small spot. None of us want a visit from them. "When the pigs begin to fly, then look out!" Sc said a music-hall comedian of repute. A local Councillor stated that "the pigs were playing." ,Another said that they were very peculiar, In that they always lived in the open. • Like the Arcadians, we .used to hear about, The "piggies" believe in the simple life. Dan Leno once said, when he got a tough steak, That he recognised it as an old horse Which he knew by the name of "Jim." A' Councillor, talking about the odours from old bones</p>
        <p>Alleged to come from butchers' shops in the Heath,</p>
        <p>Added that he well knew the smell. • Such merriment made the Parochial Com- mittee smile. The people of Mars are reported to be trying To signal to we .folk upon the earth By means of brightly illuminated lamps.</p>
        <p>This week the Maritians- will come to • The conclusion that theft' signals answered.</p>
        <p>Two new lamps are to be lit at. Lumb.Broc.! There was an unconscious bit of humour</p>
        <p>In the Sanitary Inspector's• report on the odours</p>
        <p>Which annoy the Grappenhall end of the</p>
        <p>"There is a much larger population now in "The district," he wrote, "and therefore, it is "Reasonable to expect a larger number, 'of cr,miplaints." True, my lord, quite true-; and it was not fair That the Parochial. Committee should have laughed at what Was, after all, very 'sound logic, indeed.</p>
        <p>1f the people are there, so are the odours,</p>
        <p>"What is the position with regard to the fire bell and appliance cupboard?" So queries a Stockton Heath correspondent, fira he adds:</p>
        <p>"I wonder if, this is the real reason? "The reinoval of the shed and the bell hang-, ing at the Police Station "Is it to be done simultaneously, and the shed cannot be moved</p>
        <p>' "Because the passage abuts on land which It_ag °To be paved under the Private Streets Con- struction Act?"</p>
        <p>Perhaps some of the Councillors will answer our correspondent's query.</p>
        <p>Parochial Committee.—The Stockton Heath Parochial Comthittee met on Tuesday night, Councillor George Pilling presided, and there wogs present Councillors J. Whitley, J. H. Chxrke, W. Mclntyre, Charles Dukes, A. Bottom- ley, J. Hallows, C. W. Hallows, F. Pearson and J. McDermid.</p>
        <p>Councillor H. J. Westbrook.—After his long illness, Councillor H. J. Westbrook returned to business this week. At a meeting of the Stock- ton Heath Parochial dommittee on Tuesday a letter, was received from him expressing his thanks for the •resolution of sympathy passed at the last meeting of the committee.</p>
        <p>The Sunday Gambling Pest. —The Stockton Heath pollee are following up the crusade against Sunday gambling, which has proved such an annoyance of late. As a result of a raid upon a "school" of gamblers in Mayfield- drive on 28th October, four youths appeared at the local Petty Sessions on Tuesday and were fined is.</p>
        <p>Extensions at the Forge.—A plan was passed far the erection of an addition to the Stockton Heath Forge at the monthly meeting of' the Parochial Committee on Tuesday, on condition that the walls were made of brick 14in. thick instead of nine, and that damp_courses be put as required by the building by-laws. - Lumb Brook Lights.—The lighting of the road at the dangerous corner near Lumb Brook will soon be en accomplished fact. Councillor ,Whitley reported to the Parochial Committee on Tuesday that the gas main was laid and the position-of the lamps had been fixed. A slight change had been, made from the original pro- posal. as it would not have been safe to have fixed a lamp at the first position proposed.</p>
        <p>The Recreation Ground.—Councillor A. Bot- tomley sarcastically inquired at the Parochial Committee on Tuesday if a committee had not been appointed to deal with the question of pro- viding a recreation ground for Stockton Heath. —The Chairman, Councillor Piling, replied there was a committee appointed.—Councillor Bottemley : What have they done?—Mr. Ashton, the Clerk : There has been no meeting.</p>
        <p>Sanitation.—An echo of the pointed remarks made at the recent Parish .Meeting about the sanitation of the district was beard at, the meeting of the Parochial Committee on Tuesday evening, when the Sanitary Committee ap-</p>
        <p>Ready—for—Service Overcoats</p>
        <p>OUR OWN MAKE, from 45/= NEW AND CHOICE GOODS.</p>
        <p>GEORGE SUTTON</p>
        <p>(LATE H. 5. PAGF. ec CO.)</p>
        <p>74, SAINK.7Y ST. Warrington. TEL 553. LEGH ST. CORNER.</p>
        <p>May Queen's Death.—We regret to announce the death of Miss Maud Kirkland, a well-known local young lady and the last May Queen of Warrington. A full account of the sad bereave- ment will be found in another column.</p>
        <p>The Circus Man.—lgnorance of the law was pleaded by William Sedgwick, . circus owner, Oldham, charged at Stockton Heath Police Court, on Tuesday, with not exhibiting the axle weight and the tare weight of a motor trailer. Inspector Breese said these were not displayed on the vehicle, as the law required. Sedgwick was fined 10s., including costs.</p>
        <p>The Fire Brigade.—Councillor McDermid, as chairman of the Fire Appliance Committee, reported to the Stockton Heath Parochial Com- mittee on Tuesday that the repaired fire hose had been tested on the low pressure mains and had stood the test well. They had had a good turn out at a recent practice of the firemen, and the results were satisfactory.</p>
        <p>Tar-spraying of Roads.—The question of tar- spraying of, certain roads was again mentioned at the meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee on Tue(sday. The Borough Surveyor of Warrington wrote in reply to a letter from Mr. Ashton, the' Clerk to the Committee, that he did not know if it would be possible to hire the Warrington tar-sprayer for the work when the busy season arrived. The matter was left in abeyance meantime.</p>
        <p>The Motorist Forgot.—Forgetting that he had his motor driver's licence in his pocket, Walter Sutton, a Warrington motorist, told the police he could not find it when asked to produce it. After a short time he re-discovered it in his pocket. He was charged at the local Court for failing to produce the licence, and he was also charged with having the rear lamp of the motor in such a state as not toi exhibit the number.— P.C. Worthington said the lamp was so dirty that it would not show the number.—Case dis- missed on'payment of costs.</p>
        <p>Boating in Derby-road.—The delay in paving Derby-road was mentioned at the Parochial Committee on Tuesday night.—Councillor C. Dukes: Have you any idea when Derby-road is to be made? I see they are boating on the pools there.—The Clerk, Mr. Ashton, said delay had arisen through the sudden death of the con- tractor, Mr. Stringer, but the work would be re-started immediately.—Councillor Dukes said cases of illness there had been attributed to the water in the road.</p>
        <p>"Peculiar Pig s. "—Councillor Dukes, at a meeting of the Parochial Committee on Tues- day, complained of a nuisance he alleged was caused by pigs on a farm in the centre of the village. He had seen the pigs " playing " about within a few yards of the people's back doors.— Councillor J. Whitley: They are peculiar pigs. This breed live out of doors..—Councillor J. Pil- ling, the chairman, said the Sanitary Inspector's attention would be drawn to the matter.</p>
        <p>Photo Frauds.—A well-dressed young man named George Allen Cooper, of Stockton Heath, was brought up on three charges of obtaining money by false pretences at St. Helens on Tues- day. The prisoner called at houses in the Thatto Heath district, and asked if they wanted photographic enlargements making of anyone. In e4ch case he was given an order for a couple of enlargements, and then asked for a deposit, receiving sums of 55., 55., and 6s. 3d. The next day he returned the photographs, but there was no explanation with reference to the enlarge- ments, and when inquiries were made he had gone. The prisoner pleaded guilty to all the charges, and was committed for six months, three months on each of two charges.</p>
        <p>-""141114 11T</p>
      </div>
