        <p>Address</p>
        <p>F 4</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>ZlOfor the BESoTr x.RinEACSIPEpufIo)rmaNGTXMAS CAKE OPEN TO ALL WOMEN. NO ENTRANCE FEE.</p>
        <p>CO U PON.</p>
        <p>1101101 mom</p>
        <p>Councillor Pearson: Yes, and I know the smell well. (Laughter.) The Chairman: The contractor comes from Widnes, and if we write him will that meet with the approval of Mr. Dukes? Councillor Dukes assented and the matter dropped.</p>
        <p>THE GRAPPENHALL ODOURS. Whence Lies the True Source?</p>
        <p>I agree to accept the decision of .the Directors.</p>
        <p>No recipes will be returned. z</p>
        <p>75; Norbury 75, Webster. 68; Crossley .74, Ad- croft ,75; Isherwood 75, .ParlcgT 34; .llankey 75. Smith 15; Lampard_ 61, Webster 75; Spencer 65, Webb 75; FairhUrst 45; Stockton Heath, 520; Academy-street, 491. .Stockton .Heath won by 31 points The following members met at Academy- streets School, Stockton Heath nam;esj. fii.t Green- wood Messrs. 75, AW. law A. 0 oik7tsliel4rn IBradbury 000dhead 75, G. Baxter 46; F. Lynch 55, J. Tilley 75; G. Rushton 45, J. Walker 75; J. Vernon 75, A. Walker 54; A. Isherwood 75, A. Davies 40; J. Holt 42, G. Bailey 75; Stockton Heath, 517; Academy-street, 423. Stockton Heat)ewon by 94 points. •</p>
        <p>The odours which emanate- from the end of. the' parish near Grapbenhali were again a sub- ject for discussion at tilt) meeting of the Stock- ton Heath Parochial Committee on Tuesday evening, Councillor Pilling presiding. The Sanitary Inspector reported on the matter and said he attributed the smell largely to the discharge of manure boats at the Latchford Wharf, and these gave off very objectionable odours. About 3,000 tons of manure were annually discharged at the wharf, and it was difficult to suggest a way to obviate the nuis- ance. "There added the report, "a larger population now in the district, and therefore it is very reasonable to expect a larger number of complaints." (Laughter, and a Member: "Logic.") Councillor Bottomley did not agree with the report. There was no doubt very offensive smells emanatal from the wharf, but more particularly offensi smells came from where the refuse of Stockton Heath was tipped. There was paper there, and surely that did not come from the canal boats. The paper was very offensive and it came from where the rubbish was tipped. He had also found a geld three-parts covered with refuse, and the stench was abotainable. There were many particular smells which emanated from the tip, as there were .many offensive things beside ashes tipped there. The time had come when the Committee must provide a proper tip for themselves and keep it under proper control. Councillor Dukes: How can we prevent a butcher or a fish dealer from putting stuff there, even if it is a nuisance?</p>
        <p>Councillor Pearson: We cannot. The tip is in another parish.</p>
        <p>"Alive With Rats."</p>
        <p>"Dolls' Night."—St. Thomas' Band of Hope members and, friends crowded thb Parish Room on Thursday evening. It was "dolls' night," and over 100 dolls were brought, which were arranged on the platform by the lady workers. Several recitations referring to 'dells Were very nicely given by the members, and one by Mrs. Wliiteman met with a, cordial reception. Mr. Hallows spoke or! "Famous dolls and Bible babies." Mrs. Milling, who had kindly called in to see the children, was asked by the lady workers to present the secretary; Mr. Stannard, with a christemng.bonnet'ar!d fall for his little baby girl. Mrs. Milling said how pleased sh&amp; was to see such a very happy gathering, and wished Mr. and Mrs. Stannard and Baby Stan- nard every good wish. The secretary thanked</p>
        <p>Councillor Bottomley : There is a larger popu- lation at the tip where you place your rubbish than in Stockton Heath. I should think there are ten rats at that tip for every human being in Stockton Heath. It is absolutely terrible; it is alive with the rats. (Laughter.) Councillor McDermid : Is it not possible to make representations to get the wharf removed to some place where there is a less population? Councillor Pearson: They are unfortunate for smells at that end of the parish. There is a slaughter-house, which is also abominably offensive; there is the manure wharf and other things, including our Own tip at the Cobbs. In. humid, quiet weather the people, there are worse. off— Councillor J. Hallows: Mr. Reporters, do not put that in—Grappenhall-road is the flower, of the village. (Laughter.) Councillor McDermid moved that representa - tio.ns be made to have the wharf removed, and this was agreed to unanimously.</p>
        <p>and the Tory party seemed to be getting into its second childhood. (Laughter and applause.) The Liberals had held their land inquiry, they had formulated their land proposals, and they were to go on with their, land campaign, and after that campaign they woull get the neces- sary legislation. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>, , – La., W 3</head>
        <p>, , - La., W 3</p>
        <p>THE IDIOTIC BACILLUS.</p>
        <p>Councillor H. Roberts and the To</p>
        <p>Position.</p>
        <p>MR. P. M. OLIVER AND 'HOME RULE.</p>
        <p>An enthusiastic meeting of Liberals, to in- augurate the winter campaign, was held in the Hall Cafe, Stockton Heath, on Thursday even- ing. Councillor H. Roberts, who presided, said he felt rather sorry for the Tory party. He did not remember any other occasion in which that party were in such a hopeless and desponding mess as they were to-day. Their programme proved how utterly the failed to get the ear and sympathy of the electors. It was perfectly true with whatever they toed,: in hand they tried to do. the people thoroughly. _ -</p>
        <p>The old question of rfari-ff Reform, in spite of many infusions of corpse reviver, had become nearly dead, and its body had become a bit ob- jectionable to the political olfactory nerves. Their attacks on the Insurance Act were far less effective than they were even a few weeks ago, and every child born, and every man, woman or child who had received benefit from treatment under the sanatorium • clauses were liv- ing testimonies to the falsity of the charges of the Tory party. On the land question they were in a singularly unhappy position. Already a large number of their younger men had espoused some of the principles incorpbrated in the proposals which Mr. Lloyd George had put be- fore the country. But the public must not be misled by that fact. They must remember the Tory attitude when they so proclaimed the pro- visions of the Insurance Act and promised all kinds of support, but when the Tories had the opportunity to make political capital their pro- mises and praise went to the winds, and proved how utterly the Tories were incapable of being depended upon. He hoped, therefore, nobody would-be deceived by Tory professions in regard to the land.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>" Streptococcus Idioticus."</head>
        <p>" Streptococcus Idioticus."</p>
        <p>. Then the Ulster fever was proving to be non- iidectious. The bacillus had been discovered. Its scientific name was "Streptococcus Idioti- cus." (Laughter.) Whatever might be the state of the patients aoross.the water there were very -few people troubled on this side. The country was sick and tired about Home Rule, and wanted to see the legislation carried into effect On both sides there would be immense relief when the Act was put into operation. The crowning misery of all for the Tory party was the speech intended to comfort the discontent that was delivered by Mr. Bonar Law at New- castle. How the Tory party, within 18 months of a General Election, could muster up pluck and spirit to enter into a contest with the re- motest hope of success with such advice from a' leader of that kind was beyond his comprehen- sion. The Liberal party was filled with hope and determination, and never before in the history of the country was so' much interest shown in any measure of reform as was shown in regard to the land. When that once came into operation, the labourer, the small farmer, and the landowner, and certainly the people in the towns, would be infinitely better off than their wildest imagination could possibly 'appre- hend. Liberals had to make the best of the opportunity when it arose, and to do everything they could to put men into Parliament in whom they had confidence, who would do their wishes with intelligence and determination, and make their country better. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>PEOPLE DISGUSTED.</head>
        <p>THE PEOPLE DISGUSTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. M. Oliver said the people were heartily disgusted over the Home Rule problem, and now the policy of the Government was to set the machinery of the Parlianent Act in, opera- tion, and place Home Rule on the Statute Book. (Hear, hear.) When he was in Ulster he was absolutely confirmed in his opinion that the opposition of Ulster to Home Rule was purely based upon ignorance and religious-intolerance. The opposition was a worked-up agitation. Larkin had been released in Dublin that day, and it was right lie should have been released. Whether it was right that Sir Edward Carson should be at Norwich that day was another matter. (Laughter and applause.) Ile was sorry at the way the Opposition, had received the land question. On that subject Lord Ridley said he. would not take into con- sideration any scheme proposed by a third-rate Welsh solicitor with a shady connection On the Stock Exchange. (Laughter.) Perhaps it would be better to have a third-rate Welsh solicitor than a fourth-rate English peer, but the Welsh solicitor had that modicum of brain which was absolutely inessential in a peer. (Laughter.) The Tory'party now had decided to ignore Lloyd George and the land problem, and notice had been given that no Unionist is to speak about the question, and they were to suppose that Mr. Lloyd:George, did not exist. (Laughter.) That was the Conservative attitude to all progfess,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>"Tears in His Voice."</head>
        <p>"Tears in His Voice."</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. Garside, Manchester, said because the Liberals, had succeeded in carrying through "the 'lnsurance Act and all OtherlegislatiOn 'pro- mised, the Tories called 'them reprobates. (Laughter.) When everything had been done upon scientific,, political methods the Tories called them revolutionists, red and gory, and the chief villain, of course, was the Chancellor of the Exchequer. (Laughter.) The Tories do not care a brass farthing about Home Rule. When Mr., Harold Smith, with tears in his voice—(A Voice: "Kick him out next time," . and laughter and applause)— bewails the position of the Ulster men—l be- lieve he thinks he is sincere—(A Voice: "You cannot touch him at that.") That is true, but you cannot doubt the source from which all this springs—(laughter)---and you find on this particular question that Mr. Harold Smith, like his famous brother, the Galloper—(laughter)— and the whole of the rest in the, category, have no love, sympathy, tenderness, or anything else, for the great democracy. What they are afraid of is losing the control over the legislative machinery of this country. (Loud applause.) Mr. J. R. Tomlinson, Knutsford, in proposing a vote of thanks, said their- opponents were suffering from the "staggers,' and didn't know where they were.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>LY MM.</head>
        <p>LY MM.</p>
        <p>LINES FROM LYMM.</p>
        <p>'Lymm has now got its lake. What will the ratepayers do with it? • Has boating been suggested at all? We have a faint, dim recollection That the subject Was once mentioned, And' that boating' thet with objection, As it was not desired, to turn Lyllllll,into a rowdy pleasure resort. Didn't someone say he didn't' Want " tu'penny Sunday schools to come There for the sake of the dam boating"? Although LYmin has now salt and waler, :-;1 It .water Blackpool has as yet • No need tO be lined with -jealousy At Lynn's-attractions as d " seasid,e " resort. What will the -Council do with the Dingle? Will they remove the natural beauty And infuse 'artificial landscape work, As have done other Council in similar cases? Lymm Council is a very sensible body, - And it is not likely they will do anything To mar the Dingle of its beauty. Temperance Sunday proved a marked success • Soriic strong sermons were preached against drink.</p>
        <p>Strong d 1 n 1;, of course, needs strong com- ments.</p>
        <p>Whist Drive.—At the Oughtringion • Social Club whist drive on Saturday the prize-winners were Messrs. James Tiolt, IV., Copeland and G. •Copeland.</p>
        <p>Hounds In Full Cry.--\Booth's Hill was' treated to a very unusual spectacle on Wednes day at noon, when the .Cheshire Hounds came along in full cry after a fox which had broken away and got into the Bongs. The appearance of the hounds and the scarlet, coated huntsmen presented a gay scene as they proceeded. up Cherry-lane to the Knutsford-road.</p>
        <p>Organ Recital.—At the monthly organ recital at the Parish Church on Sunday, the organist, Mr. W. Cooke, paid a tribute to the memory of the late Mr. W. S. Hodgson by selecting as his programme several pieces which were special favourites of the deceased gentleman, viz. : Marche Fulnebre et Chant Seraphique, A. Guil- mant ; Lieder ohne Worte " Duetto," Mendel- ssohn; and Offertoire, G. Major, L. Wely.</p>
        <p>Missionary M eeti ng.—A most interesting missionary meeting was held at the Higher-lane Baptist Chapel, when the chair with efficiently occupied by Mr. J. 'D. B. Smith, the honorary secretary of the local Free Church Council. A short statement concerning money raised for the Missionary Society during the past year was given by the Pastor, the Rev. E. E. Lovell. The deputation was the Rev. Trevor Roberts, who gave a most inspiring account of his work in India. The address was much appreciated, and at the clOse of the meeting hearty thanks were accorded' to the chairman and speaker..</p>
        <p>. Temperance Women.—The Lymm Branch of the British Women's Temperance Association held another successful meeting in the Primi- tive Methodist Schoolroom on Tuesday. Mrs. H. S. Brown presided over the good company present, which was addressed. by Miss Bond, of West Kirby, who gave a detailed and inspiring report of the meetings of the association, which wore held in London last May. The speaker also gave an appreciation and summary of the book "Out of the Abyss," laying stress on the pernicious effects of tampering with intoxicants 'even for medicinal purposes, and the terrible results which might thereby accrue. , Mr. Bal- sillie sang a solo, and afternoon tea was dis- pensed.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. J. Lawson.—lt is with deep regret that we announce the death of Mrs. Law- son, wife of Mr: Joseph Lawson, of Higher-lane, Lymm, who passed away at the Manchester Royal Infirmary on Sunday afternoon, after a painful illness. For many years Mrs. Lawson has been an active member of the„Broom Edge Primitive Methodist Chapel and Sunday School. A keen -enthusiast in the missionary cause, one of the last things she was troubled about was the future welfare of her missionary collecting box, and her death is rendered more pathetic by the fact that the annual missionary meetings are being held this week-end. As a cheerful and sympathetic person, she will be greatly missed in both. the public and Church life of the dis- trict. The funeral took Place on Wednesday, when the Rev. E. E. Lovell conducted a service in the Higher-lane Baptist Chapel, after which the Rev. F. W. Ferguson took charge of the committal service in the adjoining graveyard. .The chief mourners were the deceased's lady's husband, sister, and Sunday School scholars, whilst a large !Amber of persons were present in order to pay their last respects.</p>
        <p>Jumped Into the Canal.—Elsie Buckley', a young woman from Lymm, was charged at the Petty Sessions, held at Stockton Heath on Tues- day, with attempting suicide by jumping into the Bridgewater Canal at Latchford Without, on November 6th.—William Massey, a boatman, saw accused on the bank. She appeared dis- tressed and threw up her hands and jumped into the canal. The water was four feet deep at the spot. Massey moved his boat to the bank, got off,. and arriving at the spot where accused had been, found she had disappeared, but managed to get-her out. He asked her why she had acted like that. She replied that she was going to get married, an 4 that the "young fellow had deceived her." The boatman took her to Mr. Caldwell's house.—Josiah Caldwell, Chester-road, Latchford Without, spoke to tak- ing accused into his house and having her clothing dried.—P.C. Jones said when charged, accused replied, "Yes, I did."—:Mr. Thomas Ridgway, on the defendant's behalf, said the girl had for some considerable time been of a rather nervous temperament, and was attended by a doctor for nervous depression.. She had been upset about a love affair, and that had preyed on her mind. She now regretted her action. Her father (who was present in court) promised- to take her under his control, which was probably the best thing, and he asked that the case should be dismissed on that under- standing.—This was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>LAKE AND DINGLE PUBLIC PROPERTY.</head>
        <p>THE LAKE AND DINGLE PUBLIC PROPERTY.</p>
        <p>To-day (Saturday) sees- the fulfilment of the wishes of the 56 ratepayers who last January recorded their votes in favour of the Lymm Parliamentary Bill, for, in accordance with the agreement with the Lymm Water Company and the Lymm Estate Development Company, Ltd., the 'Council take over the Water Works,- Lake, Dingle, and certain other lands three months after the Royal Assent, which was given on August 15th last. Thus, whatever • other changes may take place in the' locality, the pic- turesque lake and land surrounding it, together with the Dingle, which Lymm people are justly proud of, have become the permanent posses- sions ef the inhabitants of the district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <p>Photograph by Drury.</p>
        <p>50-GUINEA CHAL LENGE TROPHY, Awarded .to Messrs. Hayes Bros., • Stockton Heath, at the International- Bakers' and Confectioners' Exhibition, Loudon, September, 1913. Inscription Championship Trophy, currant' bread class. Open to Master Bakers and Employes throughout the United Kingdom. Confectioners, Bakers, and Allied Trades Exhibition. London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>Adult School Cueists.</head>
        <p>Adult School Cueists.</p>
        <p>The members of the Social Club in connection with the Stockton Heath Adult School played two friendly billiard matches on Tuesday night with the members of the Academy-street School, and were successful in winning the double event.. The following members opposed each other. at Stockton Heath, the Stockton Heath names being first: Messrs. Aubinson 52, Harper</p>
        <p>• - , . , I Mrs. Milling and the workers for their kindness. i " Love at home " was sung and a very happy I meeting was brought to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>TALE OF BUTCHER'S "BONES." A Nuisance Alleged to Exist.</head>
        <p>A TALE OF BUTCHER'S "BONES." A Nuisance Alleged to Exist.</p>
        <p>Councillor C. Dukes drew the attention of the Stockton Heath" Parochial Committee on Tues- day evening to a nuisance which he alleged was occasioned by the collection of bones and refus6 meat from -butchers' shops. He said he con- sidered the practice an abomination. The'col- lecting cart stood often for half an hour at a shop and people passing were' half suffocated by the smell. He thought such material should be collected in, the night, as the present method was dangerous, as well as insanitary. Councillor Pilling, the chairman: A complaint was raised twelve months ago. The collecting contractors were written to and promised not to repeat the cause of annoyance. Councillor Mclntyre: The danger is not so much in the bones, but in the bags they are placed. Councillor Bottmnley : Cannot we take action? Councillor Mclntyre: They get only clean bones here.</p>
        <p>Councillor Dukes: And they bring sufficient microbes into the district to exterminate the lot of us. ,</p>
        <p>, Councillor Bottomley : Is it a nuisance? The Chairman: We can call the attention of the contractors to the complaint. Councillor Bottomley: If it is a nuisance you can do something. Councillor Mclntyre: The visits of the cart are not so long as they formerly were. Councillor Whitley: Mr. Dukes complaint is correct. I know, as_ I have passed the van.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <p>"THE GREAT DRINK WASTE."</p>
        <p>Rev. J. A. Penaliggon's Striking</p>
        <p>Comments.</p>
        <p>Temperance' Sunday was marked by the sermon delivered by the Rev. J. A. Penaliggon at the lyening service in the Lymm Congrega- tional Church. The Pastor said:</p>
        <p>There is a deadly: foe that to be fight- ing: furiously and irresistibly against- the best and truest and the noblest,life.of this country. There is a disease silently but surely eating out the very vitals and the health of the commu- nity. That foe is drink, that disease intemper- ance. The results are everywhere too appar- ent; the cry of the homeless, the sob of the little children, the cry of despair from lost souls as every day they pass over the frontier of time into a hopeless eternity. All of us have some causes, perhaps, almost to break our hearts, because of this evil and this terrible thing, aid yet here is the paradox, here is .the amazing fact, this trade which is responsible for so much human suffering, this thing which can divide families, break hearts, and can mar God's best and brightest work, this thing is a privileged and protected trade, and I venture to say, too, there is not a trade so protected and so 'privileged in England as this drink trade. We have heard a good deal about shopkeepers open- ing on Sundays, but when we close our services the public-houses will he open to offer our young men and women the thing which will ruin their .very souls.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>Privileged and Protected.</head>
        <p>Privileged and Protected.</p>
        <p>Privileged, Protected, given every facility for development! why is it? We are told the revenue it brings in is a very important item for the national exchequer. I agree the revenue it brings in is a very important item.. -Take three of our great institutions, the gaol, the asylum, the workhouse : :who keeps them going; who has to pay. for their upkeep, but the nation? But who are they who go into them? Granted we have great revenue from the drink trade, but then, on the other hand, we have to keep these institutions going; these institutions which are fed from the hotel and public-house; and I put it to you in a common-sense way, it is an extremely bit of bad finance that we keep these institutions going. We have on the top of it ft degraded manhood and something which is sap- ping our very strength, power and happiness. That is extremely bad business. They protect themselves in Parliament, and everywhere and in everything connected with their trade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>All Important Question.</head>
        <p>All Important Question.</p>
        <p>It is quite time we realised something of the importance of this all important question. Because we are so familiar with it we take very little notice. Its very presence amongst us has blinded our eyes too often. We must waken to it. It is small use trying to uplift and ennoble our manhood and the life of the cOmmunity when there are these temptations and these stumbling blocks which make it impossible for a man to live the life he ought fo live. First of all, I want us to consider the duty of the State in relation to this question. Good statesmanship must first of all have a sound moral policy. That is an essential. It must also have a sound domestic or home policy, that is, first to protect the children. We have begun to understand this in a way, but we hare not finished.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>Cry of the Children.</head>
        <p>The Cry of the Children.</p>
        <p>There are 75,000 Children in England who have never had a chance because of drink, and who have not had sufficient food to eat,-and further there are hundreds of children who are born drunk. Can you imagine that in Christian Eng- land? I say that a sound domestic policy is going to say this thing must stop, otherwise it is going to sap the life of the nation. A sound nation must protect the weaker members of the community. You cannot make a man altogether sober by Act of Parliament, but you can do a great deal towards it. True 'statesmanship pro- tects the children and the weak members of the community, addresses itself to weak sources of national life, and says we must give men an opportunity to grow as God means them to do. The true domestic policy must put all stumbling blocks out of their way to look to the welfare of the people, the care of those who are in awk- ward positions, and those who are down, to see that these have better housing and proper con- ditions of living, and to watch so that men are not so harassed by care that they have to go and drown their troubles in drink. These things are the duty of the State in relation to this question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>Duty of the Church.</head>
        <p>The Duty of the Church.</p>
        <p>In the second place we conic to the duty of the Church. It is the duty of the Church to inspire the community, and it is the duty of the com- munity to insist for good government, but we of the Church are responsible for good govern- ment. I venture to say everything for the up- lifting of humanity has been born in the Church. It is only the Church that can take to the State the true value of the individual, and it is only because we see the true potentialities of the nation we say you must give him better conditions. Our work is to fail if we do not do this. It is our privilege as the Church not to preach small reform, for we can offer you the strength necessary which will enable you to stand firm in this battle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>Preventive Work.</head>
        <p>The Preventive Work.</p>
        <p>Then there is the preventive work in connec- tion with this movement. We have to see about licensing reform. It is the Churches' work that the public-houses have to be publicly conducted. We have to do everything we can to improve this state of affairs; we must not merely talk about this matter and listen to a recital of its dire results. We must be quickened in our re- solve that anything that mars men and destroys human personality is evil, and we must wage a hard warfare against it. In the third place, there is the responsibility of the individual. When I began to preach I believ6 I was not an out-and-out temperance worker. But I soon had to learn. I put it to you our duty is clear in this matter. If drink can take away people from the community of the Church and make them despise the Church, it is an evil thing. In the name of Jesus Christ, Who came that we might be sons of God, we</p>
        <p>For Early Risers</p>
