      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>PEN K ETH.</head>
        <p>PEN K ETH.</p>
        <p>Lord Wolmer's Visit—A meeting was held the Penketh Wesleyan School's on Thursc.l. evening, presided over by Mr. Fulton-SD:II4 Lord . Wolmer was the principal speaker IV j confined his remarks chiefly to Home Rule an Welsh Disestablishment.</p>
        <p>The Tannery Band.—The Penketh Tanneq Band •;:ook part in the instrumental rlllaAroter competition held on Saturday in the Wis:o Barracks, Edgehill, Liverpool. There lveri.be sets in the competition. Mr. J. C. Taylor, conductor of Crosfield's Band, Warrington, sci; the adjudicator. Results: 1, Earlestown NO. 2, Liverpool North End; 3, Penketh Tanner.' 4, Earlestown No. 2. "The Cloister and the Hearth."—lt is on rare occasions that Penketh is favoured such an entertainment as Mr. Charles Posvehi recital of "The Cloister and the Efeart-'o which was given in the Wesleyan Schools lit'e Tuesday night, under the auspiees of r Penketh Wesley Guild. Mrs. Charles E. 133"-eeb. Presided. She, personally, was pleased that su,er, a choice had been made for that evening's 0114 tainment, although there were some who 103,1,1 have preferred a miscellaneous prograinn:re to those she would say a word or two. ITOe was no doubt that a miscellaneous progrtln. ir was pleasinc, and interesting, but, after all, thet. Guild stood' for a great deal more than and they should have something of educati"g value. "The Cloister and the Hearth fp, one of the most wonderful books ever It was recommended to her by Mr. Moulton, now Professor of Literature at Chicago University, as one of the,most interesting 110 t in the English language, and those who ha", oit read the book would get a good insight inlio as the result of the recital. (Applause.) , recital was listened to with great interest sd few realised that two and a quarter hours, nk, so l p sped by in unfolding and h olading the lecturer reAr vote s o t pa t the close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Cricketers at Whist—Members of the Cricicet Club sought to knock, up big scores at whit and they held an enjoyable whist drive ao dance in St. Mary's Schools. Whist results:"... Ladies: 1, Mrs. Ray, 211 points; consolation' Mrs. W. Booth, 175. Men: E. Bullen, 212; solation, H. Daintith, 175. Mr. W. Booth, whist, and Mr. Plumpton, for dancing, af:e acted as M.C.'s. Mrs. G. Clarke was in Ow' of the refreshments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>ORFORD.</head>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>o Promising Life Ended.—The death occurrel,llstl' Monday, after a very short illness, of 3L,iss Carpenter, the six-year-old daughter of Mrs. Robert Carpenter, a well-known Wer'es" r ton family, now resident in Orford. gr'iittle penter is one of the school managers. The :;cittf girl was a member of the Day and Su"Sli's Schools, and was a general favourite. ,ere used to take part in the concerts which "tile held in the school, and was preparing Or ot pageant at the Missionary Exhibition Øe week. week. The cause of death was diphtheria. Anes- funeral took place at the Cemetery on We'. rs day afternoon. A large number of wreaths ter3. sent, including those from her fellow-sell°-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>eet' Wesley Guild.—The monthly devotional ifr ing was held on Monday evening. The Beic.l7! Morgan presided and the roll was calleuver' Mr. H. Tomlin. The address was by the roll W. A. Beckett, who took as the subject el. admirable discourse, "The greatest of Vice/ Miss Alio() Brough contributed a solo, "In.,.enas° of love/6 with much expression. The C110",:4 t° expresspd the appreciation of the merabe— the speaker for his visit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>MOORE.</head>
        <p>MOORE.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—There was a good atteu-ict` t;• on Wednesday, when the speaker was gr. roe O'Callaghan, of the Warrington Branch cu° ti o• British Union for the Abolition of Vivisect Taking for his subject "The Cat of Bobllg-ithe and showing a number of beautiful Sll' Pt lecturer traced the history of the cat in L113.c and modern times—from her domestication- i;er retrieving of game under the E" tians,tn° hard lot under the witchcraft pePrsecot'°":goi" the Middle Ages, and on to the fuller ref. pi' tion of her good qualities in the presen"felio An extremely interesting analysis of the race's mental powers, their surpassing °All" f• or spirit of independence, and deep regal.' Atl" those who befriend them, was also givell'dotoo verting ,to the burnings and other cruelties inflicted upon this valuable servti-s• ou former times, the outcome of ignorance it . superstition, the lecturer claimed that the ;les tyrdom of the cat still continued on the of selfish interest at the behests °'frlie medical tyranny and a spurious scienc,e'petioo pain and misery thus inflicted in viv's—ph 05 upon a number of sensitive creatures su-tileir the cat was a cruel wrong, an insult Maker, and should arouse the righteous 1-110 nation and anger of every thoughtful wan woman. Mr. J. S. Cartwright presided.</p>
