      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>ABOUT LYEb.</head>
        <p>ABOUT LYEb.</p>
        <p>, M. Boucheny e Grandval, a French writer, 11as published a study of the appearance of in- -Bcriptions on the iris of the eye. The "Cologne azette" gives the following extracts :—Dr. produces,' on the evidence of a -physi- . eian of Montpellier named Formius, the case, Of a young man who had on the iris of his left qe an inscription in very legible ildnian 4cChar- ters, "Loue -soit Dieu" (God be praised!). Another case is that of Josephine Louis. This 1:11111g girl was born in Paris in 1825. As she carhe of poor parents, they accepted the offer of a showman, and let her be exhibited as a curi- ?,sitY, first in France, afterwards in Ghent, and in England. On the iris of one eye was to be seen the Word "Napoleon," on .the other; "Emperor:,</p>
        <p>Well-known doctors, Roguetta, Giraldes, Cornaz, and Wilde examined the girl, and the last-named reported as follows: "A few years ago I heard of a child on the retina of whose e3re the words 'Napoleon Empereur ' were writ- This phenomenon might have been caused ",Y an unusual arrangement of the cavities, ithrils, and streaks which fire characteristic ele- lhents of the surface of the eye. I possess," "ded the writer, who was at that time a physi- lan in Dublin, "very good engravings of Jose- Louis's face. On the lower half of the V.gh-t iris are marks which resemble the letters ellning the word empereur,' and the word ;r4aPoleon ' occupies just about the same posi- 'On on the left retina." „ In the eighteenth century the celebrated eye 713ecialist Jacques Daniel had informed scien- 'lle circles that in the eye of a peasant woman i "ving in the neignbourhood of Rouen he had detected marks which resembled the atin 'yards "Post Mortem." Finally, Dr. Tenon, d"lom no one would think of charging with- a rsire to mystify the credulous, writes concern- Ing a case which singularly resembles that of he'sePhine Louis: "On the blue retina are to "he seen white stars, some having the shape of htlinerals, while another might be described as 'ving a resemblance to a capital T." It :"°uld be added that Tenon had a copy of this `ernarkable eye made in enamel.</p>
        <p>,_OTTINGS BY JOURNALIST—cont.' be looked upon as a gambling transaction. Altlas! those who gamble will gamble at any- Prior to the Victorian era, gaming was e among the "upper ten." I read that Sundays 4„tal weekdays throughout the London season e gaming tables were crowded. High play 11c1 ruination were the fashionable talk of the I°Wn. The stakes were enormous. Lord Car- lost £lO,OOO at one cast at the club. Lord tavordale won the same amount at a throw !O, the Cocoa Tree and "swore a great oath, say- 11\bg ' If I had been playing deep, I might-have vc'n millions.'" We may read how Lord 1304, •.Y, "having lost a very monstrous sum of i.:,°rleY," took the liberty of applying to Selwyn ns'r a debt which he owed him; how Fitzpatrick, ‘A:vP„P„r,e'llehed by Selwyn with the same object, bf'u have "coined his heart and dropped his it °°(l into drachmas" had he been able, but as to Was he could not raise a guinea. We learn that Admiral Rodney had to run off to ram-ice to avoid the bailiffs. Fox sat down one %edrling at Brooks's at seven in the evening, tf" Played till five on the following afternoon. e then went to the House of Commons, and (telivered a speech upon the Church weharles Fox prepared himself for that holy Orli," says Cibbon, "by passing twenty-two thcitirs in the pious, exertion of hazard." After to? debate he went to White's, where he drank hu seven in the morning. A few hours later returned to Brooks's, where he won £6,000 hazard, arid between three and four in the "gernoon he left London for the races at New- -4 , ‘et. At a subsequent period, faro became of uesperately fashionable game, and "the scion nlanY a noble house fell a victim to its t,Seination. But that also in time died a Oral death. In this twentieth century we 4° not hear of such high stakes, but the evil is u7ll,_eh more widespread than ever it was. peas at the time to which I have referred letY (with a capital Sr was systematically irtY of astounding follies in the way of bet- and gambling, the habit has now got hold kv,the masses, and may be said to honeycomb 'tele communities throughout the country.</p>
        <p>- 44</p>
      </div>
