      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <p>, ,</p>
        <p>THE TUTORIAL CLASS.</p>
        <p>A Movement that Aims at the</p>
        <p>Re-making of Society.</p>
        <p>A POPULAR RENAISSANCE.</p>
        <p>The lecture was finished and the subject was open for discussion. Twenty-five !men and women of various creeds and politics, -and of very various abilities, had been fistening to a description of, industrial organisation in the seventeenth century. This, it might be thought, was not a very exciting theme; but, the 'class khew well that they were approaching the origins of trade unionism. seAenftehro‘avptahuino seawqeuniet, t voice I cebtehgiannk: I can trace "We have a few principles at work." There was a little movement amongst the class, as if already they were scenting battle. "The first is," continued the quiet voice, "that whenever' one class of people has succeeded in its fight for freedom, it begins to destroy the freedom of others. This always has been so, and always will be-so."</p>
        <p>tr He proceeded to state his other principles, explaining them by reference to the seventeenth century, and finally declaring that the root of 411 our ills was private capital. Up jumped an eager man to prove, that not capital, but false ideals, had dislocated society; and that the collapse of industrial organisation in the seventeenth century v,-as due to the decay of religious principles. He was earnest, but not half so 'earnest as the next speaker, who, with the zeal of a prophet, declared. that the Goverment, as usual, had helped capital to crush labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <p>Here the tutor begged the class not merely to make statements; .but to produce arguments, and to consider the-relations of their subject to trade Unionism. The game was now in full swing. Most people contributed something to_ the discussion. From one man came an illus- tratiye fact from his own experience; _from another an intuitive flash of comprehension; from a woman a bit of common sense that had somehow been missed.; from the weaker mem- bers many questions that showed that they were beginning to understand. When at last it was ten o'clock, and time for the meeting to close, the discussion was adjourned to the street. There, in a freer atmosphere, ad under the influence of a delicate drizzle, a little knot of men were to be found at 10.15 p.m., thrashing out the question of individual rights against the State. This is roughly what happens once a week wherever a group of men and women has formed itself into a tutorial class. They may have chosen to study industrial history, or natural science, or literature, or psychology, or any other branch of knowledge. They may be a second or third year class taking economic theory or constitutional .history. 'They have at the outset pledged themselves for a three years' course. It may be thought that few people would ever be found to pledge them- selves for so long a period. Yet so far is this from the truth that in many centres classes have undertaken a fourth, and even a fifth year of study. There is, indeed, generally some discontent felt at the compulsory -three years' pletle, but by the end of the first winter those who seriously intend to study have got over their early doubts. Having once come into being, and having chosen its subject, a class meets once a week for two hours, over a period of twenty-four weeks. The first hour is taken up with the lecture, given by a tutor appointed by the local University and the W.E.A. In the subsequent dlscusston everyone is encouraged to criticise the lecture, to ask any number of questions, and to 'contribute his or her share of informa- tion or ideas. Each class is provided with a circulating library of its own, • consisting of books specially selected for the needs of the class. Essays are expected to be written on the various subjects discussed. Though it is real- ised that the ordinary working man has little time to spare for literary effort, the Board of Education insists on a certain amount of essay- writing, as only by formulating his own thoughts can a man ever grasp the true mean- ing of what he has heard or read.- As far as possible the tutor looks over each essay in com- pany with its author. This ensures consider- able private tuition and personal intercourse between the tutor . and individual students. Private tuition generally takes plaCe during the half-hour before the class opens. During these little meetings many individual difficulties are explained; and the tutor himself is better fitted to deal suitably with the whole class by learning to understand the individuals separ- i ately. -</p>
        <p>Such is the general mechanism ,of a tutorial class. Its object may be defined as serious study through intimate discussion: It aims not merely at the spread ,of knOwledge, but also at intellectual fellowship. Since the num- ber of students is limited to thirty-two there is no danger that a class will be unwieldy. Every member will have the personal attention .of the tutor. Every member will also have personal intellectual contact with every . other member. Men enter tutorial classes with many fixed ideas that will not stand a thorough investigation. The tutorial class will. not shake them from their deep conviotions if those convictions are the ke- sult of careful thought; but if they are mere prejudices the tutorial class will either. over- throw 'them or give them a secure founthltion. Only by intellectual intercourse with people of all shades of opinion Can a man feel the single- ness, of purpose that is really behind all our differences. He will be the staunch friend of his enemy, so long as his enemy is only wrong- minded, and not wrong-hearted.</p>
        <p>The University of the People. This,movement represents the effort of serious and zealous working men and women to lit themselves for their great task of re-making society. They are sitting down to study the whole complicated organism. They are already beginning to act on the .knowledge they have gained. An increasing number of labour lead- ers of one kind an another is being ,drawn from thp tutorial clarises.. There May come a time when some such education as this will be held essential in any leader of labour.</p>
        <p>It is not, however, merely for the provision of leaders of social movements that this system is intended. It is the University of • the Pe6ple. It affords them a high standard of intellectual training while allowing them to live at home. and to continue their bread-winning. It may never become a universal medium of education, but it is surely destined to spread. Already three thousand persons are studying in W.E.A. classes. They are no more than pibneers of a great popular renaissance in which every man and woman has a part to play. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>SILENT MATRIMONY.</head>
        <p>SILENT MATRIMONY.</p>
        <p>Married people do not talk to each other. If you question that, first look about you. Watch married couples in the restaurants when you get the chance, which will not be often. Watch them as they walk to and from church, as they drive about together; watch them in their homes. Have you ever, now, when you have entered suddenly a room in . which two 'mar- ried people were sitting a deux, found. them talking? The cleverest' man I know—he is'mar- ried to quite a cleverish little woman, and he married her in a romantic, an extremely un- conventional' way—said to me one day "Joan and I never talk; we have nothing to talk about." And he is a scintillating talker.—From " Olivia's Latchkey," by Hubert Bland..</p>
        <p>Dr. Atkin Swan, in a lecture at the London Salon of Photography, describing his experi- ence at the hospital of St. Bernard, said that a fact not generally realised was that the lives of the monks were greatly shortened Owing to the stress of the climate. Oil his recent visit he was introduced to the Oldest monk, who was thirty-seven. This was considered to .be record tige among them. The dogs numbered .01MT-six, and earl had a Stone cell of its Own, and WFIS named after a saint. One of his reeol- lections was Of St. Joseph and St. Christopher having it out over a bone.</p>
        <p>The Very New Reporter.</p>
        <p>The story of the very ,Dew reporter who took rather too much to, heart the general instruc- tions of his chief never to state anything as a fact which has not been proved to be one, is /given in the "Glasgow Herald." The young man's first duty was to "do 'something, about the Mayoress's reception this afternoon," and this is what he produced: "It is rumoured that a reception was given yesterday afternoon by the .Mayoress to a large number of reputed ladies of the city. It is alleged that the gener- ally accepted wife of our Worthy Mayor, the so-called grocer,' was attired in accordance with what in some quarters is assumed to be' fashionable. style."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>REASONS WHY YOU SHOULD – DRINK – • 9 4., -1•! ;.:::•:,,:fr. ,' ' ''' – • • Y 4,54%, ,*, _''i'it . *,11.F0) 411,4,rA‘ • at 4 ,t ,:11 li ::''. . ' ..., '' 'l.:':' „i„Vpdflr „.:Mr: ~• Eliza ' ~,,,, t, '•gil• 9., .0 ''..,;„ ',..i- – • ," • it</head>
        <p>REASONS WHY YOU SHOULD - DRINK - • 9 4., -1•! ;.:::•:,,:fr. ,' ' ''' - • • Y 4,54%, ,*, _''i'it . *,11.F0) 411,4,rA‘ • at 4 ,t ,:11 li ::''. . ' ..., '' 'l.:':' „i„Vpdflr „.:Mr: ~• Eliza ' ~,,,, t, '•gil• 9., .0 ''..,;„ ',..i- - • ," • it</p>
        <p>I It is the only Cocoa with the doliclous "Rowntres Flavour."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>It is always of the finest quality and does not vary._</head>
        <p>It is always of the finest quality and does not vary._</p>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN. OLD JOURNAUST.</p>
        <p>the People Called Quakers. Last Sunday evening's address at the Friends' Meeting House in Buttermarket-street somehow reminded me of an incident at a Coroner's in- quest which I once attenged—it must be upwards of 30 years ago. Among those sum- moned to serve on the jury was a tradesman whom I will call John Brown. Being a Quaker, he of course declined to be sworn with the rest, therefore it was necessary to administer the special form of affirmation provided for such ocCasions. "Repeat 'after me," said the Coroner; "'I, John Brown.' '' " I, John Brown.' " " Being one of. the people called Quakers,'" continued the Coroner. "Being a mdmber of the Society of Friends,' corrected the juryman. "No, no, that won't, do," said the Coroner; " I, John Brown, being one of the people called Quakers.'" And the juryinan had no alternative but obedience to the very letter. Drink's Ravages. - -</p>
        <p>At last wed-es meeting 'of the Runcorn Board of Guardians, the substitution of wood-chopping for oakum picking in the case of old male in- mates was decided upon as an experiment. I was glad of it, for I regarded the former as degrading as well as profitless. I have visited four prisons (now don't, please—each time, I was with a party armed ;with a Home, Office order), and I have watched oakum-picking there, so that I know something about it. And this brings me to justification of my headline. In almost every instance that carne under my notice the. prisoners engaged in picking oakum were in durance vile directly or indirectly through strong drink—and in no inconsiderable proportion of cases as the direct result of, their own excessive indulgence therein. Some years ago, while going through a Yorkshire Union Workhouse, I looked into a building where a number .of elderly' Men Were following the very occupation. which has just been resolved upon at Runcorn, and among the wood choppers I found one whom I had known as a distin- guished and prosperous architect. His infatua- tion for drink had ruined his professional career. lam personally acquainted with a clergyman who, through the like cause, has fallen as low as it is possible for mortal to get. It would be easy to multiply such illustrations, but there is no need; every one of my readers can do that for himself or herself without effort. I</p>
        <p>Temperance Sunday" I mention these things in view of Temperance Sunday. Nowadays, none need fear to own himself a total abstainer. The youthful mem- bers of the English Royal Family are brought up that way. Victor Emmanuel 111. of Italy, Alphonse XIII. of Spain, Gustave IV. of Sweden, and Ferdinand the First of Bulgaria are all abstainers. The King of Spain drinks only water at official banquets. The Royal Family of Sweden is at the head of the temperance reform movement, the Queen-mother having been associated with it for 40 years. Her son, Prince Oscar, has been for a long time the president of the Total Abstinence Society at Stockholm. In short, the 'once despised .cult is now quite fashionable; ,and no doubt as time goes on the average public-house as we know it will become intolerable. Even now the "Saturday Review" admits to-its Columns such criticism as this: "If some one with a mental endowment corresponding tn. our own were to descend from some other planet and look upon our English life with clear, open eyes, un- clouded by prejudice and undulled by custom, he would regard the public-house as .the most entirely ugly and hateful thing in England." Concerning Schoolmasters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <p>Mr. Sydney Walter Jeffery, whose promotion to the headship of the Wycliffe Council Schools was reported a , week ago, is to be congratu- lated on the due recognition of his talents and reward of his long and faithful service. I suppose we have all known deserving teachers who were not so fortunate on the recurrence of vacp,icies to which they had naturally aspired, there. being no question as to qualifleation ad character. I have in mind one such who had the mortification and humiliation of seeing his subordinates transferred in a body to a new school with a young fellow at their head, he himself being offered, and having no alternative but to accept, an ,assistant-mastership else- where. I think again of the very capable and clever assistant-master of a Secondary School who, although himself a candidate for the higher position, has twice had to submit to the appointment of a junior over his head. In these cases unworthy influences were at work; and Warrington may well be proud, as the recipient of the honour .now in question may be thankful, that no such injustice has marred the case of the Wycliffe SchoOls. My experi- ence is that such instances are not. too common.</p>
        <p>Shakespearean Society. The quotation- heading the account .of the formation of this new , local society reminded me of an ingenious beggar who made use of the fact that, of the players on the world's stage, some play ill and some well, to explain his own failure, the implication being. that he was fairly entitled to pecuniary aid. at the hands of the luckier or more gifted of his fellows. Well, all good wishes in relation to the War- rington Shakespearean Society. May it be spared the fate which has overtaken one or two similar, organisations of which I had inti- mate know edge. For a while these "did rim well," but were eventually hindered" by a handful of conceited members who always would have the best parts or mine, and yet would not put in the required, attendances at rehearsals. As we -recently saw at the opening performance of "Miss Hook of Holland," when an understudy gets 'a chance, it sometimes leads to the discovery of superior talents previously Unsuspected. I take it, however, that the new society Will have plenty of educational Work to do over and above the production of plays. .</p>
        <p>Nonplussed. The methods of the gamblers who evaded the Stockton Heath police by playing " banker " on the Warrington side of the canal, recalls a story which John Hartley, the Yorkshire dialect writer, used to tell. It was to the effect that a vigilant constable surprised two young men playing dominoes for stakes in similar circum- stances to the above. They were so absorbed in their game that the officer got close behind them unnoticed. "Con ta come, Tom?" said one to the other, whereupon the policeman interposed with "I can come." The previous speaker unhesitatingly jumped into the canal, and, swimming to the opposite bank, sat down and called to the man izz blue, "Con ta coz,ne, Bobby?" It must be more than 40 years since I heard Mr. Hartley, who was a friend of ours, narrate this., He now resides in Cheshire, and has just celebrated his 75th birthday.</p>
        <p>Betting and Gambling. There was a good deal on this subject in the last issue of "The Examiner." Many readers will remember the Tranby Croft baccarat case, in which the late King Edward, as Prince of Wales, was in a sense concerned, being ope of the guests at the time. Respecting it, he sent a private letter to the Archbishop of Canterbury (Dr. Benson), in which he said: "I have a horror of gambling, and should always do my utmost to discourage others who have an 'in- clinhtion for it, •as I consider that gambling, likd intemperance, is one of the greatest curses that a country can be afflicted with: Horse racing may 'produce gambling, or it may not, but I have always looked uPon it as a manly sport, which is popular with Englishmen of all classes, and there is no reason why it should</p>
        <p>( Continued in preceding column).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 15, 1913. TABLE TALK.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 15, 1913. TABLE TALK.</p>
        <p>The World's Tribute of Interesting Items of News.</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AMUSING.</p>
        <p>The Perth (Western Australia) Legislative Assembly have added a new clause to the Criminal Code, Bill, providing that any person ,or corporation prohibiting under threat of dis- missal the marrying of an employe over 21 years of age is liable to a fine of £5OO or three months! imprisonment.</p>
        <p>Fighting Against Worry.</p>
        <p>"The most effective moment for making re- solves is in the comatose calm which precedes going to sleep. .. . . If 'regularly at that moment the man says ,to himself, 'I will not allow my business to Pre-occupy me at home. • • .' he will be,astonished at the results.'— "The Plain Man and his Wife," by Arnold Bennett.</p>
        <p>Miners' Superstitions.</p>
        <p>As men who have to risk their lives daily, it IS not surprising that miners hold many super- stitions. One widespread belief is that to in- troduce a rabbit into a mine is to court dis- aster; and many stories are current of catas- trophes heralded by the appearance of a white rabbit to the men who were doomed to die. He would be a bold miner, too, who would whistle at his Work, for whistling is a direct invitation of disaster; and though miners are cleanly folk, very few ever wash the .small of their backs lest the root should fall on them..</p>
        <p>Tunnel Disasters.</p>
        <p>Some of the most serious accidents in the his- tory of English railways have, like the colli- sion at Liverpool, taken place in a tunnel, but disasters of the kind have been uncommon in recent years. The last of any note was in the Catesby Tunnel, on the Great Central railway,' but happily no lives welt • lost. Although the vehicles of an express train left the rails when travelling at about sixty miles an hour, they only just scraped the tunnel sides. Not even a single case of serious injury was reported.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>Emigration and Marriage.</head>
        <p>Emigration and Marriage.</p>
        <p>Perhaps in consequence of tile fact that so many female " assisted " emigrants to Western Australia marry within a 'year or two of their arrival, the Minister-in-Charge of the Immigra- tion Department has been obliged to announce that his department is not a Matrimonial agency. He has made this annoufirement_ in a statement to the Australian 'Press, in which he says that his officials are continually being in- undated by letters from young farmers de- sirous .of entering the .state of matrimony. In some instances, the Minister even receives a personal request to select a wife for the appli- cant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>Monks Who Die young.</head>
        <p>Monks Who Die young.</p>
        <p>"Growing Pains."</p>
        <p>Beware of "growing pains." Lecturing in Manchester recently, Dr. A. E. Cunliffe said that "growing pains" are not what the anxious parent usually supposes them to be. They are really a form of rheumatism, and neglected rheumatism is one of the most fertile Causes of the breakdown of the heart and its ultimate re- fusal to go on working. "Therefore," said Mr. Cunliffe, "treat every case of growing pains, or joint pains—or any form of rheuma- tism in children—as a disease of the very grav- est importance. There are no such things as growing pains. They mean rheumatism, and rheumatism means the necessity for the doctor."</p>
        <p>The Longest Bridge in the World.</p>
        <p>It seems probable that next year a commence- ment will be made with the construction of the railway bridge'. between Rugen, in Germany, and the mainland, which will be the longest in the world, exceeding even that over Hohangho, with its 3,580 yards. The cost of this great en- gineering work is, however, not expected to amount to more than £1,000,000, or less than a third of that of the Forth Bridge. When the bridge, which, it is said, is to include a track for'pedestrians, though none for road traffic, is completed, it will substantially shorten the journeys between Berlin and .Hamburg on the one hand, and Stockholm and Christiania on the other.</p>
        <p>Oil on Troubled Waters.</p>
        <p>"Pouring oil on troubled waters" is one of the many, metaphors which we have derived from the life of the sea. Oddly 'enough, al- though this device was known to Aristotle and practised by Pliny, it fell into complete disuse for hundreds of Years. It became "practical politics" again some thirty years ago, when the Admiralty prescribed the best means of using oil for calming the waves, but, appar- ently, was forgotten again until Captain Barr summoned the Narragansett to his aid, and thus saved the majdrity of the passengers in the burning Volturno. Professor Bay Lan- kester, writing in the "Daily Telegraph," in- forms us that so small a quantity as a pint an hour is sufficient to secure a zone of calm water round a ship—sufficient space for boats to be launched in with safety.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>War Against Field Rats.</head>
        <p>War Against Field Rats.</p>
        <p>Kaltern, near Bozen, in the Austrian Tyrol, a village credited with producing the finest grapes and the best Tyrolean wine, has re- cently shared the fate of other districts of a 'less elevated situation (Kaltern itself lives over 1,000 feet above the sea level) in suffering from an invasion of field rats. ,The burghers, grow- ing alarmed, called to their aid the modern Pied Piper of Hamlyn, Dr. Danysz, of the Pas- teur Institute, Paris, whoso beneficent work has ,frequently saved French agriculturists in similar circumstances from serious loss. Dur- ing the last week or so, several tons of crushed oat S have been impregnated with Danysz Virus, and the bait distributed over the infested fields, ' and vineyards. The operations have proved re- markably successful, and great relief is felt that {he harvest and vintage have been saved from 'lie havoc with which they were threatened.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER. Conducted by UNCLE JACK. Love,,,wo.tp, 0 0 0 0 0 0</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER. Conducted by UNCLE JACK. Love,,,wo.tp, 0 0 0 0 0 0</p>
        <p>ARE YOU GOING TO ENTER?</p>
        <p>• My Dear Children,— Have you taken yourselves by the hand, as the funny man in the pantomime said, led yourselves into a corner, put on your thinking cap, and thought? If you .have done so you will have made up your minds, and what you will have made up your minds about is to enter for our great Christmas Competition. I -want a large entry this time, and I want every nephew and niece to send me a letter. The subject is an interesting one. All you have to do is to write an imaginary letter to Santa Claus, and to tell him, just in your, own way, what you Would like him to bring you, wfite about- what you would dike and tell why YOU would like it. Then send the letters on to me at 'The Examiner" Office, Warrington. The competition will not close until the 6th December, bd I hope to see a large number of letters. I was telling the printer- man that .1 might require two columns to hold your letter. He smiled and said we could have three i need be. I do- believe he would like to enicr for our great competition himself, but You see I know his age and will not let him. It is. only my nephccw's and nieees,who are to 'have that chance. Nov:, then, let us makes a fine show. More new members are joining every week, and below I give a list of our latest additions. I am sure we welcome them most heartily. Your loving UNCLE JACK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>NEW MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>Dear lipele ill you please enrol me a member of your . corner? . MA BY 13Eac1i.-AL, 27, Mill ED- STH EET, STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Jack,—Will you lease enter my name in y'our big book as a member of the Children's Corner? `,</p>
        <p>MADGE DODD, 3, CHAPEL-COURT, JOHN-STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>' Dear Uncle Jack,—Will you please put me down in your book as one of your nephews? GEORGE PERCIVAL, 27, ALFRED-STRELIf, STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Jack,—Please put my name in your big book as your nephew. JAMES DRURY, 79, CHORLEY-STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Jack,—Please enrol me a new member. •</p>
        <p>FREDERICK JAMES BELLAMY, 178, WILDERSPOOL-CAUSEWAY.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Jack,—Please enrol me in your big book.</p>
        <p>FRED SHELLEY, 85, WINWICK-ROAD, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>You are-duly entered members, and I hope to hear often from you. I hope you will take part in our competition.—U.J.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <p>A BASKET OF FISH. Here is a new puzzle for you. Can you solve it? The answer will be given next week : I caught a sunbeam; a portion of a shoe; a young girl; humouring ; a cooking apple; toll-gate from the king's highway; a past tense of an act of one of the senses; an insect on a streamlet ; a; verb that disagrees; a crooked 'letter listening; the signet of the seas; a shoe for icy weather'; some limewash for your ceil- ing; a bird's seat; a woman's jewel; a letter on a card; a tumble.</p>
        <p>THE PATCHWORK QUILT. I have a quilt upon my bed Of patchwork done in white and red, With stars of blue set in beside, It's very big and long and wide, Yet grandma made it, I 'am told, When she was only ten years old, And now its countless stitches show How hard she toiled, SO long ago. She never knew a lazy day, And when I waste my time or play Too long, I stop and think how she Was always busy as could be.</p>
        <p>THE WAYSIDE INNS. When autumn goes and cold begins, Guests flock to all the wayside inns. While clouds hang low and snowflakes fly, They hold their dainty revelry. The flowers are gone that summer knew, Long since the summer's songsters flew, But_still remain .the sturdy weeds That know the little sparrows' needs.</p>
        <p>Though sapless stalks are brown and dry, The weeds still bear their feasts on high; The goldfinch here may eat his fill, The tom-tit lunch on seeds at will. We never praised, in brighter hours, Their sober hues among the flowers. What need have they of human words Who bring such joy to all the birds?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>CHOCOLATE 400 YEARS OLD.</head>
        <p>CHOCOLATE 400 YEARS OLD.</p>
        <p>You all love chocolate, and perhaps my nephews and nieces, like most folk, think this is quite a new thing in England. It is not so, for it was introduced 400 years ago,, and although you are not 'celebrating it, this is the fourth centenary of its introduction. The earliest known use of chocolate or " chocolatl,", as it was called, was by the Aztecs, who Made it .as a beverage; It was also a form of -currency, and an annual tax was twenty chests of chocolate. A writer of the period referred to it as "blessed money, which exempts its possessor from avarice, because it cannot be hoarded nor can it be hidden under- ground." .</p>
        <p>- Chocolate seems to have come into fashion, in this country in the seventeenth century, and among the earliest places to popularise it was ",White's," in the Haymarket, which became known as the Chocolate House. Chocolate speedily became a favourite beverage, and before long the morning cup of chocolate grew to be a fashionable institution. The founda- tion of merchandise with chocolate- and cocoa in England was due to a far-seeing young Quaker doctor, who in 1728, at his shop in \Vine-street; Bristol, began a trade in "choco- late, nibs and cocoa." • The business flourished exceedingly, and when its _founder, Joseph Fry, died in 1787, the great house of Fry was well established An what for those days were commodious prem- ises in Bristol.</p>
        <p>But prior to this, in 1659, a French mer- chant named David Challon, obtained the monopoly for the sale of the new product in Franceoand rapidly amassed a fortune. How popular it became may be gathered from the fact that Linnaeus described it as The "food of the gods," and waxed enthusiastic about its merits.</p>
        <p>This book has come at the .time who we hear so much of "Feminism," and those who are its chief supporters would do well to peruse, ." The Vocation of Woman," for it is I only in seeing and understanding the opposite I view that one can uphold one's own ideas. Although we do not entirely agree with the writer in many instances, we are certainly in favour, of a broad-minded tolerance, and those who read the book will be able to judge for themselves. The book goes over much ground in its 340 pages, and the writer has certainly tried to deewith the much-discussed question of woma • 'position nowadays in every aspect. The chapter on "The 15110 Vocation" is really beautiful, and what is of more value; it is expressed in quite a matter of fact and common-sense -manner. Her remarks are espe- cially true with regard to modern education: " . . . our modern girls are given an edu- cation which has little if any connection with home life and no relation to the tasks and duties connected with 'motherhood." She fin- ishes the chapter by saying a feeling of help- lessness comes over her as she pens the words, how many girls there are now who seem to think that to do "servant's work" suggests an insult to one's intelligence, and cofisequently a sacrifice ,of their "independence." Certainly, if the education of woman. has tended to this state of affairs, it is time that everyone under- stood," there is nothing intrinsically finer about one kind of work, than .about another." We heartily endorse the writer's ideas that the woman who can cook a meal that is good is a veritable "master of art," and produces -Qs much a chef d'ceuvre as• does a painter of a masterpiece. We•hope the work will act as an incentive to many of the modern type of women, who consider housework an indignity to their sex, and who are concerned only in the glory of the title of "business woman." In the course of the book, the writer suggepts new employment for women, one that has no legal barrier--women architects. Truly! for that we owe her our thanks. "Every woman is aware of a hundred imperfections in every house she inhabits, yet they are repeated again and again . . . but' the house of the future, which can be daintily and economically run by one educated woman, needs to be built by another."</p>
        <p>The writer's ideas on the modern training of girls in our elementary and secondary schools are worthy of consideration, and so are also the ideas of sport and the strenuous effort of the competitive system of -education on the human frame and its ..(in many cases) far-reach- ing and evil results. On the whole, the book is very fair and some of the ideas are in the highest way appreciable. The book maintains Messrs. Niacmillan's standard for sending out models of good workmanship.</p>
      </div>
