      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>WORLD OF LETTERS.</head>
        <p>THE WORLD OF LETTERS.</p>
        <p>New Books.</p>
        <p>The Theosophie Heresy.</p>
        <p>By W. Lancelot Holland, with an Appendix by J. N. Maskelyne. London: G. Rout- ledge and Sons. qd.</p>
        <p>The object of this book is an attempt to expose the supposed heretical philosophy which Theosophy offers to the world, and In our opinion the object is by no means achieved. It would seem that one of the best ways to expose any particular system or theory after thor- oughly understanding one's subject is to be less prejudiced in making comparisons. In one, part we read: "It cannot be too clearly stated that there is all the difference in the world between the Christianity of Christendom and the Christianity of the Christ and His Apostles." Is it not possible that there can be a difference between the philosophy of Theosophy and the -philosophy of Theosophists? The writer seems to have an especial dislike of Theosophy because- it has "a peculiar fascina- tion over the minds Of women." Have we ever had a census of regular Churchgoers? Mr. Lancelot Holland certainly makes us under- stand that when he does not want to see any- thing he won't see it. "The Theosophic Heresy" is a pyrotechnic exhibition of personal distressed exclamations.</p>
        <p>The Vocation of Woman. By Mrs. Archibald Colquhoun. London: Macmillan and Co., Ltd. 4s. 6d net.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>STRONGER LIFE WITHOUT MICROBES.</head>
        <p>STRONGER LIFE WITHOUT MICROBES.</p>
        <p>The possibility of animal life under conditions quite free from microbes is occupying the atten- tion of experimenters, and the Paris scientist Guyenot uses the common fly ,for this purpose (Drosophila ampelphila), obtaining their repro- duction and following their descendants. He now possesses a collection of specimens dating from two years past and comprising a great number of generations. Development from the egg to the imago, at 24 degree Cent., lasts for twelve days, and the females commence to lay on the first and second days after hatching. In this way he obtains two generations a month, and each generation contains at least 10,000 flies. The line followed by_hint since May, 1911, includes some 40 generations, or 400,000 speci- mens. Under these conditions, living in a medium free from micrObes and feeding upon specially prepared food, the specimens do not show any lessening in strength of' fecundity, but, on the contrary, their, development takes place under exceptional conditions which are rPot found in nature. While in the ordinary case the mortality during development is often great for larvre and pup, it is practically nothing in the present case.'</p>
        <p>Captain Bull, the Adjutant of the sth East Lancashire Regiment, at Burnley, is shortly leaving the town to resume his regimental duties. His successor is Captain W. M. Acton, of the 18th Royal Irish Regiment.</p>
        <p>"A Journalist" gives glimpses of life in Fleet- street ,030 or 40 years ago. The following pas- sages refer to O'Donovan and Charlie Williams, two well-known war correspondents. He (O'Donovan) lodged at a public-house in Hol- born kept by a fellow-countryman of his named' Peter Cowell. This house was at the time known to the police in connection with the v.isits of Irish patriots of the physical force party in national polities. It was the resort of the scattered remnants of a disintegrated Fenianism. Cowell revered his strange guest, and when custoiners heard the sounds of revol- ver practice in the upper part of the house you may, be sure that he did not give his patrons the true explanation of. the noise. The fact was that O'Donovan, in bed at midday, had grown greatly annoyed at the crude art evinced in the engravings that Cowell had hung upon his was, and that he was engaged in shooting' these masterpieces to smithereens. This revol- ver practice in his bedroom only ceased whe-n there was nothing breakable left- to fire at. "Glory be to God!" said Peter Cowell, in relat- ing the circumstances to a correspondent, "there's not a pictur' nor a frame nor a utin- shill of anny kyoind that Misther O'Donovan hasn't bruk an' ped for !" "Charlie Williams could have given Baron Munchausen a stone and a beating. He spbke with a rasping North of Ireland accent, and 'his campaign anecdotes gained greatly by the stolid, matter-of-fact manner in which they were narrated. I recall now one of his cam- paign reminiscences. It is a quafiat experience of 'a' correspondent under fire. I had got under cover of a big boulder, and had tethered my horse beside me. I was just munchin' 4 beskit, when a shell burst on the_rock an' shot the nosebag right off my charger. He had sho-ved his damned ould head out of cover.' 'And you?' asked Pearse. 'I just went on munchin' my beskit.' 'But,' suggested Dun- ning, 'if the shell took away the nosebag it ought to have carried away the beast's head as well.' 'lt did!' replied Williams, with the ut- most sangfroid."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>Literary Gleanings.</head>
        <p>Literary Gleanings.</p>
        <p>TWO -WAR CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>KING EDWARD STORIES.</head>
        <p>KING EDWARD STORIES.</p>
        <p>Edward Legge in "More About King Edward," tells some good stories about the late King. When his Majesty was Prince of Wales, Mr. Louis Sterne, an engineering inventor, had the honour of explaining to the Prince exhibits at Kensington and the Crystal Palace. "The next time I met the Prince," says Mr. Sterne, "I was crossing PicCadilly, at the top of SL James's-street, before the days of refuges. In trying to escape being run down I ran directly- into the arms of his Royal Highness. He at once recognised me, and said: "Mr. Sterne, you should give this traffic time, and never cross the bows." I expressed surprise that he should recognise me, to which he replied that he "rmVer forgot either face or name if associated with any matter of interest." Mr. Sterne ginet tpe Prince again at a picture exhibition, where a portrait of the late eminent -statesman, Mr.‘W. I-I. Smith, was hung. Turning to the Princess, his ,Royal Highness said, " Hullo ! therers Smith." At once a man standing near turned round and acknowledged the recognition, offer- ing his hand, which the Prince unhesitatingly accepted. When this man., whose name hap- pened to be Smith, recognised the Prince, be became embarrassed . . . and with the lady who accompanied him*made his exit as soon as possible."</p>
        <p>An interesting glimpse of the family life at Sandringham is given in the following Para- graph:—</p>
        <p>"One. custom always observed at Sandring- ham on the eve of the New Year waS that of the Virst-footing. King- Edward instituted the ptactice every year of clearing the house of every adult staying there, servants and all, at five minutes to twelve, and was himself the first to open' the.main door after midnight had strfick and to enter the place, leading ,Queen. Alexandra by the hand. The only time he was forestalled was at the close of 1909, when a younger member of the Royal Family dashed round to another entrance and threw the great door open on the stroke Of midnight.. 'We shall have some very bad luck this year,' re- marked the King gravely, and it was a curious coincidence that his Majesty died four months afte! wards."</p>
        <p>The King was not offended when he got the worst of any conversatiopal incident. On one occasion 'a lady, whom , the King highly es-• teemed for her good works, scored , neatly off him. . "She had just been presented to him, and was somewhat nervous. To put her at ease, his Majesty said, 'Oh, Miss ,, I want to have a long dud -wittrytni; but if I should unff,i-tu- nateiY '6Ore you pray tell me so.' The King, who was an adroit cross-examiner, wished to ascertain the yOung lady's age, which she bad no intention of divulging. You have already, said you were born at -,' said the Monarch. May I ask in what year?' 'You bore me, sir- was the smiling reply, and his Majesty, taking the checkmate in: the greatest good humour, diverted the conversation into a,less embarrass- ing, channel.". '</p>
        <p>When taking part in a boar hunt during a visit to Austria-Hungary, the King showed his native kindness in the following incident: After the sport, - a basket of provisions, brought with them by the two Princes,. was opened in the presence of several villagers. Among them was a young girl, looking very cold and pinched. Our Prince noticed her wan figure, and before taking bite or sup himself, gave her some sherry from his flask and a handful of his sandwiches. Then, refilling the cup, he drank from it without rinsing it out."</p>
