        <p>BOY SCOUTS' NEW COLOURS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. J. Crosfield and the Movement.</p>
        <p>At the invitation of Mr. J. J. Crosfield the 2nd Warrington Troop of Boy Scouts, Crosfield's Own, visited " Netherdale," Frodsham, on Saturday, for the purpose of holding a cere- monial rally. The troop is raised entirely among employes of Messrs. Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., and 100 Scouts, under the command of Scoutmaster IL A. Seymour-Jones, assisted by Assistant Scoutmasters J. Young, B. Garner, and Quartermaster W. Percy Taylor, marched from Frodsham Station. to Mr. Crosfield's resi- dence,, accompanied by the band and drums, which played suitable marches. The boys were inspected by Mr. and' Mrs. J. J. Crosfield, who were supported by Mr. Holman Kingdon, Captain A. B. Layton, and Mi. C. J. Newton, secretary of the Warrington District Boy Scouts Association. The troop was then drawn up in inipi formation and the colours were presented with all due ceremony by Mrs. Crosfield. A Scout display followed, including the general salute, the intricate maze, the cele- brated lEngonyam# song, • and the Zulu cheer. The' troop finally 'marched past in column of patrols, headed by their colours. Mr. J. J. Crosfield, in addressing the boys, re- gretted there had been no Scout movement when he was a lad. He knew of no other organisation, which was so calculated to form the character and broaden the mind. The enthusiastic recep- tion accorded to the movement throughout the world testified to a universal recognition of this fact, and but a few days previously a Swedish gentleman had told him of the great progress of the Scout movement in his country.</p>
        <p>Although he had never had an opportunity of becoming a member of their organisation, he, nevertheless, carried the Scouts' badge of friend- ship, the Swastika, which he hoped would com- mand the assistance of the Boy Scouts if ever he should need it. He advised them all to act up to the letter and the spirit of their, promise to fear God and honour the King, to help other people at all times, and to obey the Scout law. After saluting the inspecting paity, and sing- ing the National Anthem with . great fervour, the troop marched off to partake of a hearty tea, at which they were the guests of Mr. and Crosfield. On the return to Warrington a route march took place through the principal streets of the town.</p>
