      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>NEW MAYOR OF WIDNES.</head>
        <p>NEW MAYOR OF WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Widnes Town Council on Monday Councillor D. Lewis was unani- mously elected Mayor. • Mr. Lewis said, in a short review of his life, that at thirteen years of age he was left father- less,, with six brothers and sis4erB, for whom it had been impossible to make any provision. He owed all to f-he devotion, example and inspira- tion of hi 3 mother, who, with sublime courage, had sacrificed her all for the well-being of her children. During his Mayoralty he would like to see the establishment of a Guild of Health. A long-felt want would be supplied if an insti- tution of this kind was established. The Cor- poration had now readied its majority, and in twenty-one years the rates had gone up from 4s. Bd. to 7s. 6d., but the expenditure had been wise and necessary. Taking a list of 132 towns, only 27 were lower in the matter of rates than Widnes.</p>
        <p>Messrs. S. Neil, G. I. Neil and S. Owen were re-elected aldermen.</p>
      </div>
