      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 15, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 15, 1913.</p>
        <p>ALDERMEN AS "DICTATORS."</p>
        <p>Lively Discussion in the Warrington Town</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>Council.</head>
        <p>Council.</p>
        <p>COMPLAINTS OF THE COUNCILLORS.</p>
        <p>Do the Aldermen in the Warrington Council play the part of dictators? It was alleged at the annual meeting held on Monday that they did, and that the common Councillors never got a chance of promotion. The debate became acrimonious at times. The Mayor, Councillor Peacock, presided. The discussion was raised by Councillor Pem- berton, who desired ,to have a Standing Order altered so that every clause could, in any minute of a committee, be moved back indivi- dually, if it was necessary. Councillor PEMBERTON thought they should have a new Standing Order altogether about the subject, as the existing one did not meet his desire. The MAYOR sugge4ted that Councillor Pember- ton should bring, the matter before the General Purposes Committee. Councillor PEAIBERTON answered that he would bring the matter then and there before the Council. There was much misunderstanding as to the meaning of the Standing Order. He thought every Councillor should be able to ") It should be S. move every clause of minutes back if he felt so disposed. ("No, no. In some cases the Town Clerk had ruled members out of order. The MAYOR: The Town Clerk does not rule any member out of order in this Council Cham- ber. (Hear, _hear.)</p>
        <p>The Councillors did not get a fair share on the committees. On the Watch Committee they had seven Aldermen and five Councillors, and other committees were much similar.</p>
        <p>We Councillors, he added, are chained itnd manacled in this Council. Our work has be- come a farce in a great many things. We have here Councillors, selected by the people of War- rington, who can't get a footing on any of these committees, who can't get their nose in at all. (Laughter.) It is not right to the ratepayers; it's a poOtive insult to them. Why should the Councillors representing the ratepayers not have a right of representation on the committees?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>"Made One Look Foolish."</head>
        <p>"Made One Look Foolish."</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON retorted that he had been so ruled out of order. He wanted the meaning of the Standing Order definitely settled so that there should be no question about the matter in the future. To be ruled out of order, as had happenedlo him, made one look foolish to the public. He could not see any objection to the Standing Order being made absolutely definite. It had been said that a member could speak on a minute as often as he liked. It was not so. The mover of a minute could only speak once;and therefore no other member could speak more than once. The argument against his proposition was, if it was adopted the Council would be kept all night. "If it is necessary," added the Coun- cillor, "in the interests of Warrington, I should be pleased to speak all night." (A Voice: "No doubt," and loud laughter.) His proposition was that each member should be able to move an amendment to each clause in the minutes of any committee. It had been disputed that a member had such power. He moved that the Standing Order be altered to permit that.</p>
        <p>Of No Advantage? Alderman JAMES Evarrs did not see the advan- tage of making any alteration. If the existing Standing Order was adhered to pooh member could move an amendment, and when it was disposed of he could move another. The MAYOR pointed out that no member could move two amendments on the same minute. Alderman JAMES EVANS asked if it was thc Mayor's ruling that two amendments from the same member upon one minute would be out of order. The MAYOR replied that it would be out of order. opposed to an stronglycould keep them N there for a week, and they would have no power to stop him talking. Councillor Pemberton Ayldaeltreinraatnion T as smN one was member alone might be able to keep them there for six weeks. (Laughter.) The obstructionists could keep the Council as long as they liked, and could talk everyone else to death. Surely there were enough members in the Council to move amendments when matters were important. Why one man should have the right to move as many amendments as he liked he (the Alder- man) failed to see.</p>
        <p>Discussed £5; Passed Thousands. If you want to make the life of the Council unbearable pass this resolution of Mr. Pember- ton, said the Alderman. Then you put it in the power of one or two members to waste all the time of the Council. I have known instances in this Council Chamber when we have discussed over a few pounds for one and a half hours, and thousands have been passed because the men were so anxious to make points over .t 5 that they have missed the question of the thousandg. The business of this Council should be done at the committee meetings, where you have ample opportunity to discuss the question, and then why should you put it into the power of one or two members to keep us in the Council listening to their discussions? Councillor PEMBERTON : I did not move that a member can move as many amendments as he likes. What I want is that a member should have the right to move a new amendment on each new minute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>"An Abominable Shame."</head>
        <p>"An Abominable Shame."</p>
        <p>Councillor -6-0- UGH thought Councillor Pember- ton's proposition reasonable. It is an abomin- able shame, he added, that we, as Councillors, should be limited and only allowed to speak on one minute. I do not think any member who seek's to talk wants to waste the time of the Council. I do not think even Mr. Pemberton, who talks a lot, wants to keep us a long time. (Laughter.) What we are here for is reform; we want to carry out several reforms and economic government and it is in the interests of the ratepayers, as well as the Council, that we should do so. (Hear, hear.) Alderman Bourox thought the Standing Order was quite satisfactory. .He objected to members who wanted to discus every matter in the Council so that they could fill the newspapers with their speeches. (Laughter.) Most of the questions should be discussed in the com- mittees. Councillor GOUGH : Qne reason is that we are not on the committees. • Councillor ROBERTS supported the Standing Order, but did not agree with the •interpreta- tion given by Alderman Tinnion. It was per- fectly competent for any member to move an amendment to a minute, and, on being defeated, to move a further amendment. • The MAYOR said he had ruled pgainst that, and on a vote being taken Counelllor Pember- ton only got four other supporters, and his scheme was defeated.</p>
        <p>".Chained and Manacled." Councillor PEMBERTON raised the question .of another Standing Order, which said that no member should be appointed chairman of more than two committees. They had, he said, just ,re-elected Aldermen for six years, and no other Councillor was able to get a chance of becoming a chairman or deputy-chairman of a committee. Those Aldermen went on with their chairman- Ships just like a running river. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>ST. HELENS' TITLED MAYOR.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS' TITLED MAYOR.</p>
        <p>Despite protests from the Labour members of the St. Helens Corporation against the method of selecting a Mayor, Sir David Gamblw was unanimously elected MayOr on Monday. Sir David's grandfather. was the first Mayor of the borough. Sir David Gamble, in reply, said he could only say that while he occupied that office he would do as his father and grandfather had done ,before him--his very best for the interests of the town in which he was born and in which he had lived, all his life. (Applause.s There was one stipulation which he would join' the ex-Mayor in making, and that was that he and the Mayoress could not' undertake to open bazaars and sales of work. They were so num- trous in St. Helens that he hoped the Mayoress and himself would be excused.</p>
        <p>. The voting for the election of five aldermen Isesulted in the following being re-elected : Alessrs. C. J. Bishop, F. R. Dixon-Nuttall, A. J. Foote, H. Martin, and W. W. Pilkington. Mr. Peter Phythian received seven votes from the Labour party, but was not elected.</p>
        <p>A five-year-old child named Samuel Horton, of Marsh-street, Oldham, was killed through being run over by a cart.</p>
        <p>In commemoration of the King's visit last July school children in pury were given an extra day's holiday on Monday.</p>
        <p>A body thought to he that of R. Frankland (20), an insurance agent, of Nicholson-street,. Helton Downs, was recovered. from the River Wear at Sunderland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>Councillors versus Aldermen.</head>
        <p>Councillors versus Aldermen.</p>
        <p>Each Councillor has more right to be on the committees than have Aldermen. Some of you who have got to be Aldermen would not have been elected to the Council if you had to go to the public for the vote. Not only do the Alder- men occupy the chairs of the committees, but they are dictators to the committees. It is time this was broken down if Councillors are to gain experience of the duties of the chairmen. On the same -ground we Might vote the Mayor into office for his lifetime. It would be quite as logical. It is perfectly ridiculous the way the business of the Corporation is carried on. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Councillor GOUGH said the Council proceedings had become almost a farce. Why should a Councillor have to depend upon an Alderman to get him into a committee.</p>
        <p>- The MAYOR.: It is the chairmen of the com- mittee we are discussing. Please keep to the point.</p>
        <p>- Councillor GOUGH thought the Mayor should extend to . him the same courtesy extended to Councillor Pemberton.</p>
        <p>Tail-end Dictating.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON objected to what had been said, and he strongly took objection to the tail- end dictating to the chairmen of- committees. Dr. JOSEPH was sorry to say anything unplea- sant, but everyone knew he was against multiple chairmanships. Personally, he would not accept a chairmanship, but he thought that one man should not have the chairmanship of two of the most important committees, and thus prevent another man who might be ambitious for pro- motion getting one of the positions. He thought it would be good for the Council, and in the ' interest of the town, to encourage such ambi- tions. They; all came there to work, and one of the rewards of work was promotion. Councillor LINDLEY objected to the Aldermen having so many chairmanships. Alderman Minion; It is due to their experi- ence and ability. Councillor LINDLEY : I do not think they have any more claim to ability than the rest of us. I think there is no chance for promotion. Councillor LASHPORD : No one can have a chance with the Aldermen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <head>"Painful to Listen."</head>
        <p>"Painful to Listen."</p>
        <p>• Councillor HENSHALL said there were 18 com- mittees. (A Voice: "20.") He would give them 20, and there were 36 members, and they had members who were chairmen of more than one committee, therefore it was' not unreason- able that the offices should be better distributed. Aldeiman SMETHURST said it was painful to listen to Some of the remarks. He could name committees where Councillors occupy the chairs and could not attend the meetings of the com- mittees. He had been proposed as a chairman for the last time, and after what had been said he would not occupy the position of chairman of the Electricity and Tramways Committee.</p>
        <p>Councillor JENiaNs said he had never discov- ered any enormous brain powers in the senior members that they should get preferences. He was an old man, but he. was not an old fogie. (Laughter.) He agreed the younger men should be given a chance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>Resented the Attack.</head>
        <p>Resented the Attack.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said that Councillor Jenkins had said every. Councillor should have an equal chance. Every man had an equal chance on the committees.</p>
        <p>Councillor LOCKER said while not failing in respect to the Aldermen the CounCillors claimed that they had equal ability for the position of chairman, and if it was laid down by the Stand- ing Order that one member should only have one chairmanship they would know who were not eligible for the positions. Alderman TINNION said if it was only proposed to have a different chairman for each committee he would support it, but he did- resent that attack upon the' Aldermen. (Councillor Pem- berton: "Of course, you do.") He was pre- pared to fight any such attacks fair and square, but would never go behind people's back to say what he thought of them. (Hear, hear.) Someone 'had said that there were seven Aldermen on a committee and others never got a chance. He denied that. The Aldermen had tho interests of the town as much at heart as anyone, and there had unnecessarily been a good deal of mud thrown at them. Councillor PEMBERTON said to make the matter clear his -amendment was that no one hold More than one chairmanship. Councillor ROBERTS thought there was nothing to be gained by supporting the•amendment. On a vote, Councillor Pemberton's amendment was carried, the Aldermen and Councillor )14:iberts alone voting against it.</p>
        <p>An Exciting Episode. On the Council being asked to appoint the. selected committees, Councillor PEMBERTON said. that on the Watch Committee there were seven Aldermen and only four common Councillors. On the Cemetery Committee there was only one Alderman. The Aldermen, should, he thought, be on that committee. (Loud laughter.)' It is most. unfair, continued Councillor Pem- berton, the way Aldermen think that they ,ought to rule the whole roost. (Laughter.) I resent it very much. The 'INIA•koR, : If you leave the matter till the next meeting you might get some of the Alder- nien to resign. •</p>
        <p>• Councillor PEMBERTON : I, think they ought all to resign. (Laughter.) Councillor GOUGH : I move that Councillor Lindley be put on the Watch Committee instead of Alderman Bolton, who has been on a long time.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON .angrily): If I am not a better man than you it is time I left the Coun- cil. _ (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>The committees were then appointed,_ and Councillors Henshall, Broadhurst and McKee were re-elected to the Education Committee, while Messrs. C. E. Parker and H. Kingdon were, re-appointed co-opted members of the Education Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0276" n="art0276">
        <head>Danger of Indigestion,</head>
        <p>The Danger of Indigestion,</p>
        <p>Indigestion is a very real danger, a menace to your health. and strength. It robs you of the nourishment you should obtain from the food you eat • it loads the system with impurities that find their way into the blood, causing headaches, languor, and blotchy skin. Certainly you can't afford to ignore indigesticn. It won't let you in any case. You will, therefore,, be well advised to take an occasional dose of Mother Siegel's Syrup, the great remedy for the common ailments of the stomach, liver, and bowels. There is nothing better for indigestion, or for biliousness, constipation, flatulence, headaches, and pains after eat- ing. • Not only does -Mother Seig,cl's Syrup- speedily b anish thesy mpt onis imP aired di- . gestion, but it keeps them away altogether,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>LORE'S APOLOGY.</head>
        <p>A LORE'S APOLOGY.</p>
        <p>Lord Wolmer after all Says he was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>Correctly Reported.</head>
        <p>Correctly Reported.</p>
        <p>ATTACK ON MR. LLOYD GEORGE</p>
        <p>Lord Newem and Lord Wolmer addressed the quarterly meeting of the Newton Division Conservative Association in the Old Music Hall, Market-place, Warrington, on Saturday after- noon. There was a fair attendance of delegates presided over by Lord Newton, who was sup- ported by Messrs. J. Fulton-Smith, C. B. F. Barron, Lionel F. Pilkington, W. Collingwood, A. Smith, W. Fairclough, W. W. Thompson, and Alderman Bennett.</p>
        <p>Giving his views on the recent controversy between Mr. Lloyd George and Lord Wolmer, Lord Newton said he regretted very much that he was not present on that occasion, because for once in a way he should have been of some value as a witness. ,At all events, he should have been able to express an opinion as to what really was said, and it was just conceivable that his evidence might have carried some little weight. He did not know in what light they re- garded that controversy, but to his mind it had .been an extremely useful controversy to the country at large, and he entirely failed to get that the member for the Newton Division had anything but cause to congratulate himself upon it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0279" n="art0279">
        <head>"The Correct Method."</head>
        <p>"The Correct Method."</p>
        <p>Of the two ways of dealing with the Chan- cellor of the Exchequer, Lord Wollner had adopted the correct method. He had not the least doubt that a controversy of this kind in- flicted great pain upon moderate members of the Liberal party. He happened to know from his own experience—he .had many friends among the Liberals—that it caused the orthodox members of that party acute pain. It was quite a new feature in political life that the Chan- cellor of the Exchequer should have reconrse to this sort of thing, just as it was an entirely new feature in English political life that he should indulge in a gamble upon the Stock Ex- change.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <head>Conservative Flattery.</head>
        <p>Conservative Flattery.</p>
        <p>He had always thought that the incorrect method of dealing with Mr. Lloyd George was to treat him as a serious politician or as• a Serious ' statesman. Personally, he disliked talking about him at all, but if they were to talk about him, and treat him seriously, they ought only to treat him seriously in matters for which he was himself responsible. It was quite a mistake to look upon Mr. Lloyd George as a serious politician or as a serious states- man. In reality—and nobody knew it better than himself—he was a demagogue pure and simple, He did not think the Chancellor imagined himself to be anything else. He (Lord Newton) knew there were a great many mem- bers of the Liberal party who disliked the Chancellor just as much as his political oppo- nents did, and nothing distressed them more than the great fuss that the Conservatives always made about him, and the inordinate importance that they attached to anything he said or did. The most essential point was that they did not trust him; they had not the small- est confidence in him. They had almost as low an opinion of him as the women had at the present moment—and, after all, women were remarkably good judges of character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0281" n="art0281">
        <head>IN REGARD TO LAND. Leave the Chancellor Alone.</head>
        <p>IN REGARD TO LAND. Leave the Chancellor Alone.</p>
        <p>Lord Newton then spoke in regard to the Home Rule and the land questions, and said the Government, finding themselves in a hope- less difficulty, had turned Mr. Lloyd George on to talk about pheasants, which were, in the opinion of many people, far more interesting than the prospect of civil war in Ireland. Per- sonally, he attached very little importance to Mr. Lloyd George's opinion about land or any- thing else. If we were to be told what the Government were going to do upon the land why could we not have it from the lips of the Prime Minister or the Minister of A gricultufe, whose opinion on the ,question of pheasants would be quite as interesting as that of Mr. Lloyd George? He thought that for the present the best thing they could do was to leave the Chancellor of the Exchequer alone on the ques. lion of the land—they might leave him to settle the question with the dukes. (Laughter and cheers.) He (Lord Newton) remembered a pathetic exclamation that came from the Chancellor at the time of the Budget: "Oh. these dukes; how they do worry us!" (Laugh- ter.)" They were worrying him now more than ever. Thre was nothing more embarrassing than to be offered 40,000 acres straight off. It was like offering a man a four thousand to one chance, and there was a reasonable prospect of his winning the money. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0282" n="art0282">
        <head>NOT OF IMPORTANCE.</head>
        <p>NOT OF IMPORTANCE.</p>
        <p>Lord Wolmer spoke in regard to they recent 'controversy, and said the: subject was not, of any real importance except that Mr. Lloyd George, being at a loss to find an answer, had stated that he (Lord Wolmer) was sheltering himself behind the reporters. He referred to this matter now only because he thought it necessary for him to explain that he would scorn to take any such action. They knew him too well to believe that. But he would like to say that he adhered absolutely to his version of what he said on that; occasion. They were present, most of them, and he thought they would agree wi▪ th him when he said that the impfesSion which he intended to 'convey in his remarks was a general stricture not only upon Mr. Lloyd George but also upon his colleagues mi. well. But in order to prevent any of the reporters from being drawn into this question, which really di▪ d not affect them, he had -taken full responsibility. The report referred to was h. perfectly accurate summary of his remarks. He had always experienced the utmost fair- ness from Lancashire reporters, and especially those gentlemen who reported their meetings. He had received a good deal of violent abuse from Mr. Lloyd George for his conduct. He Supposed ' the Chancellor of the Exchequer thought he (Lord Wolmer) Was not accustomed to .that in the Newtan Division. But they were not accustomed to fight opponents who would not take their word. Whatever might be said of the Labour party, they were sufficient gentlemen to take the word of a political oppo- nent. He did not know a Labour leader; either inside the House of Commons or out of it, who would not scorn to adopt the tactics of Mr. Lloyd George. (Hear, hear, and cheers.) How- ever much abuse their opponents might shower upon him it would not deter him from saying exactly what he thought about thein. Mr. J. -Fulton-Smith proposed and Mr. Wm. Fairclough seconded, a vote of thanks to the speakers, Mr. A. Smith supporting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0283" n="art0283">
        <head>WIDOW'S PATHETIC PLIGHT.</head>
        <p>THE WIDOW'S PATHETIC PLIGHT.</p>
        <p>Sequel to a Railway Fatality.</p>
        <p>The story of a widow's plight was told at an inquest on Tuesday, held by Mr. S. Brighouse, on Thomas Hannon, ironworks labourer,' 23, Kerfoot-street, Warrington, who was knocked down and received fatal injuries at Dallam sidings. Deceased, in crossing the line, slipped, and was knocked down by an engine, and later died from his injuries. There was a footbridge provided. for the workmen of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles, and it was stated that deceased was trespassing in erossing.the line. •</p>
        <p>George Albert Joynson, in the employ of ' the L. and N.-W.' Railway, said while engaged shunting he heard a cry. The engine was im- mediately ,stopped, and then Hannon was found injured. No one saw him attempt to cross the line.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a verdict of " Aceidenta death."</p>
        <p>The widow stated that her • husband was not insured, and that she was left with seven chil- dren. The Coroner asked if the L. and N.-W. Rail- way Company and Messrs. Pearson and Knowles would give the widow „anything, although there was no legal obligation. .They were both rich oompanies.,Ale gave Mrs. Hannon £3 out of the poor box, and he trusted that the 'companies would do • something for her. The representa- tives of the companies said they winild lay the statement Wore their clients.</p>
      </div>
