      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0286" n="art0286">
        <head>Fighting the Drink Traffic by Bands</head>
        <p>Fighting the Drink Traffic by Bands</p>
        <p>of Hope.</p>
        <p>TEMPERANCE SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>Foremost amongst the societies that are fight- ing the drink traffic in Warrington is the War- rington Band of Hope Union, under whose auspices a public meeting Was held in the Co-operative Hall on Temperance Sunday evening.. The speeches were of a very inspiring and encouraging nature, and the large audience fully enjoyed the various vocal items by the Jubilee Choir, which was under the able leader- ship of Mr. Spruce. Mrs. James Dodds pre- sided. She was supported on the platform by the Revs. E. Morgan, W. A. Beckett, H. Ogden, J. S. Chedburn, and T. T. James, Messrs. Gandy, J. Hallows and J. Jarman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0287" n="art0287">
        <head>MRS. DODDS AND PREVAILING INDIFFERENCE.</head>
        <p>MRS. DODDS AND PREVAILING INDIFFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Mrs. DODDS said as one looked back on the past year one realised that progress had been made. They could take heart from the fact that they were marching on. Anyone who had been at the Band of Hope rally in Manchester must have been inspired 'b.&gt;, the enthusiasm there: They had stroll.' opposition to fight in Warrington. (Hear, hear.) One met strong opposition. Somehow they could/combat oppo- sition; it was the terrible indifference that paralysed temperance workers, indifference from many people who, in a number of cases, were abstainers. That was the sort of thing that took heart Out of the work. When she was at Man- chester, she realised that though they were only a small community in' Warrington, and had such difficulties and opposition to face, they were one of a Yery great army. (Applause.) As she saw the different people answer to the call she realised- that all the world over there were people fighting the drink traffic. (Hear, hear.) She wanted them all to realise that they were going to win, and going to win in Warring- ton. They should set themselves a high ideal, and work 'for that end. If they were content with small things they, would only realise small things, bid if they were to strive, pray, and do all they could for the attainment( of a high ideal, the victory• would be theirs. I(Applause.) DUTY TO CONSCIENCE AND SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. MORGAN said he would do all in his power to sweep away the drink traffic. Very frequently they heard -people say, "What do you want to interfere with my liberty for? I can do as I like." But they could not, for they were bound by three golden chains. First, their own conscience; secondly, their duty to the ever- living law—if they were Christians; and thirdly, their duty to their brother. "Let every man be persuaded in his own Mind." That; day -ministers and Sunday school . teachers were asked to draw attention to the appalling re- sults of drunkenness. The man who was, not an abstainer should be impressed with the great- ness of the evil, and the vastness of the drun- kenness with which they, as • temperance workers had to contend.' (Hear, hear.) °John Foster told them of the ship that left the har- bour, and instead of getting to the right port, got to the 'enemy's harbour. Why? Because of the lodestone influencing the compass. They should see to it that the compass of Conscience was not controlled by the lodestone of passion. Bring the question of drink before their con- sciences and ask themselyes whether or not they ought to abstain. They should also do something by example in order to diminish the great evil of drunkenness. Then again, Christ was the law aide, and they ought to ask Him whether they ought to abstain or not. Every thing belonged to the Master; Their' life was the temple of God; their body was the outer Court; their mind Itie holy place, and the spirit was the sanctum sanctOrhm. They should let nothing go into that temple that degraded its sanctity, marred its beauty, impaired its use- fulness. or gave cause that the Holy One would abandon it as defile. Then there Was the rela- tionship to their brethren. They should do all that lay in their power to help their weaker brethren, those who had not the power of re- sistance.</p>
        <p>But for the drink they could close nine out of every ten of the gaols in England. Drink was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0288" n="art0288">
        <head>MAKING HEADWAY.</head>
        <p>MAKING HEADWAY.</p>
        <p>-----••-- --- The Rev. T. T. JAMES paid a glowing tribute to the excellent work done by Mrs. Dodds int the temperance movement. He hoped theY would all be encouraged by that meeting. TileY" were making headway in their fight against the drink evil, and they were burying it a little more as days went by. A great many of the. things for which our forefathers had suffered were beginning, to be admitted on all hands- (Hear, hear.) Even on the railway stations, ;11 the refreshment bars, one noticed that Peelu,t/ did not go with the same easiness and frequeno to indulge in intoxicating liquors. The Pre(i lions speaker had said that it would be a Pe •thing when the Church had got its hands clean(i They could be thankful that the Church 1";‘, improved ,immensely in this direction. Christian Church had made a definite advance' ,ment in regard to the drink trade. One of 01 most encouraging things to-day was the I'6'l' change 'in scientific and medical opinion as le the value of alcohol in the treatment of disease' Sir Victor Horsley showed in his book that Ode use of alcohol in the treatment of disease ha • decreased to-a very great extent. In some 11°5; pitals, where, ten years ago, the cost of alcolit". ran .into four figures, now, it Nc3s down to two figures, and in sonir the only enemy England,had to fear. Throng; the drink evil they saw scenes of squalor low 'hospitals the item was almost down,to vanishing point. (Applause.) It was well to look on such things as intensely encouraging, and temPer;: .ance workers should see •that all those years ° work and sacrifice had not been wasted. Via had reached a point where they could congratir late themselves on what had been done. they must not say that such successes were al" excuse for them lessening their efforts, becan having made that advance, this was just tj3 time to press it home, and see that the foundii: tion which had-been laid became a full and ti'e.; structure in the days that were to come. should be urged forward to victory by the 01.,15 CPSS which had attended their forefathers their - their efforts in the ,past. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Substitutes for Public-houses. He advocated temperance work along the of providing substitutes for public-houses. man who had his eyes open knew that public-house was not simply a drinking sis°r; The public-house actually fulfilled a certal;" social function in the life of the natiOn, althol't!, in a wrong way. They ought never to for ve,, that. many men used the public-houses as centre for social intercourse and social ship. Men Men went into public-houses, not were • to drink, but to talk on different things—h°rse: racing, football, politics, and lots of theologs; • (Laughter.) He heard men discussing theol, on the ke,rbstones at 11 o'clock at night. (14111%11 ter.) Temperance ivorkers should strive to a substitute for the public-houses, where riev could have all the social '•intercourse tb,:e desired without beer. tHear, hear.) rictilat palaces and football' matches had done a greo. deal to draw men away from the public-how They should provide something to attract. id man's attention,' call for his energies, excite his interest. (Applause.) He coulei there and discuss the lighter as well as theo.lqiii,, serioui aspects of life without damagino soul. The Band of Hope movement did' a great diees:. in saving the child life of the country. . _ _ _</p>
        <p>would help the children all.they con to . '„r the temperance pledge they would have a hew nation in the future. •</p>
        <p>DAY SCHOOL LECTURES. .• e Mrs. DODDS gave an interestin,g report inlee- gard • to the work done by the day school : -0 03 'hirers, and said that during the Year a r certificates had been awarded to children A 7 their essays. their utmost for the children, so that they er.ol,o` reap the good things that were being them to-day. den- I 7 kg (ATpphieayussel.i)Ould. endeavour to %tot Mr. J. JARMAN proposed a hearty vote of than to all those who had taken part in the meeting; Mrs. Dodds was a great worker in the teuiPfl..e, auce cause, and the services she rendered werla invaluable. (Applause.) The Revs. Morgan James. had given them. Most intelligent The dresses, which were very encouraging- re-- Free Churches in the town were always P,,c pared to take their stand iii the ter,re-r-a-' cause. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
