        <p>iiw.,,,,,„..:„, ~.....,..„,,,,..., „,...„,..44.,....,,..,, .... •,,5,.5,....,„, , . ~...,.... .„. ..., ...._.•,.y„,„ ii.. ~,.. ... . ...... • ~,,,,,,,, ~.. , .•_..,•.. ~.. .. 4,...... ...,. ~, 744.1„. ,C,..,.4.6,11/4 7 . . &amp;: ,Vtg '.4,. 4, ei _,, * - - ~ , A. '4 , .c. 1 '-‘,.. -1 ,ii:44447t 44194,ft1a '- 7 ''' r ..,:.:*Ol. ~ . 11 I '' ~ .. , ~ ::. :::.11111Win .', ' i''''',4 ~,, gsr*.t. !.• - ' ..t4 11 ,„..„. ."i''.';;::" . ..9</p>
        <p>PICKS OUT OF THE SCRUM. Pithy Pars About the Islll. Games. Batley came for a bagful of points. They got ten; Warrington got eighteen. The " laads " were terribly .disappointed. It looked at one time as if they would get All the points they had reckoned on. Warrington, however, saved the situation. The "gallant youtlif" were very vexed, Because they calculated on 'revenge. It is not to be their luck this season, Unless the clubs should meet in the N.U. Cup. Even then Warrington ought to do Just as they have done in the League matches.</p>
        <p>0(4000a Why would Batley be a valuable asset To the Opposition in the House of Commons? Because they are good obstructionists. They have adopted the Huddersfield tactics In this respect in their games, and It was very much in evidence on Saturday. In the game they played at Wilderspool. It was so very palpable that it made The standites roar with indignation. But for such a phase of tactics Warrington's score would have been a bit taller: Yorkshire clubs, however, have a weakness that way.</p>
        <p>e c -0 e e It was a well-fought game, Warrington. Just play a bit better at the Cliff, And Broughton will. be beaten to-day. Fix your eyes on the bagful of gold, And play for all you are worth. The hundred sovs. are still on offer. Who said Nicholas cannot get tries? And who said he could not kick a goal? All these he can do—repeat the order. • Four Batley men tried Thomas's weight. They could not throw him so easily. The Batleyites found him too heavy. ee e e</p>
        <p>Bevan, the Batley crack, didn't get away well, For he was well watched by Waywell. And so all was well that ended well. Cox made a bit of a record with The try he so adroitly scored for Warrington. It is rare a forward gets such a try. A tricky bit of play it did prove. And it took Batley by surprise. Ruddick simply rollicked in the game. The more he was tackled the happier Did the " little 'un " seem to enjoy it. He was always in the front of the picture.</p>
        <p>Interest in local football matches centred around the Cheshire Cup-tie at Reatley, where Oughtrington 'United were opposed by Latch- ford. The home club were at full strength, - Jones again taking up his usual position on the left wing, whilst the visitors had to take the field minu3 the valuable services of J. C. Bardsley, which caused a re-arrangement. They were beaten by 4 goals to 3. • In the Warrington and District League Great :Sankey had as their visitors the Orford Bar- racks team, *horn they defeated by four goals to none. The Weston Club 11,04 Crosfield's Reserve as visitors and were successful in annexing both points. Stockton Heath, in .their encounter with Daresbury, could not score in the first portion, but during the second moiety had matters much their Own way and ran out easy winners. After suffering several defeats, the committee of the Emmanuel Old Boys have improved the team, and from the Bank Quay Recs. gained their first point.</p>
        <p>followed suit A penalty against the visitors was well saved by Ravenseroft. Shortly after Jones notched a third goal, and -Taylor and Giiford added-kfurther. points. Final:' Stockton Heath, 5 goals; Daresbury, nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Second Division. Emmanuel Old Boys V. Bank Quay Recs.</p>
        <p>At Victoria Park. During an attack on the home goal the visitors were awarded a penalty for "hands." No score took place, owing to the cleverness of Knight, the home goaAeeper. The home backs, were penalised again. This time Logan took the kick and secured the lead. Ectleston, on the right, got away and increased 'the lead for the visitors. Roused by these re- verses, the home men managed to make an attack on the visitors' goal, and Wainwright was successful in reducing. the lead. Interval: Two goals to one, in favour of the ,visitors. The Emmanuel Boys gave the visitors' defence a warm time of it, but they were successful for 'a time in saving their goal. Their downfall came from a corner-kick, .which was very well placed by Wainwright, and Chester, the Recs.' .goalkeeper,, unfortunately put through his own goal. . When the whistle blew the score was two goals each. Players :—Old Boys: Knight; Eccles and Ashton; 'Lawless, Hatton and O'Brien; Wain- wright, Aitkenhead, Isherwood, Caldwell and Harrison. Recs.: J. Chester; Bushell and Tay- lor; Worrall, Logan and Booth; H. Eccleston, Davies, BradSlia*, J. Smith and E. Green.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0292" n="art0292">
        <head>000000</head>
        <p>000000</p>
        <p>Where was the man with the "rattle" ? He was heard at Oldham—defeat! And also in the cup-tie—defeat again! Wilderspool heard him not on Saturday. He had vanished like the swallows—victory! A "rattle" seems to be a bad, mascot. The threequarters were very scrappy. How can, they be made to •Combine?' Now; can anybody answer that 'fiddle? . Perhaps the man who gave the conundrums Away at the entrance gate can reply. • The committee would like a 'pointed Solution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0293" n="art0293">
        <head>ceeee*</head>
        <p>ceeee*</p>
        <p>The grand-standites think that Ruddick Should be tried at centre-threequarter. Evidently others exist who think likewise. "I am certain," writes one of the bhoys, "That if Ruddick was tried ateentre "He would weld the threequarteris together." And so say all Change a letter in a certain word And it means quite the opposite, and which Is the 'word?—united becomes untied. Change the half-backs, who seem untied, And you may have the threequarters united. Ce</p>
        <p>There was .plenty of hand shaking On the Wilderspool pitch on Saturday. This was to mark the try-getting. It was a happy sign of unity amongst The " b-hoys " on the Warrington side. Cox scored the trickiest try of the day. We ,want some more of the same, Cox. Chesters got his first try since He scored in the Cumberland county match. We want still,more from you, Chesters. Bradshaw had very hard luck, for on, Two occasions he should have got over the line.</p>
        <p>C. Brown played an improved game. -He wants to learn how to pass more smartly. If he did so, Tindall could not have inter- cepted The pass from which he managed to score. Ruddick, in charging Lyons, missed and Slid along five yards upon his face. The "little 'un " merely smiled at the glide. Ruddick's glide is scarcely a " Gaby glide." Is Jolley learning the Tango step? He seemed to get into a tangle pretty often. The "gallant youths" were puzzled greatly By the strength Thomas is happy to possess. e</p>
        <p>The Warrington "A's" gained two more points. Leigh Shamrocks were thbir victims. ' This is four points they have got out of The " swate little flowers" of old Leigh. Critchley is the " A's " goal-kicker-in-chief. He got two fine penalty kicks over. Brown, the " A " half-back, is a scorer When he gets a proper opportunity to do so. Dickenson was reported in good form. The veteran surprises the boys at times. Are the Reserves to win the Combination Championship? They are going the way to achieve that result.</p>
        <p>HOCKEY. LATCHFORD WIN THEIR MATCH.</p>
        <p>Latchford first had Swinton Park as visitors. The game was anything but a brilliant one but the honours went tct the right quarters, when Latchford got their second goal, which proved to be the winning one. Swinton were the first to score, shortly before half-time, and they retained this lead until half-way through the second half, although Pearson had hard lines on several occasions. Then Latchford got a couple of goal, one scored by Pearson, from good work by Travis, and the other one shot by Colton. The home team were all over their opponents for the major part of the game, litut did not finish well. Travis and Dale played well in the Latchford attack, while Billington was the best defender.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0294" n="art0294">
        <head>Second Side Vanquished.</head>
        <p>Second Side Vanquished.</p>
        <p>The second eleven were vanquished at Swin- ton by 3-1, after a scrappy game. The real difficulty the.visitors had to contend with was the uneven ground, which was the worst Latch- ford have played on this season. Ali the scor- ing was done in the second halt_ Swinton get- ting their goals from three breakaways, and though Latchford did most of the attacking the forwards were weak in front of goal, but Mars- den scored near the end. G. Mynekyme, in the visitors' goal created a good impression, and got some nasty shots away in the first half. Brad- shaw and Charlie Newbold were the best of the backs. The game was spoiled by the hom&amp; 11Da- pire (who took the whole fields repeatedly fail- ing, to whistle _against the Swinton players for down-checking and tackling on the wrong side. Sltogether a visit to Swinton, for various reasons, is not exactly a thing of joy. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0295" n="art0295">
        <head>Teams for To-day.</head>
        <p>Teams for To-day.</p>
        <p>To-day the first eleven are at. home to Man- chesfer. University first with the following team: J. Bruhl ; W. Fletcher and C. Billington ; F. W. Isherwood, J. B. Wilkinson and A. Knight; R. A. Dale, D. Travis, J. E. Pearson, W. H. Minards and H. R. Colton. Umpire, Mr. F. Grounds. Bully-off 2.30. The second eleven travel to Manchester, and the team will be: G. Mynekyme ; W. T. Eccles and J. Albinson ; A. E. Bradshaw, F. C. New- bold and. T. Banks; C. Newbold, V. H. Fletcher, W. S. Furness, A. G. Marsden and A. B. Star- key. Train leaves Central Station 1.55, Umpire, Mr. H. Eccles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0296" n="art0296">
        <head>CHESHIRE AMATEUR CUP. Latchford Out of the Competition.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE AMATEUR CUP. Latchford Out of the Competition.</p>
        <p>At Heatley, before a good gate. Teams : Oughtrington United: Burrows; F. Thomasson and Kay; J. Holt, Owen and Lord; H. Thomas- son, Haddon, H. Holt, Louse and Jones. Latch- ford: Kite; Robinson and Downs; Siddall, Webb and Carter; Smith, Challinor, Lees, Taylor and Tinder.</p>
        <p>The game started in a sensational manner. From the kick-off, the home team attacked, and Thomasson shot over the bar. The home halves obtained possession. Louse received .a long pass from the right wing and scored inside three minutes. Encouraged by this early success the homesters played very briskly, but a good pass by Webb set his forwards going and Burrows was called upon to save. Both teams settled down and some good play was witnessed. Tinder shot straight for goal, but Burrows was safe, and a .good attempt by Siddall just skimmed the bar. Directly after, Burrows had to concede a corner, which was cleared. Owing to faulty kicking, Haddon pounced on the ball and sent it into the net, adding the second point for the 'home team., H. Holt scored a third goal for the home .team. After some good play in mid- field, Lees secured the ball, worked for position, •and scored a grand goal .for the visitors.. Half- time arrived with the score three goals to one against .Latehford, . After some good play in midfield, early in the second half, offside against the home forwards gave .Latchford a chance, and later, from a throw-in, Thomasson had to concede a corner, which was, accurately placed by Smith. • Bur- rows, in attempting to clear, put the ball into the net, and the visitors scored their second point. Encouraged by this. success, Latchford became aggressive, but offside against four of their forwards sent them back. • Thomasson received on the right, and, passing several opponents, shot splendidly into goal, where Kite only partly cleared. H. Holt rushed up and put the ball into the net. Roused by this reverse, the visitors went straight into the home quar- ters and gained a corner, .which was taken by Carter, and Lees added a 'third goal for the visitors.. Result: Oughtrington, '4 goals.; Latch- ford, 3 goals.</p>
        <p>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. Lymm V. Broadheath United. At Lymm. The home team were: Haddon; Hind and Taylor; Daniels, Woods and Daniels; Owen, Foster, Wilson, Guest and Barber. From the kick-off Lymm went into the visitors' territory. Barber scored the first goal. Jones soon after scored the second goal for the home team. The home men continued to press, but no further score took place up to the interval, when the score was two goals to nil in favour of Lymm.</p>
        <p>The visitors' forwards, early in the second half, got past the home half-backs and had a short spell of attack. Clegg was successful with a good shot. This put confidence into the visiting team and they played hard for the equaliser, but met a stern defence in Hind and Haddon. Owen scored a third goal for Lymm, and the result was: Lymm, 3 goals; Broadheath United, 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0297" n="art0297">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>First Division.</p>
        <p>Great Sankey v. Orford Barracks.</p>
        <p>Played on the l'enketh Recreation Ground. Teams :—Great Sankey: Bond; Glover and Stephens; Ireland, Gardiner and Payne; Dar- lington, E. Ditchfield, J. Ditchfield, Poulter and Houghton. Orford Barracks: Morrison; Staples and Lonnen ; Callow, Birkenhead and Topping; Campbell, Hicks, Hanley r Trafford and Spicer. 'The home forwards at once attacked and scored, Poulter doing the needful. Darlington was successful in intercepting a fine centre from A. Houghton and added a. second goal. The visitors had a turn, but failed to score. Half- time found Great Sankey leading by two goals to nil.</p>
        <p>- The Barracks team played smartly and mut more energy into their game, but they failed to push home the onslaught Sankey entered upon an attack on the visitors' goal and S. Poulter registered a third point with a fine shot. A. Gardiner, the home centre-half, scOre,d a fourth goal. The game ended: Great Sankey, :4 goals; Orford Barracks, none.</p>
        <p>Bond, Glover, Darlington and A. Houghton were the pick of the home team, while the visi- tors were best served by Staples, Birkenhead and Trafford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0298" n="art0298">
        <head>Weston v. Crosfield's Reserve. •</head>
        <p>Weston v. Crosfield's Reserve. •</p>
        <p>On the ground of the former club. The home forwards were very nippy and put the visitors' goal in danger. After some open play the home team made an attack and this resulted in one of the visiting backs conceding a corner. Deakin placed well and Aiolyneux succeeded in heading the sphere between the posts. So the score stood at half-time.</p>
        <p>Resuming, the visitors got to the home end, but their efforts were spoiled by offside play. The home defence conceded a corner to the visitors, and the goal was heavily bombarded, but Nancollis staved off defeat by some remark- able saves. Conway increased the Weston score. This reverse nettled the visitors, and from the centre-kick they raided the Thome goal and a defender handled inside the penalty area. Wil- son took the kick and shot straight at Nancollis, who saved at the expense of a corner, which was also got away, and the home right wing was a source of trouble to the visitors' defence. Cros- field's came again. and this time Wibon safely stQered the boll between the posts. Result; Weston, 2 goals; Crogfield's, 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0299" n="art0299">
        <head>Stockton Heath v._ Daresbury.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath v._ Daresbury.</p>
        <p>Daresbury, the "Heath's" old rivals, were the visitors to the West-avenue ground. Teams: Stotskton Heath: Simcock ; Gosling and Waller; Gifford, Denny and Magill; Gerrard, Lockhart, Jones, Smith and Taylor. Daresbury : Ravens- croft; S. De Prez and G. Fleet; Ryder, Cash and Forster; H. Forster, F. Forster, H. De Prez, Plumpton and Cartwright._ Owing to. having to face the wind, the "Heathens" concentrated their efforts to pre- vent ,the visitors from scoring, and they were successful.. Half-time arrived with no score.</p>
        <p>On resuming, the visitors had to play with only ten men, owing to S. De Prez being injured in a collision with Gerrard. The home seam had the most of the game, and they set up a continuous bombardment of the Daresbury goal. Lockhart Opened the score, and Jones quickly</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0300" n="art0300">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES. Orford St. John's v. Adult School</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES. Orford St. John's v. Adult School</p>
        <p>Played at Long-law, Orford. The game was keenly contested: and from a good passing bout the home forwards tested the visitors' custodian, who fisted the ball away, and the game was carried to the home quarters. The home team played up vigorously, and a grand shot by Mill- Ward was saved by vigorously, at the expense of a corner. Smalley placed the ball favourably for Hankinson to open the score for St. John's. Encouraged by this success the home - team 'played rolendidly, and Denny sent in a fast, low shot, .which the .visitors' goalkeeper failed to clear. At half-time the s:7ore was two goals to nil in favour of the home team.</p>
        <p>After much open play Millward, the home centre, broke away, ,dribbled, through the oppo- sition and scored a third goal with a low shot. The game was evenly contested up to the finish, but no further scoring took place. Result: Orford St. John's, 3 goals; Adult School, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0301" n="art0301">
        <head>St. Barnabas' v. Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>St. Barnabas' v. Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>At Lovely-lane. Vernon distinguished himself by centring finely and the ball was put through out of the reach of Holmes. Midfield play fol- lowed and neither could claim any advantage. Half-time arrived with St. Barnabas' leading by one goal to nil. On resuming, the home centre-forward scored a second goal from a scramble in the visitors' goal-mouth. Both sets of players began to tire owing to the heavy state of the ground and there was no more scoring. Result :.St. Barna- bas', 2 goals; Stockton Heath Reserve, none.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0302" n="art0302">
        <head>Orford Juniors v. Widnes St. Marie's.</head>
        <p>Orford Juniors v. Widnes St. Marie's.</p>
        <p>Orford Juniors played Widnes St. Marie's at home, and an exciting game ensued. G. Red- ford cleared splendidly for the Juniors, placed the ball to J. Redford, who made a run, and put in a fine centre, from which A. Williams scored the first goal. Widnes retaliated by scoring from a scrummage in front of the home goal. The Junior forwards made a great effort, which ended in Williams again scoring. Half- time found the Juniors on top, 2-1. Widnes pressed strongly, and scored after forcing the home keeper to bring off some bril- liant saves. Orford now had the better of the play, O'Day and Jackson scoring in quick suc- cession. Practically on time J, Redford recorded a fifth for Orford. For the winners, Pickering, G. Redford,- J. Redford, Williams, Archer and Riley played well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0303" n="art0303">
        <head>Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's Reserve v. Excelsior Reserve.</head>
        <p>Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's Reserve v. Excelsior Reserve.</p>
        <p>The Excelsor attacked, to be repulsed by Trankey and Massey: Grappenhall soon forced matters, but owing to good defensive play on the part of the visiting backs were unable to score. The Excelsior , replied with a rush, which car- ried them to the home goal, and opened their accomit through the medium of Harrison. Stung by this reverse, Grappenhall pressed, and Nicholson scored after good combination on the part of the forwards. , The visitors, however, were not to be denied, and Harrison again netted the ball for them, half-time finding the score 2-1 in the visitors' favour. On resumption, Grappenhall tried hard to score, but Crozier defended splendidly. How- ever, with good combination, they equalised, Cameron doing the necessary with a good shot. Play now became of a very strenuous nature, both 'teams striving desperately to get. the decisive goal. The Excelsior repeatedly pressed, but. the home backs were always safe. Several corners were forced, but were unfruitful.' At last Davies and Woodcock got going on the right wing, ending in the latter player scoring for Grappenhall, who thus ran out winners of a hard game by 3-2. For the visitors, Harrison and Crozier played well, whilst Hankey, Evans and Davies were the pick of the Grappenhall side.</p>
        <p>Latchford Juniors defeated Bank Quay " A " team by 8 goals to 3.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Institute met Fairfield United at Latchford and won by 6 goals to 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0304" n="art0304">
        <p>SHOTS AT THE GOAL.</p>
        <p>In the enp-tie at Oughtrington, Louse opened the score with a rattling shot in the top corner of the net.</p>
        <p>Lees scored with a beautiful shotwhich beat Burrows all the way.</p>
        <p>H. Thomasson gave Kite a warm handful from long range and scored the winning goal. Oughtrington United should thank Burrows for his display in goal. - Hinds played a grand game at back for Lymm. Woods was very effective at centre-half. Barber, Jones and Owen scored Lyrnm's goals. Great Sankey were successful in beating the Soldiers by four goals to nil. A. Houghton played a fine game on the extreme left. Herbert Ditchfield was given a rest last week. • Glover is in fine form,this season at back. Trafford was the best forward on the Barracks team.</p>
        <p>Birkenhead played a very good game at centre- half.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's left two points at Weston. They Nund Nancollis, in the home goal, to be very, clever. Wilson missed a penalty for Crosfield's, As also did Trafford for the Barracks team. Molyneux is very useful for Weston in the scoring line. Wilson scored with a splendid shot for Cros- field's Reserve.</p>
        <p>Alolyneux and Conway scored for the Weston Club.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath added two points and five goals at the expense of Daresbury. Jones scored twice.and Taylor, Lockhart and Gifford one each.</p>
        <p>The tmmanuel, team have not done very well this season. Knight has proved himself a good goalkeeper. Last week they gained their first point. More luck to them now they have changed their name. Probably their luck has changed with them. Wainwright was successful in scoring" two goals, While Logan and Eccleston responded for the Recs.</p>
        <p>Why did Excelsior protest against Stockton Heath?</p>
        <p>"Only" because their weakened team included six men who were over age. The "Heathens" complained about the Excel- sior players' supposed bad language. •There is an old saying about people who live in glass houses, &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0305" n="art0305">
        <head>ELEMENTARY SCHOOLS' FOOTBALL LEAGUE. Results up to date,</head>
        <p>ELEMENTARY SCHOOLS' FOOTBALL LEAGUE. Results up to date,</p>
        <p>Goals.</p>
        <p>P. W. D. L. For Agt. Pts. Evelyn Street 5 5 0 0 21 10 St. James' Wycliffe 3 2 1 0 15 4 5</p>
        <p>Arpley Street 0 2 9 8 4 Heatbside 4 1 1 2 4 9 S Parochial 3 0 1 2 2 5 1 St. Ann's Heamont St. Barnabas' Peuketh 0 0 2 2 15 0</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0306" n="art0306">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's play Earlestown Y.M.C.A. at home with: Makeshaft; Evans and Booth; Brazendale, Ford and Parr; Thomas, Ford, Richards, Parr and Hampson. Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's Reserve against Pad- gate United, at Padgate, play: Woodward; Massey and Hantley; Fairhurst, Ravenscroft and Davies; Woodcock, Carey, Peters, Cameron and Nicholson.</p>
        <p>The following will represent Adult School against Orford Barracks: Welsby; Garnett and Spillsbury ; Ridyard, Hunter and Spencer; Rhodes, Rowe, Dykes, J. Leigh and J. Banner. Stockton Heath, against Weaver Refining Co., at Acton Bridge, will play: Simcock ; Gosling and Woodward; Magill, Denny and Gifford, Gerrard, Lockhart, Jones, Smith %Ind Taylor.</p>
        <p>In the Widnes and District Alliance match, at West-avenue, against Widnes St. Paul's, Stockton Heath play: Vernon; Waller and Jefferson; Glover, Bailey and West; Parr, Mor- timer, Rigby, Marsh and Houghton. Latchford Juniors meet Padgate Wesleyans with: Podmore; Brown and Forster; Granger, Cross and Howard; Yates, Robinson, Llewellyn, Holmes, and Heastings. Emmanuel Institute play Longford Juniors, at Longford, with: Broadhurst; Newton and A. N. Other; Banner, T. Hughes and W. Ayres; Knight, F. -Hughes, Roberts, Heacock and Berry, forwards. Emmanuel Old Boys play Orford United at Victoria Park with: Whittaker; Eccles and Warham ; Aikenhead, Caldwell and Ashton; Singleton, Smith, Carter, Brown and Allen. Padgate United play Grappenhall St. Wil- frid's Reserve, at Padgate, with: Nork ; War- burton and Sankey; Hodgkinson, Marsh and Williams; John Houghton, Derbyshire, James Houghton, Rigby and Hardman. Orford St. John's meet Orford Barracks, at Long-lane. St. John's team: J. Forber; W. Smith and L. Atherton; J. Denny, W. Stretford and T. Sudell ; T. Tickle, W. Millward, A. Hunt, A. Meachin (captain) and S. Hankinson. Warrin'gton Excelsior No. 2 team play Widnes Juniors, on Bewsey-road ground, with: Jackson (captain); Crozier and Warburton; Done, Turner and Stringer; Hands, Barton, Ashton, Payton, and Harrison. Great Sankey play Diver at Penketh Recrea- tion, with: Bond; Glover (capt.) and Stephens; Ireland, Gardner and Payne; Darlington, H. Ditchfield, J. Ditchfield, Poulter and Houghton. Orford Athletic play Fairfield at Orford-road with: Holden; Battler and Timmins; Dooley, Tranter and Fitzgerald; Woodhouse, Fallon, Sudell, Newton and Jennings.</p>
        <p>A club bearing the name of the Elmwood- avenue Congregational Chtrch C. and F.C. has been formed in connection with the new church and schools there. They play their first match to-day, when the following team will face Latch- ford lovers: W. Phoenix; J. Phcenix and But- ton; Mould, Jas. Phcenix and Looker; Hughes, East, Clayton, Bridge and Ellam.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. North Cheshire League. Latchford v. Oughtrington United; B. Howell.</p>
        <p>Warrington and District League.</p>
        <p>Orford Barra-As v. Adult School; C. S. Essen- high. Great Sankey v. Divex; G. H. Rushton. Weaver Refining Co. v. Stockton Heath; S. Breeze.</p>
        <p>Widnes and District Alliance. Stockton Heath " A" v. Widnes St. Paul's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0307" n="art0307">
