      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0309" n="art0309">
        <head>Warrington Revived When They seemed to be a Beaten Gide.</head>
        <p>Warrington Revived When They seemed to be a Beaten Gide.</p>
        <p>NICHOLAS !NOW IN A 441111Y"-Irtia HUMOUR.</p>
        <p>lifThe first fifteen minutes of the match betw'een of° rington and Batley looked as if the threat , t Yorkshiremen to have a heavy revenge 414' the defeat at Batley in September was to aterialise. At that stage the visitors were ten lkittts to the good, and going strong in every Partment. The Warrington men were limp jurld inept, and the spectators were in a blue i , n,k, but the optimist n the Press box was tlite cheerful, "Just wait a bit and you will change." The optimist was happy at half- but he was nigh bursting with delight at me close, which found Warrington victors by Points to 10- t It could not be called a great game; it was too PPY to rank as that. Still it was interesting, What was more important, it ended in War- egton annexing two much-needed points. 10,4t,1e3, thus are the first team this season out of .Ctell Warrington have been able to gain four v'agne points. In the first half the game was ,at the see-saw order. Warrington started with ti dash which promised well, but did not realise glit promise. Then Batley, more by luck than guidance, scored. The Warrington three- barters had the ball, and in passing it out C. Pr!‘",n. made a- clumsy throw to Bradshaw. 11.111dall happened to be in the line of the,ball's jumped to it like a trout rising tb a fly, tVght it, and, with only Jolley to pass, ran • rough with his try.</p>
        <p>A Good Bit of Ltszk. it;11 Was a pure piece of luck, but, all the same, 4k4(1a11 deserved his try on account of his alert- This put life into Batley., and they played welerly for a time. Tindall scored another well th°l'ked try, and Warrington were 10 points to the, bad. Tee Warrington men then realised %ell' position. Time after time they invaded tve 'Batley territory, and here they could do ii,erYthing except score.. ftuddick had hard k'es in being pulled down when on the ace of 411111 g from one of his brilliant individual which were a feature of the game. , icholas later got the ball, and he ran over 414:irtly. The try was made into a goal by 1:t11,e37. We then witnessed a change for the t4."er on the part of Warrington. About a D:llute later Nicholas gut away once. more and te4 to Chesters, who ploughed his way ItinTugh the Batley defence and scored. Jolley ed the goal and the *teams were on a level. trll-rington thus retrieved their position in or a sensational ma-nrier. The play then tjaine limp, and it was a relief to the specta- -I'B when. half-time arrived -with drawn points.</p>
        <p>Warrington Add Eight Points. The th ground team were ,certainly the better in e second portion. Ruddick and Bradshaw ral times came near scoring, and Bradshaw, • ,intercepting a Batley pass near the home diverted Batley's most determined attack kr); the second half. It fell to the lot of Cox to the home side the lead. The ball came out scrum almost on the home goal-line, and as Ol,Pligh,lin was passing it on to his partner, Cox 'Dell out of the pack, caught the sphere and over with a smart try. This was a feat id°lll Performed by a forward. and Cox well erved the cheer he (nat. Jolley missed. t Warrington played from now better than they • done in any other period of the game, and 6iper several futile attempts to add to the score a leholas got possession, showed a fine turn of e,seed, and, swerving grandly, outwitted Lyons b'd Tindall, and scored almost under the cross- Nicholas essayed the kick and got the goal. Ce game was won, but the Warrington men i:fDt up the attack until time, when they were t victors by 3 goals 4 tries (18 points) to 4°,41s 2 tries (10 points), and Batley went home L'out the much-desired revenge.</p>
        <p>xt The Home Forwards Do Well. fkri lich of the credit for the Warrington victory m4,,a. to the lot of the home forwards. - They isiit.s°(l admirably all through and gave the la "kits no peace. In addition the home pack Viel./3 hot so guilty of obstruction as were the f lug sextet Skelhorne was always to the r°ht tors • Thomas proved a sore trial to the visi- and the way he kept the ball saved War- Oil on several occasions. Cox deserved his At was without doubt the smartest bit of ork, seen during the afternoon. In fact, it was hot- 8° quickly that many of the spectators did els" how the score came about Pir 'testers got his first try of the season for . arrin 14 vs gton, although he had already scored one " Lancashire-Cumberland county match at fotiletester. Taken all round, the Warrington DaZards were decidedly superior to the Batley tor 48 they were at Batley in September. But Pleir hard and purposeful work, the score /I'd Probably have,told a different story.</p>
        <p>The Threequarters Weak. eee more it was demonstrated that the weak 6Det • in the Warrington team is at threequarter. " centre men are lacking. In defensive i4711z C. Brown and IL Davis did very well, but 44,1 °Dening up the attack they lack initiative, are tots slow for the wing men. The passing zil3' did indulge in was not executed with judg- iPt, and an illustration of that was furnished oce„tile intercepted pass from which Tindall 11,:ed. Bradshaw was no so good as h he has 411,11. and on quite a number of occasions e 1- "wed himself to be outpaced Some of the . lq,,L'ies which brought him down were very for Batley, but twice in the second half 4hasnould. have scored. Way-well was, a quiet tha energetic worker, and did not get as many iTees as his colleague on the other wing. I)eiZete seemed to be a better understanding *itlZell Nicholas and Ruddick than we have 10orp scsed this season. There is still room foil- en, and if it comes about the pair will about as good a brace of half-backs as 41 r;i: 3'uejeu nd in the Northern Union. Nicho- Irbr-ea his game of the season and his tries fic,oie well worked, Ruddick should also have 111 e and his tricky dashes were a feature of Jolley was in better form/ 11A), A TEAM BEAT LEIGH SHAMROCKS. Tl, h. v•ictery of Fifteen Points to Eight. 01-et E.:. Warrington Reserve secured a fine win (1.5 44eigh Shamrocks at Leigh by 3 goals 3 tries tj,einte) to 1 goal 2 tries (8 points). Early game Critchley opened the score with a °044 goal, and soon after he kicked another ,sllooti'reni a penalty chance. Leigh now played Vrej, and Warrington ran all over them. A. -tdei 1,4 got a try, which Critchley converted. 44: scored a try and the interval found the 1)014-t standing in favour of Warrington by nine 8 te three. Fine passing by the Warring- -14 thquartet enabled Brookes to score two tries oot e second portion, but the major points were ettedB!lired• Leigh got a try, which was con-</p>
        <p>TO-DAY AT BROUGHTON. tr Lurther Warrington Team Changes. 411,10 arrington play up to expectation they tt-,%14.414.8.:21±ire two more valuable points at the</p>
        <p>By "Scrum"</p>
        <p>Cliff ground, Broughton, to-day. There has been a re-arrangement of the Warrington three- quarter line and the change should tend to make that quartet more of a, striking force. Brookes, who has played consistently with the " A " side, is given a trial on the wing, and Dickenson has been brought in to partner him. The teams will</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. Jolley. Brookes, Dickenson, C. Brown, Bradshaw. Nicholas and Ruddick. Chesters, Cox, Fearnley, Thomas, Clare, Skelhorn.</p>
