        <p>Only Address- -109 L ondon Road.</p>
        <p>rt for FORD and DE.LAGZ GAR, I TAXIS FOR HIRE. RING Ur 63.</p>
        <p>Prior to the senior match between Warring- ton and the Rangers, the Warrington schoolboys' will meet a Broughton team. The following have been selected to do duty for Warrington: Smith (St. James'); Brady (St. Mary's), Jones (lleathside), Walker and Oxley (Arpley-street) ; Skelhorn (St. James') and Watson (Arpley- street); Graham (St. Mary's), Spittle (Arpley- street), Armitage, Bibby, Lawton and Lockhart (St. James'). Reserve, Worrall (Heathside).</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' STILL IN WINNING FORM. Prestwich Their Latest Victims.</p>
        <p>Rylands' met and defeated Prestwich Institute at Prestwich in a League matoh, before a moder- ate crowd. Both teams were short of two of their regular players through injuries. Rylands' winning the toss, elected to play up the brow and against the wind. Harris (late of Brough- ton Rangers) kicked off, Carter returning. Prest- wich, with a good pack, elected to play a for- ward game, and continually rushed the ball to their opponents' line, but the defence held good. From a pack Evans dropped a goal for Prest- wich.</p>
        <p>Rylands' were nearly through at the other end with good passing among the backs. Half- time arrived with the score: Prestwich, one goal (two points); Rylands', nil.. Stretford commenced the second half down hill, but the Prestwich men were determined, and swarmed round Rylands' goal. For obstruc- tion Harris kicked a goal from half-way. This roused the " Wiremen," and good passing among the baOks saw the ball travel from Carter to Horrocks, who parted to Morris, who scored at the corner, the goal kick failing. With only a point between ,thezn, both teams put in all they knew. F. Horrocks, fielding a kick, instead of returning, raced up to his threequarters, and Carter doubling back, Hr- rocks passed inside, for Carter to drop d lovely goal. Prestwich raced away again, but the de- fence held good, the game ending in favour of Rylands' by one goal one try (five points), to Prestwich, two goals (four points). Both forwards played a grand game. Warren, Carter and Hdrrocks played fine for the visit- ors, while Forster, Harris and Evans shone for the home team.</p>
        <p>The Cup Tie To-day. In the third round of the Lancashire Junior Cup, Rylands' Recs. meet Sherdley Athletic at Gorsey-lane. The home team will be: F. for- rocks; J. Griffiths, S. Carter, A. Horrocks and P. Morris; G. Warren and G. Floyd; A. Stret- ford (capt.), J. Whittaker, J. Brindwood, H. Morris, J. Broadhurst and F. Mason.</p>
        <p>Rylands' Recs. "A" v. Westfield Amateurs. This match, in the St. Helens and District League, took place at the Gorsey-lane enclosure. Westfield were soon on the aggressive, and a good passing movement resulted in Hull scor- ing, Glover converting. Play was of an even nature till half-time. On resuming, the "Wire- men" pressed and a good run .by Riley fin- ished with Hewitt scoring at the corner, but the try was disallowed. Westfield now pressed hard, and Griffiths scored a second try, Glover failing at goal. Final: Westfield, eight points; Rylands', nil. Potter, Riley and Myles played well for Rylands'.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION ITEMS. St. George's Mission, ilopwooestreet, War- rington, are willing to play any Warrington and District Second Division team. Secretary, .T. Phillips, 2, Tilley-street, Warrington. . St. George's Mission have made a good capture in Joe Phillips, who scored two smart goals against Longford Recreation, at Longford. The newly-formed Elmwood-avenue Congrega- tional Church Football Club would like fixtures with junior clubs. Write to the Secretary, J. Phcenis, 82, Dalton Bank, .Warrington.</p>
        <p>• „ 9.!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0313" n="art0313">
        <head>I Ls 'PM</head>
        <p>I Ls 'PM</p>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Workingmen's</p>
        <p>Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0314" n="art0314">
        <head>N.A. CENTRE.</head>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>THE GOWY.</p>
        <p>There was a falling off in numbers here. J'er- haps it was because the catches had not tome up to expectations. Or are membere*wstiting for the stone- baits to come in? When these baits are going there is no doubt that more Specimens are caught than are caught with-the maggot, as records go to show.</p>
        <p>_ Speaking of records, our notice has been called this week to anglers saying the Gowy is not the stream as is represented in our notes. Well, records alone will prove this, and, accord- ing to our set boards we have over a dozen roach weighed in over 11b., trout over 11b., dace over 120 z., and pike over 91b. for this season. If these are not specimen fish to put up on set boards, well, it would be interesting reading to Warrington anglers, as well as beneficial, to let us know of some spot, anywhere within a 20-mile radius, where we can put up the sarce records. We can assure our readers that the Working Men's Angling Association would not be long before their members gave it a fair trial, as a change would do good at present, when all places we know of are not fishing up to expectations.</p>
        <p>However, the Gowy was never a place where an angler could depend on having a good catch week-end after week-end,•hut they know it is a water where specimen fish are to be caught, without our information through the Press, and that is, and always will be, the succeoe of the River Gowy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0315" n="art0315">
        <head>ELLESMERE.</head>
        <p>ELLESMERE.</p>
        <p>One or two members paid a visit to Ellesmelre last week-end, but roach and perch were not on feed, although a couple of roach of lib. 40s. were creeled. Pike seemed the only fish feeding, and about a dozen were taken by anglers on the Ellesmere Mere. It is most surprising that more Warringtonians do not pay this place a visit, as it is an ideal spot for the sport. ale mere at Ellesmere is only about twelve minutes' walk from the station. Fishing grounds are got at by punt, for which a charge of is. per day is made, and waters from 10 to 20 feet can le found at the different stakes. Upon making inquiries at the boat-house anglers can find out the waters that would suit theta best, according to style of angling. Good accommodation for anglers is provided by different villagers abort five minutes' walk from the mere: Further information on these matters can be had upon making inquiries from the secretary. We know of no better spot that would suit Warrington anglers, as they are practically surrounded by good fishing grounds. Here we give a Est:— Ellesmere, five minutes' walk; Newton Mere, fifteen minutes; White Mere, twenty zniyinteS; Cole Mere, thirty minutes; and Shropshire Union Canal, ten minutes. This is the walk- ing time from the village. Surely there are enough waters here to satisfy any angler, and it is a wonder more Warringtonians do net pay the 'district a visit, as it is a spot where a holi- day can be combined with angling; beard and lodgings can he obtained at reasonable prices. Any member can spend a week here for 405., that is, for everything he will require, and allowing him a few shillings to spare. When this place is fished more by Warrington mot- hers, we can expect to see several specimens taking premier place on board, as two members this season have taken 601 b. of fish in one day's angling with bream amongst them 211 b.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0316" n="art0316">
        <head>OTHER PLACES.</head>
        <p>OTHER PLACES.</p>
        <p>The canals seem to fish worse than ever and those who did take fish last week-end were ex- ceedingly lucky, as they were reported entirely off at all places round Warrington. A. S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0317" n="art0317">
        <head>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. NORTHWICH.</p>
        <p>From the Red Lion, at Preston Brook, trail was laid over about 51 miles of hilly .cocuatry, in the Norton and Preston Brook neighbour- hood, by Frank and William Antrobus. H. Holbrook, W. Bolton and H. Jepson formed a small pack, which ran through at a splendid pace.</p>
        <p>The racing division which followed comprised 13 of Warrington and 12 of Northwich: With view to giving Northwich an idea of their men's form, Chris. Vose and Jim Parsonage accom- panied the leading flight throughout, byt neither counted in a closely contested race, which resulted in a win for Warrington by a single point. Warrington A.C. : E. Scott 1, J. Livesey 2, J. Hayes 6, N. Stafford 9, S: Grounds 10, A. Inman 11-39.</p>
        <p>Northwich : E. Gordon 3, R. Eyes 4. A. Pass 5; A. Sayle 7, G. Moore 8, H. Ward 13-44. 44,4401 Points Competition.</p>
        <p>The second stage of the Points Competitions resulted :—INTERMEDIATE: E. Scott 10, J. Divesey 9, J'. Hayes 8, N. Stafford 7, S. Grounds 6, .A. Inman 5, A. Potts 4, P. Price 3, j. Scott 2, B. Leach 1. Novlcz : J. Hayes 10, A. Inman 9, A. Potts 8, J. Scott 7, H. Leach 6, H. Payton 5, T. Howliston 4,, A. Hazelhurst 3.</p>
      </div>
