      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0330" n="art0330">
        <head>(fb-urcli rtttL</head>
        <p>(fb-urcli rtttL</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN GUILD.</p>
        <p>The members of the Guild met on Monday evening, Mr. Cox presiding. The paper for the evening was given by Mr. B. Williamson, the subject being "Our duty to animals." The speaker drew attention to the failure of our countrymen to realise their responsibilities and duties toward the animal kingdom. Until recent years little or nothing had been done to check the great cruelties perpetrated in our "Christian England," and even at present' the law required strengthening and broadening, to put an end to many existing cruelties practised under the name of sport.</p>
        <p>Much of the cruelty to animals was caused through want of thought, the result of people being town dwellers, and knowing little of the ways of the animal world. The questions of sport, vivisection, animal mythology, &amp;c., opened out a subject worthy of a great deal more attention than it received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0331" n="art0331">
        <head>BOLD STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH. Temperance Sunday.</head>
        <p>BOLD STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH. Temperance Sunday.</p>
        <p>The annual practice of giving especial promi- nence to the subject of temperance obtained at Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Sunday. At the afternoon session of the Sabbath school, the Rev. H. Williamson spoke to the children about the evil effects of alcohol upon all sides of the nature, and total abstinence was urged as the only safe and effective method of avoiding the clutches of the drink evil.</p>
        <p>In the Men's Bible Class a paper on "Total Abstinence" was read by Mr. A. Sander. A careful examination of the evils attendant upon the' drink traffic was made, and the need for vigorous action by the combined churches was suggested as a remedy. . This suggestion caused a merner to refer to a recent vote of thanks proposed by a very prominent clergyman in the town "to the two local breweries." Such an alliance would always cripple any efforts put forward by the other branches of Christ's Church.</p>
        <p>Sunday closing was the first great step which should be taken against the evil of intemperance in alcoholic beverages. At the evening service the Rev. H. Williamson preached a " temperance " sermon, and again insisted that total abstinence was not only the duty of every Christian man and the sole method of resisting the temptations of alcoholic indulgence, but that it was absolutely necessary if full mental, spiritual and physical strength was to be attained to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0332" n="art0332">
        <head>MEMORIAL SERVICE AT ST. JOHNS CHURCH. The Death of an Office Bearer.</head>
        <p>MEMORIAL SERVICE AT ST. JOHNS CHURCH. The Death of an Office Bearer.</p>
        <p>The heavy loss which has befallen St. John's Church in the death of Mr. Thomas Stringer, one of its office-bearers, was emphasised on Sunday evening, when a memorial service was conducted by the Rev. Wm. Reid, minister of the church, and the Rev. J. J. Glover, of Ris- ley. Large numbers gathered early, and before 6.30 many had to be turned away, and the church doors closed. The service throughout was most solemn, the congregation reciting Psalm sc. and chanting Psalm ciii., while the hymns, well known and held in high favour by the late Mr. Stringer, were "Just as I am," "Peace, perfect peace," and "When the day of toil is done."</p>
        <p>Mr. Reid, preaching from Philippians iii., 13 and 14, emphasised the value of the man beyond systems and creeds, and described St. Paul's man as one who constantly and persistently sought truth, in order that character might be built of its material, and who, as an end, sought to express that character in service to Christ and men. In applying this he spoke of Mr. Stringer's possession of ' a mind most vigorous, broad and ever open; of a character manly beyond question, utterly dissimilar to that likened by the Apostle to hay and stubble, and built upon the solid foundation of a living belief in Jesus Christ, could not possibly suffer destruction, but must live for ever.. So sure were they of this that, to quote the preacher's own words, "We say not good-bye, Thomas Stringer, only good-night, we shall meet again in the morning." Mr. A. G. Lewsey was organist. A similar service is to be held in the Risley Presbyterian Church, in which the late Mr. Stringer was brought up, to-morrow afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0333" n="art0333">
        <head>"CURSED "THE CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE. The Rev. W. Bracecamp Defends the</head>
        <p>"CURSED "THE CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE. The Rev. W. Bracecamp Defends the</p>
        <p>Movement.</p>
        <p>To the sound of martial music, the members of the St. Elphin's, St. Paul's and St. Clement's Companies of the Church Lads' Brigade marched to St. Paul's Church on Sunday even- ing for their church parade service. The officers present were Lieut.-Col. F. M. Appleton, Major J. A. Brookes, Captains Henshall and Hepherd, and Lieutenants Henshall and Johnson.</p>
        <p>The Re-V-. W:Brac- ecamp, the chaplain of the battalion, preached and noted the fact that it was almost the 22nd anniversary of the founda- tion of the brigade. He dared say there were some Churchpeople who looked upon one siae of the work of the brigade only, and, instead of blessing them, were inclined to curse them, especially when they heard the bugle blowing and the drums beating, and said "It is the wretched Church Lads' Brigade making their noise again." But there was something far better underneath that demonstration. He could tell of the wonderful results which had been achieved in Warrington by the association of boys with the brigade. In the brigade they taught the lads strict Obedience, and one of the best things the members could do on the occa- sion of the anniversary was to dedicate them- selves, body, soul and spirit, to God. Person- ally, he regretted he had not taken up the work earlier, as it was one of the most promising in connectiOn with the parish. Mothers had told him that since their lads had joined the brigade they had become more obedient and respectful at home. Some looked upon the brigade as a means of recruiting for the Army. It was nothing of the kind, and the beating of drums and sounding Of the bugles was just the scaffold- ing- Behind it all there was the influence of the officers.</p>
        <p>MRS. DODOS AND TEMPERANCE. How the Times Have Changed. Temperance Sunday was celebrated with en- thusiasm at Wycliffe P.S.A. The chief speaker was Mrs. Dodds, president of the Band of Hope Union. She said there was a time when tem- perance and its advocates were laughed at with scorn, but now the temperance cause was sup- ported by learned men and scientists of every shade. Doctors, ministers and many others were now found fighting on the side of temper- ance. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>As president of the Warrington Band of Hope Union she had worked, and intended to work still harder for the saving of the boys and girls of the town from the drink evil. She urged fathers and mothers to assist in that noble work, and make it easy for their children to do right and very difficult for them to do wrong. (Ap- plause.) Mr. G. Boyle, Ashton-on-Mersey, made a strong appeal to all to do their utmost to keep the drink curse away from the child life. He had discovered many facts which proved the great evils of intemperance, men robbed of their manliness, wives punished, children robbed of food, peace and happiness, and robbed of any little opportunity to rise in the world. The drink evil was deplorable, and it was the duty of all to end it. (Applause.) Mr. W. Carter and Mr. B. Farrar also took part in the gathering. The Band of Hope Mixed Choir rendered several items most charmingly, and these were greatly appreciated. Miss Eva Tanner was a delightful soloist. Mr. Spruce conducted with his usual skill, and Mr. Tanner was a most capable organist. The Rev. T. T. James expressed the meet- ing's thanks to Mrs. Dodds and the other speakers and the vocalists.</p>
        <p>AVliispers. When a man and a girl whisper together they are almost certainly saying nice things. When two women whisper together they are almost certainly saying nasty things.</p>
        <p>PEVERIL FIRE GRATES never fail to please.— Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0334" n="art0334">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Opening Concoct cf the Season in</p>
        <p>the Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>GOOD CONCERT--POOR AUDIENCE</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Musical Society made a good beginning with their season with the concert given in Parr Hall on Wednesday evening. The audience was a very sparse one, but that fact is, of course, and fortunately, no criterion of the merits of the concert, though it must be admitted to have some significance in regard .to its attractive- ness to the general public. And in this connec- tion one cannot but deplore an obvious, and, it is to be feared, lasting change for the worse, as we cannot but think, which appears to• be taking place, locally, as all over the kingdom, in public taste for this class of entertainment. How the change is to be met or warded off is, for concert promoters, indeed a difficult ques- tion.</p>
        <p>From a musical point of view there was much that was admirable in Wednesday's programme —admirable, that is, in the manner of perform- ance of the music, rather than as to Some of the items which formed part of it.</p>
        <p>The Singing of the Chorus. The principal work—and one in which the society's forces were alone concerned—was a ballad for chorus and orchestra, "The Deacon's Masterpiece," or, "The Wonderful One-hoes Shay," a quite recent setting of Oliver Wendell Holmes's satiric poem, by Mr. Percy E. Fletcher. In it there is some good music, not too difficult or ultra modern in character, which, after listen- ing to it, one•cannot but think is largely thrown away in being allied with a " book " that lends itself so little to effective musical expression. One feels that while the work is unquestionably descriptive in many of its details, it is, as a whole, somewhat noisy in performance, and that the, little oasis of relief afforded by the " moral " quartet near the middle is unsatisfy- ing both by reason of its insufficient bulk and in the sense that it itself reaches a disappointing conclusion. The choral members of the society sang with great energy and an obvious deter- mination to get out of the composition all that was possible. If one may regard Mr. Fletcher's clever little work as a test piece in intonation and enunciation, and in those very necessary qualities of steadiness and precision in choral singing, members must be - admitted to have come through the ordeal with flying colours. Nor was the balance and volume of tone less than surprisingly good, when it is remembered what a serious handicap the society labours under in the bad relationship of the numbers of its sectional voices. The eight ladies and gentlemen who took part in the before-men- tioned semi-chorus are to be congratulated upon their singing of some progressions none too easy of vocalisation, singing, too, in which, except for a slight failure in •intonation in one of the middle parts, a oneness of intention and a neat- ness of method were quite conspicuous features. For the bright way in which the choral ballad ran its length, a high meed of praise is due to the orchestra, the members of which played the instrumental parts exceedingly well. The society's chorus had two other items to sing of very unequal intrinsic merit. Dealing with Mr. Ed. German's unaccompanied part- song, "My Bonnie Lass, She Smileth," they had material on which they could worthily try their skill. And so successfully and so daintily did members vocalise this charming little ensemble piece that a discriminating audience paid them the high compliment of demanding its repeti- tion. The other number was the chorus with orchestra, "Our Island Home," by Eaton Fan- ing, embodying a tune of so common-place a character, heard with such wearisome iteration, that one looked twice at the programme to make sure that it was really from the pen of so well-known a writer of graceful part-songs and short choral pieces. The Principal Vocalists.</p>
        <p>Miss Eva Rich made a welcome second appear- ance at these concerts, singing with great suc- cess " Elizabeth's Greeting" (Tannhauser), and a number of songs in that rich and flexible soprano voice, and in that animated way which so charmed us at her visit a year ago. Tht, only regret one cares to express is that of the songs she sang—and they were at least four in num- ber—not one of them can be said to have afforded full scope for the display of Miss Rich's strong points, nor to have been potent in capturing the audience. Of Mr. Norman Allin's singing of the two songs "Shipmates o' mine" "A Dinder Courtship," one need only say in high praise of it that it won all hearts, just as it disarmed all criticism. His appeal to owes sympathies in voice and manner are alike irresistible.</p>
        <p>The Playing of the Orchestra. The orchestra discoursed some music of more than ordinary interest in a highly praiseworthy manner. In addition to a very attractive Schu- bert overture, there were a set of three "Dream Dances" by the late Coleridge Taylor, and the Valse Triste of Spelius, from the musTE to Jarnefelt's drama Kuolema." The dances are marked by all that freshness, gracefulness and pleasing sense of difference from the manner- isms of other and contemporary composers, that characterise all Coleridge Taylor's works —the works of a man who, one feels, was cut off ere he can be said to have reached the zenith of his powers to the great loss of our day and , generation. The Sibelius music for muted strings, with a slight admixture of wind instru- ments, received i an exceptionally meritorious in- terpretation, its ethereal quality being finely brought out. The fact that the audience loudly encored the performance was well-merited tes- timony of its high appreciation of its many good points. i Mr. F. 11.• Crossley, the society's conductor, piloted, n a most painstaking way, all concerned, through the difficulties of the evening's work, while Mr. W. 11. Payton played the pianoforte accompaniments with excellent taste and the best of judgment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0335" n="art0335">
        <head>NO LOOKING BACK IN EARLEStOWN</head>
        <p>NO LOOKING BACK IN EARLEStOWN</p>
        <p>Since the long succession of local reports were first published in the Press there has been no looking back. Local evidence continues to pour in, and—better still—those whose reports, were first published many years ago, verify all they said in a most hearty and unmistakable way. Read the experience of Mr. Williams. NEARLY SIX YEARS AGO, Mr. A. Williams, of 58, Houghton-street, near to the Railway Station, Earlestown, said: "I suffered with my back for three pars. I could get no peace for the shoot- ing pains which attacked me across the kidneys. If 1 attempted to lift anything the pains would seize me, so that I could hardly get my back straight again. The urinary excretions caused pain, and contained a dark, heavy sediment. "I tried various remedies, but the medicine that cured me was Doan's Backache Kidney. Pills. They proved splendid in my case, and I find it a pleasure to speak for them. (Signed) A. WILLIAMS."</p>
        <p>NEARLY SIX YEARS LATER, Mr. Williams says: "Considering I am 70 years old I enjoy very good health. My back and kidneys do not trouble me now, I am thankful to say.. I still hold a high opinion of Doan's Backache Kidney Pills."</p>
        <p>Price 2s. 9d. a box, 6 boxes 13s. 9d.; of all dealers, or from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells- street, Oxford-street, London, W. Don't ask for backache and kidney pills—ask DISTINCTLY for DoAN's Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mr. Williams had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0336" n="art0336">
        <head>ADULT SCHOOL</head>
        <p>ADULT SCHOOL</p>
        <p>ACADEMY-STREET MEN'S.</p>
        <p>An interesting lecturette was given on Sunday morning by one of the members upon "The social ideas of Progressive Christians." The speaker pointed out that a Christian did not discuss social conditions from the point of view of elementary rights; he would not ask whether a man had a right to work, a right to a just wage, to a healthy house, and to clean and elevating surroundings. He recognised at once that every person from infancy should re- ceive every possible help to become the best of which he or she was capable. He would not engage in the futile controversy as to whether the environment made the man, or the man the environment; he knew that neither was true by itself, because both were true. The sensible Christian would not pit individual effort and social effort against each other; he would see that they were but two sides to one great work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0337" n="art0337">
        <head>EMMANUEL CHURCH.</head>
        <p>EMMANUEL CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Sale of Work to Pay off Mortgage.</p>
        <p>THE MAYoR OFENS</p>
        <p>The first sale of work to be opened by the new Mayor (Councillor Peter Peacock) was that held in connection with the Emmanuel Church, in the institute, Knutsford-road, on Wednesday, Thursday and yesterday. There was a large at- tendance at the opening ceremony on the first day, and the Rev. W. T. Jaye presided. In addition to the Mayor and Mayoress, the Rev. Wm. Reid, along with many influential mem- bers of the church, attended. The stalls were tastefully arranged, and the secretary of the sale was Mr. J. Bibby, who has worked most assiduously for the success of the effort, _which has been promoted in order to help to pay off a mortgage. A sum of £475 is required. The stalls were presided over by the follow- ing: Mrs. Jaye, Mrs. Lees and friends, Mrs. A. Gandy, Mrs. Atherton and friends, Misses Booth and Parsons and friends. the Sunday School teachers, Mrs. Bibby, jun 4 and friends, Mrs. J. Bibby and friends, Miss N. Gandy and friends. Mrs. Wm. Bibby, son., and friends were in charge of the tearoom. A musical programme was given during the day, and Mr. J. W. Jowett was the director.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0338" n="art0338">
        <head>WELCOME TO THE MAYOR.</head>
        <p>WELCOME TO THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said they were making that effort for the upkeep of the work. Sales of work were often criticised, but he thought they were better than taking up a thanksgiving offering. They also brought them together for social in- tercourse. The work of the church had been eminently successful airing the past year. It had been one of the most successful years they had had. In addition to maintaining the ordin- ary church work, they had had to raise money at short 'notice to pay off a mortgage on the building. In an incredibly short time the people had subscribed £B5, whilst the weekly offerings had not suffered through that special call. The Sunday collections had been as good as they ever were since he came amongst them, which spoke well for their loyalty and ,love of the church and for Christ's work. (Hear, hear.) He had been encouraged to a wonderful extent to know that outside their own church they had so many friends. Not only Anglicans but Free Churchmen wished them God-speed and helped them at a time like the present to the best of their ability. He thought that was a splendid spirit and helpful, not only to themselves, but to all the churches in the town. (Hear, hear.) They were exceedingly pleased to have the Mayor and Mayoress with them. (Hear, hear.) He thought that was one of the first public functions the Mayor had attended since he had been appointed .to his high office. The Mayor and Mayoress had greatly honoured them by their presence. It was not always that a Mayor was accompanied by the Mayoress on such occasions. They wished the Mayor and Mayoress a happy and prosperous time. As chief magistrate, the responsibilities of Coun- cillor Peacock would be very heavy, and they all prayed that the blessing of God would be with him in his work for the good of the town. (Applause.) HIS WORSHIP AND A DEMOCRATIC</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0339" n="art0339">
        <head>CHURCH.</head>
        <p>CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The MATOR said he had the greatest possible pleasure in attending the sale that day. The previous night he had had serious misgivings as to his inability to be present. Ile only left his bed at one o'clock that day, the doctor hav- ing given him permission to go out for an hour or so. He thanked them for their congratula- tions, and for the hopes that his year of office would be a successful one. With the congratu- lations he had had from his Warrington friends and from outsiders, if he failed in the duties expected of him it would be his own responsi- bility, and his own fault. The encouragement he had had on all hands was exceedingly help- ful to him. He had imagined that that was going to be the first function for him to attend as Mayor, but whilst it did not lessen the honour, he had• to attend the annual ball in connection with the Sacred Heart. That was the first public duty of the kind he had had since Monday, and it gave him the greatest pleasure, because the Emmanuel Church, above all others, was one after his own heart. When a church or chapel was democratic, they could understand that they got the best results spiritually and materially. They ought to be proud of the willing band of workers they had. In many denominations and in social functions of all spheres of life, it was very often the work devolved upon a few. From what he under- stood, those stalls had been laden with goods by the stall-holders and their assistants. Nothing had been bought. They had had some splendid beggars, who were necessary in any church on a democratic footing. They did not boast of a lot of aristocrats, and, personally, he did not believe in mixing too much with them, because aristocrats led, them away from the proper things. (Applause.) The Mayoress was presented with a beautiful bouquet by Master J. Bibby and the Mayor re- ceived a buttonhole from Miss Phyllis Jaye. Mr. W. ATHERTON proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor and Mayoress. .He hoped the year would be the most prosperous the town had ever had, and that the Mayor's year of office would be pleasing to himself and to the com- munity. (Applause.) Mr. J. WHITEHUREIT seconded, and the vote was heartily carried.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR returned thanks for the bouquet and buttonhole, and for the vote of thanks. He hoped they would spend all their money for that worthy object. The proceedings were opened last night by the Pastor, the Rev. W. T. Jaye, and it was an- nounced that the receipts for Wednesday amounted to £95 10s. and for Thursday £34 Bs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0340" n="art0340">
        <head>APPLETON RIFLE CLUB.</head>
        <p>APPLETON RIFLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>APPLETON v. WHITECROSS.</p>
        <p>This match was shot on Friday last at the Drill Hall. The conditions were: 8 a-side, 7to count; 10 shots at 25 yards; highest possible score 100. Result: Appleton, 657; Whitecross, 655.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-11-15_WidnesExaminer_art0341" n="art0341">
        <head>BILLIARDS—cont.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS—cont.</p>
        <p>although they had to go all the way to win this match.</p>
        <p>Jack Bluck gave the winners a good send-off by defeating J. W. Plinston 33, but perhaps the best performance of the evening was Dan Raw- lin's splendid display against D. Wylie, as he never gave the latter much chance, and ran out a good winner by - 51. The game between A. Barlow and R. Burrows Was very interesting and deserved the loud applause which was accorded at the conclusion.</p>
        <p>It was pleasing to see the two captains drawn together, viz., Harry Mather and John Wells, as they could not have been better matched, and after a ding-dong struggle the Howleyite was returned a winner by 21: _</p>
        <p>Great interest was taken in the last game, as Howley were only leading by four, but P. Gleason was on his best behaviour, and fully merited his win, which decided the fate of Town Hall No. 1 team. Results:—</p>
        <p>St. Austin's No. 2. Town Hall No. 1. W. Millington (5).. 48 P. Cowap (10) G. Holland (20) ... 100 J. H. Allen (o. 5) 3O H. page (10) W. Smellie (25) C. Cooke, (10)</p>
        <p>, 428 321 St. Austin's No. 1 won by 107.</p>
        <p>Howley No. 1. Town Hall No. 1. R. Burrows (ser.)... 100 A. Barlow (10) D. Wylie (15) H. Mather (scr.) lOO J. Wells (o. 10) .... 79 J. Bluck (ser.) P. Gleeson (5)</p>
        <p>449 Howley No. 1 won by 33.</p>
        <p>Billiard Match.—Penketh Labour Club had a visit from Mr. Clay's billiard team on Tuesday, and the match ended in a win for Mr. Clay's team by 200. The scores were: Mr. Clay's side, 832; Penketh Labour Club, 632. Scores, Mr. Clay's team's name first: A. Clay 100, T. Hughes GO; E. Pierpoint,62, H. Bolton 100; H. Shaw 100, F. Hall 48; J. Deakin 100, H. Hough- ton 29; J. Teere 100, S. Hall 67; It. Stevens 83, S. Faulkner 100; H. Stevens 87, D. Batson 100; W. Shield 100, J. Foden 64.</p>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>First Division. ST. MARY'S CATHOLIC Y.M.S. v. tATCJI.</p>
