        <p>sure about it. 1t was proved to him that in the whole scheme they must have been linked with God and godly things. (Cheers.) A MIGHTY CONVICTION. Mr. A. MorNFIELn, in a short speech, said that there must be conviction at work behind the building of a church. A commercial traveller had recently told him that he had been travel- ling through Wales and was surprised to see so many chapels. Wherever there were a few houses together it was possible to find a chapel in, their midst. They were strong evidences of the reality of Christ, and what applied to the Welsh chapels applied also to Independent Methodist -churches. That place hail arisen out of the green field because somebody had a mighty conviction. He would like to remind them that in their work there was a mighty conviction of the reality of the living God, that could and would speak to men and women. (Cheers.) A vote of thanks was passed to all who had taken part in the day's proceedings. The speeches- were interspersed with several pleasing songs, contributed by Mr. Wm. Barton and Misses Bacon and Shaw, and reaitations by Mr. A. Dutton.. Mr. F. Atkinson an Miss A. Shakeshaft were the pianists. THE SERVICES OF SUNDAY. - 'There were large congregations at the services on Sunday, following the opening ceremony. The special preacher was Mr. ,George Hunter, of Leigh. At a special musical service in the afternoon, Dr. Joseph, the retiring Mayor of 'Warrington, presided, and said he was on that occasion at- tending his last public function as Mayor. He congratulated the congregation upon the erec- tion of such a fine edifice. As a member of the Church of England he was pleased to attend their service, as he believed there should be the fullest co-operation between all churches. SPecial anthems were rendered by the choir, under the leadership a Mr. Gough: The solo- ists were Mr. J. P. McArthur, Mr. T. Goostrey, and Miss Carrie Hastings. Messrs, T. Leah and A. Trow were the accompanists. The satis- factory sum of .1:84 was realised as the result of the week-end ceremonies. •</p>
